# 60 entries (6.3.10 shipped non-i64 generics — the instantiate-once engine
# RE-INVOKES PARSE_FN_DEF / PARSE_STRUCT_DEF on the base's verbatim tokens with name +
# concrete-binding overrides (full fidelity for free); gotcha = the def-start token
# index is stored +1 because token 0 is valid. See generic_instantiate_once_reinvoke.
# 6.3.9 shipped i64 generic FUNCTIONS — the base fn IS the i64
# monomorphization (body emitted with T->i64); the load-bearing gotcha was the
# `: T` return triggering the retptr/stash ABI in the return-type rough-scan —
# see generic_fn_i64_base_and_ret_stash. 6.3.7 made non-capturing closures `|x| body` actually work — they
# were a scaffolded-but-untested, non-functional feature. FOUR codegen bugs, all
# from the closure being an anonymous fn emitted INLINE in the enclosing fn while
# sharing its per-fn state + local tables: (1) it inherited `_cur_fn_regalloc` →
# params stored past phantom callee-saved slots + read from stale regs; (2) its
# frame size was never EPATCHFRAME'd; (3) it reused the shared local-slot tables
# from slot 0, clobbering the enclosing fn's locals; (4) the enclosing fn's
# regalloc byte-scan post-pass rewrote the inline closure code → SIGILL. See the
# closure_inline_emit_shares_enclosing_fn_state entry. 6.3.8 added lexical capture
# by value — see closure_lexical_capture_by_value_env_object.)
# 56 entries (6.3.5 added the pass-1 fn-signature prescan (CO-01) + monomorph
# token-replay substrate (AR-01): the 3-pass driver structure (register / emit-fn-
# bodies / emit-top-level) means the forward-call ABI bug only bites IN-FN calls;
# pass-1 stops at the first top-level statement so the prescan covers decls-first;
# `_prescan_params` must consume `)` or `: Struct` returns never classify; the
# inline replay emit path was always intact — only the capture was gated off. See
# the forward_call_prescan_and_monomorph_substrate entry.)
# 55 entries (6.2.51 added the modular-resolver CVE-32 + `mod`-keyword trap;
# 6.2.35 added, undefined FUNCTIONS compile to a `ud2`/SIGILL stub +
# a WARNING (exit 0, valid binary) but undefined CONSTANTS/enum-values are a HARD
# error — the function-vs-constant asymmetry. So a stdlib fn call that resolves to
# nothing on a target compiles GREEN and traps only at runtime; a missing enum
# const fails the build loudly. Bit the M6 chain on agnos: lib/sync.cyr had no
# CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS mutex backend, so patra's mutex_new/lock/unlock were
# silent ud2 stubs (patra_init's unconditional mutex_new → runtime SIGILL), while
# the filed "add inert SYS_FUTEX constants" fix was dead. A "does it compile"
# gate PASSES this regression — assert symbols are DEFINED (grep the warning). See
# the undefined_fn_ud2_stub_vs_undefined_const_hard_error entry.)
# 49 entries (6.2.33 added, CYRIUS_ARCH_X86 is predefined on EVERY x86 build —
# AGNOS included — so a stdlib file guarded ONLY on `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` /
# `CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` silently takes the Linux branch on the agnos target and
# calls Linux syscall numbers (sakshi: open#2/close#3/exit#60 = agnos
# getpid/spawn/undefined → silent log corruption, compiles clean). The agnos
# branch must `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` FIRST + wrap the arch branches in
# `#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`. Nested `#ifdef` works (4-deep verified); `#else`
# does not. See the cyrius_arch_x86_predefined_even_on_agnos entry.)
# 48 entries (6.2.0 added, Phase-0 growable pressure tables: the GROW / REHASH /
# RELOCATION paths (fn-table 16-table realloc + fn_name_hash rehash; codebuf
# off-heap relocate) are SELF-HOST-INVISIBLE — cycc's 6486 fns / ~1 MB codebuf
# never reach the 8192 / 3 MB caps, so self-host + check.sh never exercise them;
# only a forced-grow test (shrink _fnt_cap/_codebuf_cap to 64/8192, self-compile,
# cmp vs the no-grow reference) catches relocation/rehash bugs. Also: the 8192
# fn-cap was a magic number scattered across 6 subsystems + colliding TEXTUALLY
# with 5 unrelated 8192-capped tables — the migration needed surgical per-context
# swaps (& 8191 → mask, probe/clear bounds → cap, live[1024] DCE bitmap → [4096]),
# never a global replace. See the two phase0_growable_* entries.)
# 44 entries (6.1.40 added, CVE-24: the four per-fn local tables
# (fn_local_names/depths/types/slice) are indexed by SFLC, which counts stack-frame
# SLOTS not vars — a `stack var buf[N]` registers N/8 fillers — so frames >~2 KB
# silently overran the old 256/576-entry tables; relocated all four to the heap top
# (0x5D9D000/0x5DBD000/0x5DDD000/0x5DFD000) at 16384 slots each + a loud SFLC cap.
# Plus the local-vs-global array convention: local `var a[N]` = N BYTES rounded to
# 8; global = N i64 SLOTS — the footgun behind the daimon report.)
# 43 entries (6.1.18 added: adding a PUBLIC stdlib fn (a top-level `module::fn` in
# lib/) makes check.sh's api-surface gate fail cryptically — `case-FAIL: test3
# addition count not reported as 1` — because docs/api-surface.snapshot is now
# stale; regenerate it: rebuild build/cyrius_api_surface from source then
# `build/cyrius_api_surface --update`. Underscore-private fns + src/backend
# internals are NOT tracked, so only intended public-surface adds show. Also: a
# new lib/<x>_win.cyr should be SELF-CONTAINED (own widen/narrow) — coupling it to
# _args_w2u8/_w_append_cstr forces every Windows consumer of the parent module to
# include args.cyr/process.cyr, FATAL under --strict.)
# 42 entries (6.1.17 added: a NEW PE syscall-reroute emitter `E*_PE` must get an
# aarch64 `return 0` stub in backend/aarch64/emit.cyr — parse_expr.cyr references
# it unconditionally inside the statically-dead `_TARGET_PE==1` block, and a
# missing stub fires "undefined function" which is FATAL under `--strict` on
# aarch64 (cx tolerates the whole cohort un-stubbed, aarch64 does NOT). The build
# passes WITHOUT the stub if you forget `--strict` — the warning is the only tell.)
# 41 entries (6.1.12 added: a large fn-local `var[]` lands in `.bss` static, NOT
# the stack — and a `var[]` in a statically-dead region after a `#ifdef`-gated
# return STILL reserves its `.bss`; diagnose via `objdump -h` section deltas, not
# `sub rsp` scanning. The agnos getenv #PF was `.bss`-image/loader-mapping, not
# stack overflow as the issue assumed.)
# 40 entries (.88-.90 added: x86-Mach-O can't compile byte-array literals — a
# parser miscompile INVISIBLE to self-host since cycc uses no byte arrays (verify
# pre-existing by stashing to the released tag's source on the target); the
# byte-array peephole must hoist &var via the obj-aware _EVRCX, NOT absolute-only
# EVADDR_X1, or obj/PIE byte arrays regress; _emit_fmt must test Mach-O before PE
# to match the old EMITELF ladder for the unguarded CYRIUS_MACHO+CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN
# combo. Plus the zsh-no-word-split test-harness trap.)
# 37 entries (.87 added: getenv on agnos walks the exec-stack envp from
# _agnos_init_rsp (no /proc); the agnos kernel is LOCALLY TESTABLE — rebuild
# with agnos/scripts/build.sh + boot under QEMU via agnsh-smoke.sh/exec-smoke.sh
# and run a ring-3 cyrius binary. Don't claim "no agnos to test on".)
# 36 entries (.86 added: callptr callee-spill frame slot can ALIAS a &-taken
# local — v6.0.70 spilled the indirect-call target to a GFLC frame slot; a COM
# out-param `&local` could land on the same slot → frame/regalloc corruption →
# AV (DXGI GetDesc1 on cass). Fix: PE callptr callee on the STACK + `call rax`,
# no frame slot. Found with cdb on cass (C:\dbg\cdb.exe).
# 35 entries (.85 added: Windows install pillar — no /proc env on Windows (read
# via GetEnvironmentVariableA PE reroute 0xF015); wrapper compile()/run_binary
# are POSIX fork/execve, Windows needs cmd.exe-redirect spawn via CreateProcessW
# (cmd /s /c strips only the outer quote pair); .ps1 must be ASCII (PS 5.1
# mis-decodes UTF-8 em-dashes); the macho-arm/x86 dual-number trap applies to PE
# reroute numbers too. cyrius build now works on Windows.
# 34 entries (.84 added: macOS owns the thread-pointer register — Darwin
# saves/restores TPIDR_EL0 (arm64) / %fs (x86) as thread state and overwrites
# any userspace install on the FIRST preemption, so cyrius can't durably own it
# for thread-local storage (sigil crypto-scratch crash); use a process-global
# on macOS. + socketpair dual-number: arm-macho=199 (aarch64-Linux enum),
# x86-macho=53 — and 53 is fchmodat on the arm enum, so don't reuse it.
# 33 entries (.70 added: callptr/IR_CALL_INDIRECT — compiler-emitted indirect
# calls; the callee-survival crux (frame-slot spill, NOT pop-after-ECALLPOPS
# which the Win64 shadow buries); aarch64 uses x6; top-level/cx = loud error;
# open real-Win64-COM-callee corruption → .71.
# 32 entries (.69 added: PE multi-DLL imports — generalize the single-kernel32
# import descriptor to N DLLs (shell32/dxgi); the flat ftype=4 fixup forces an
# imp_idx→physical-slot remap; design byte-identical at one DLL; x86-only PE
# emit fns need aarch64 no-op stubs (FATAL under --strict).
# .68 added: aarch64-Linux ESYSXLAT gaps — stat/rename/symlink
# literals hit the wrong aarch64 syscall (§D1 deps + build + pulsar); the
# native entry file diverged from the cross (missing auto-call-main); and the
# CI gate tested the cross binary not the shipped native one (placebo).
# .67 added: param_load(reg,idx) asm pseudo + the bootstrap-coupling
# lesson — migrating bootstrap-critical libs to a new compiler feature.
# .66 added: cyrius test on Apple Silicon — qemu mis-route (127)
# + unsigned AMFI SIGKILL (137); exec native + _macho_codesign in run_binary.
# .65 added: portable sleep — no raw nanosleep(35) off Linux;
# poll-as-sleep + Windows Sleep reroute; macho 35 collides with unlinkat.
# .64 added: aarch64 atomic-on-global needs 8-aligned
# var base; aarch64 atomic_cas is not a full barrier — fence the lock.
# .63 added: macho syscall dual-definition 61-vs-217;
# Mach-O output-basename breaks reproducible cmp).
#
# 119 entries (v6.5.20 — +2 gotchas. Count re-derived with
#   `grep -c '^\[\[entries\]\]'`, which also corrected the line above: it said 116 while
#   the file already held 117. Both new entries are the same shape — a pass that
#   REWRITES POSITIONS met something in the stream it had no model of. The compaction
#   pass met jump-table DATA; the derive tail remedy met a tail that was not
#   self-contained. Both shipped GREEN through a full four-host gate because the corpus
#   only ever exercised the self-contained case.)

[[entries]]
name = "a_pass_that_moves_code_must_know_every_TABLE_embedded_in_the_code_stream"
description = "v6.5.20 (P1 silent miscompile, latent for multiple minors): a table-regime `switch` whose case bodies FELL OUT jumped mid-instruction — SIGSEGV with a `default:` present, WRONG ANSWER and no diagnostic without one — and the filing's two suspects (the `load32(te)==0` gap-fill sentinel, `S64` writing 8 bytes into a 4-byte-entry table) were BOTH fine. Real cause: the v5.6.27 regalloc NOP-harvest compactor (`parse_fn.cyr`, 'Stage 4: compact bytes'). It deletes the 4-byte NOPs the regalloc picker leaves behind and repairs jump disp32s, fixup CPs and jump-target CPs — but it had no model of the switch jump table, which lives INLINE in the code stream as `(range+1)` 4-byte displacements. Every NOP harvested between the table and a case body left that body's entry 4 bytes stale, CUMULATIVELY: table index 0 (nothing deleted before it) stayed correct while every later case drifted further. Fix = a `_sw_tbl_repair()` stage 3b that recomputes each entry from scratch (both ends move independently) off a per-fn registry `_sw_tbl_reg()` fills in parse.cyr. LESSON 1: when you add a data table to the code stream, grep for every pass that rewrites code positions and register with ALL of them — position repair is not one place. LESSON 2 (the corpus tell, and it is causal not coincidental): only a case body that ASSIGNS A REGISTER-ALLOCATED LOCAL produces NOPs at all, and `tests/tcyr/codegen/switch_dispatch.tcyr` wrote all 82 of its bodies as `case N: { return N; }` — so 269 .tcyr and four real hosts were green over a P1. `return` bodies dodged it twice: they leave through the epilogue instead of the table's fall-out edge AND they allocate nothing. When a construct has several exit routes, a corpus that uses one of them is not coverage. LESSON 3: `_ra_frame_trim`'s NOP recorder is an UNALIGNED byte-pattern scan of the whole fn range, so the same table is now walked as if it were instructions; its safety comment reasoned only about emitted instructions and silently stopped covering the range it scans. A comment that argues about one category of bytes stops being a proof the moment a second category appears in the same range."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "derive_tail_remedy_must_emit_generated_bodies_BEFORE_the_users_tail"
description = "v6.5.20: `#derive` flattens its generated bodies onto the struct's closing-`}` line to keep the construct's expanded line count equal to its source line count. That line can carry a TAIL — `struct Z { a; } fn helper() { return 2; }` is legal cyrius — and consuming the tail is silent code loss, so it is COPIED (`PP_COPY_TAIL`, stopping at a `#` outside a string, never copying the newline). The first cut copied the tail FIRST and appended the generated bodies BEHIND it, which is correct ONLY while the tail is self-contained. A tail that OPENS a construct continuing onto later source lines (`} fn helper() {` … `}`) then had every generated fn spliced INSIDE that body: rc=0, no diagnostic beyond the routine DCE note, SIGSEGV at runtime — the remedy for a silent miscompile producing a silent miscompile. FIX is pure ORDERING: bodies first, user's tail LAST, so the opener is the final thing on the expanded line and the lines continuing it follow verbatim. No look-ahead, no guess about what the tail opens (`fn`/`if`/`while`/`struct`/nothing). The tail offset travels in `_pp_tail_ip`; PP_PARSE_STRUCT_DEF records it, the entry-point handler copies it. TWO MORE TRAPS in the same handler family: (1) `#derive(Deserialize)` never routed through PP_PARSE_STRUCT_DEF at all — it hand-rolled 'skip the directive line, scan to the first `}`' — so the ONE directive whose name says 'this struct is data' DELETED the struct, and worse, a malformed field inside it (`struct D { x; y = ; }`) compiled rc=0 with ZERO diagnostics, because the parser is what checks field syntax and the parser never saw the text. Silent acceptance of malformed source. (2) that same hand-rolled skip never collected the stacked-`#derive` flag bits, so a directive stacked BELOW `#derive(Deserialize)` was dropped whole while the reverse order worked. LESSON: a gate axis whose every sub-case shares one incidental property (here: the whole tail on one line) is not covering the axis — it is covering that property. The axis was literally titled 'THE LOAD-BEARING AXIS' and still missed this."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "cx_bytecode_backend_is_cli_exposed_check_backend_dirs_before_declaring_a_feature_absent"
description = "The cyrius-x (cx) bytecode backend is a REAL, self-hosting, CLI-EXPOSED target: `cyrius build --target=cx` emits a portable `.cyx`, `cyrius run` executes it through the installed cxvm, and programs/checks/cx.cyr gates the whole path end-to-end in the green check.sh. Its last-mile CLI exposure landed in the v6.4.17-.22 arc (Release A CLI wiring .17, f64 arith .18-.19, cross-OS `.cyx` .20) with float/SIMD codegen following (.32); cxvm now installs via the `[bins]` loop in scripts/install.sh (`cbt/cyrius.cyr` accepts `--target=cx`, `cbt/core.cyr` routes it). HISTORICAL (through ~v6.4.16): the backend was fully built + gated but had NO CLI verb — no `--target=cx`, cxvm built only ad-hoc inside the gate, `cyrius run` ran native code — so a grep for `wasm`/`--target=cx` returned nothing and it was easy to wrongly conclude 'we have no bytecode/WASM target' (a user premise-check 2026-07-05 caught exactly that). That gap is now CLOSED — the shape mirrors the TS->JS gap before `--emit-js`/`--target=js` wired it in at v6.1.12. LESSON (evergreen): 'grep for the literal string returns nothing' is NOT 'the work doesn't exist' — a backend can be fully built and gated yet invisible because it has no CLI verb; check git history + src/backend/ dirs + the checks/ gates before declaring a feature absent."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_access_mis_parsed_as_enum_variant_when_field_name_shadows_a_global_var"
description = "v6.4.24 (P1): `bc.exit_code` silently read the WRONG value (a global, not the struct field) when the field name also happened to be a top-level var. Root cause in parse_expr.cyr's `X.Y` disambiguation (enum-variant vs struct-field): it decided 'enum-variant' by checking whether `FINDVAR(Y) >= 0` — i.e. whether the FIELD NAME Y is a known global var — and if so loaded that global, IGNORING the base X entirely (`Enum.VARIANT` semantics = 'load the global const VARIANT, ignore the enum-type prefix'). So `struct_var.field` where `field` shadowed a top-level var loaded the global instead of the field. SILENT wrong value (no error/crash). LESSON 1 (the fix): the base of an enum-variant access is a bare ENUM TYPE NAME, never a variable — so if X is a known local/global var (FINDLOCAL/FINDVAR), `X.Y` is a FIELD access, full stop; guard the enum-variant branch on `X` not being a var. Genuine `Enum.VARIANT` (base is a type) is preserved. cycc byte-identical (its own source has no field-shadows-global pattern). LESSON 2 (why it fooled everyone): it looked 'size-dependent / only in a large unit' and got FILED as a two-struct field-name/offset collision — but it's neither a two-struct collision (renaming the other struct's field doesn't help) nor a buffer overflow (token/name buffers well under cap). It only bites when the field name ALSO happens to be a top-level var, so small repros pass; a large consumer (stiva's `var exit_code = main()`) has such a var. The consumer's rename-dodge (exit_code→exit_status) worked only because exit_status wasn't also a top-level var. LESSON 3 (method): when a filing's hypothesis is wrong and it won't repro minimally, instrument cycc — a CYRIUS_DUMP_PP env-guarded dump of preprocess_out (S+0x459D000, GBL bytes) + a token-window dump in ERR (tok_types@0x2D7C000 / tok_values@0x357C000 / tok_names@0x60000, walk back from GTI) + a filtered field-load trace pinpointed it in the live unit. Also: reproduce by FULLY reverting the consumer's dodge (a PARTIAL revert — field renamed but accessors not — produced a self-inflicted red-herring 'unexpected ,' from FINDFIELD<0→ERR, wasting a cycle)."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "heap_region_shared_by_all_forks_must_relocate_to_arena_top_because_fork_arenas_diverge"
description = "v6.4.21 (LEXID cap 16384→65536): growing a fixed-offset heap region that lex.cyr/parse*.cyr (SHARED across all 7 main_*.cyr forks) accesses is NOT a one-line bump — two traps. (1) BOXED-IN: `lexid_entries` (16384×8B @ 0x457C900) had `preprocess_out` (8 MB) starting 1792 bytes later, so it can't grow in place; it must RELOCATE. (2) FORK ARENA DIVERGENCE: the 7 forks map DIFFERENT arena sizes — x86-Linux (main.cyr) + Windows (main_win.cyr) map 0x7300000 (they include the IR node/cp arena 0x5E9D000..0x729D000, x86-only under CYRIUS_IR); the aarch64/macho/cx forks (main_aarch64{,_native,_macho}.cyr, main_x86_macho.cyr, main_cx.cyr) map only 0x5E1D000 (no IR arena). A region accessed by a SHARED file must live at ONE fixed offset common to ALL forks — and there is NO free common region above the packed 0..0x5E1D000 (the x86 IR arena occupies 0x5E1D000..0x729D000, which the small forks don't even map). SOLUTION (the ir_nodes v6.3.28 pattern): relocate the region to a fresh offset ABOVE the highest fork's top (0x7300000, past ir_cp) and extend ALL 7 forks' arenas to cover it (mmap size in main.cyr:693/main_win.cyr:351/the two macho forks; SYS_BRK in main.cyr:706/main_aarch64.cyr/main_aarch64_native.cyr/main_cx.cyr). KEY: on the small forks the range 0x5E1D000..0x7300000 (~25 MB) is now mapped-but-unused — that costs NOTHING because anonymous mmap/brk is LAZY (physical pages only on touch). So don't fear the 'waste'. This is a LAYOUT change → TWO-STEP bootstrap; but it's cycc-BYTE-IDENTICAL (the relocated offset + cap are same-width immediates) and differential-diff=0 (it's cycc's RUNTIME heap, not codegen). Verify: cross-OS self-host on ALL of ecb/cass/pi (the arena extension is the exact thing that faults on a fork if you missed one) + a synthetic >old-cap-identifier program that fails on old cycc and compiles on new (proves the relocated region works, not just that it links). The var-family tables (_vars_base etc.) can't hold a per-var FLAG because fixup.cyr SUMS var_size for the global data-section layout (a negative/encoded size corrupts it on all backends) — see the signed-global-sign-ext issue for why signedness needs its own 8th table."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "cxvm_guest_syscall_is_compiler_renumbered_at_runtime_fix_pointer_slot_and_pe_arity"
description = "cx Release C (v6.4.20): making a portable .cyx do I/O on all 4 hosts is a cxvm-ONLY fix — the guest syscall number does NOT need a translation table in cxvm, because the compiler already renumbers it per-host AT RUNTIME. cxvm's 0x70 handler does `syscall(sn, ...)` with a VAR sn (the guest's x86-Linux number). On aarch64 `ESYSXLAT` (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr) and on macOS `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` emit a runtime `cmp x8,#N / b.ne / movz x8,#M` chain BEFORE svc — so whatever runtime value is in the number register gets renumbered. Empirically: the PRE-fix cxvm cross-compiled to aarch64/macho already ran a write+exit .cyx unchanged. Adding a `_cx_hostsys` renumber table in cxvm (the scoping workflow's proposed design) would DOUBLE-translate (guest 1→64, then 64→garbage). The two REAL bugs were: (1) POINTER-SLOT — the handler translated the guest virtual-offset→real-address on the wrong register for open: open(path,flags,mode) puts path in arg0=s2, but it fixed s3(=flags), so every open() EFAULTed on ALL hosts; read/write buf=s3 were correct. Fix keys on the GUEST number BEFORE renumber, shifts only the pointer reg (offset 0=NULL untouched). aarch64 open→openat's AT_FDCWD arg-shift is emitted by ESYSXLAT and composes automatically. (2) WINDOWS ARITY — cxvm issued a FIXED `syscall(sn,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6)` = argc 6. `EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC` routes var-numbered PE syscalls BY ARITY (argc4→read/write/open/lseek, argc2→close/exit, argc7→mmap) and has NO argc-6 bucket → every guest write/read/open/close/exit hit _pe_dyn_nomatch → -ENOSYS (cass exit=-38, no output; verified defender-clean, NOT a quarantine). Fix: arity-correct dispatch — `if sn∈{0,1,2,8} syscall(sn,s2,s3,s4)` (argc4), `elif sn∈{3,60} syscall(sn,s2)` (argc2), `else` 5-arg passthrough. PE arity buckets now route to the SHIPPED EWRITE_PE/EREAD_PE/EOPEN_PE/ECLOSE_PE/ELSEEK_PE/EEXIT emitters (ZERO new backend code); Linux/macOS ignore extra args so exact-arity is equally correct → one dispatch, four hosts. cycc byte-identical (cxvm is a program, off the self-host/seed chain). SEPARATE catch: the cx COMPILER cycc_cx (src/main_cx.cyr) cross-compiles to Mach-O/PE CLEAN but FAULTS on macOS (signal 12; 2 syscalls ESYSXLAT doesn't route) — so cxvm ships to the tarballs but cycc_cx does NOT (issues/2026-07-07-cycc_cx-cross-native-macho-pe.md). LESSON: cross-compiling clean ≠ running clean — RUN the newly-shipped binary on the host; and when a scoping premise says 'add a translation table', check whether the compiler already translates at runtime before duplicating it."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "undefined_fn_ud2_stub_vs_undefined_const_hard_error"
description = "cyrius treats an unresolved FUNCTION call and an unresolved CONSTANT/enum-value asymmetrically: a call to a function defined nowhere compiles to a `ud2;ud2;nop` (SIGILL) stub at the call site + a `warning: undefined function` and STILL exits 0 with a valid binary (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr overwrites the `e8` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` when the fn target is the -1/-2 undef sentinel — deliberate, so it's a loud localisable SIGILL not a jump into low memory); but a reference to an undefined constant/enum value is a HARD compile error (`undefined variable '…'`). CYRIUS_STRICT=1 does NOT promote the fn warning. Consequence: a stdlib fn that resolves to nothing on some target ships GREEN and traps only at runtime. Surfaced v6.2.35 on agnos (lib/sync.cyr had no agnos mutex backend → patra's mutex_* were ud2 stubs → patra_init SIGILL), and explains why the M6-chain issue's 'add inert SYS_FUTEX constants' fix was both unnecessary AND a misdiagnosis (patra ≤1.11.x referenced SYS_FUTEX as a raw CONSTANT → real hard error; 1.12.x uses mutex_lock() a FUNCTION → silent ud2 → no error)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "fixup", "ud2", "undefined-function", "undefined-constant", "agnos", "cross-target", "silent-failure", "v6.2.35"]
content = """
**UPDATE v6.3.2 (2026-06-28) — the default FLIPPED.** A *reachable* undefined-fn call is now a
**HARD ERROR by default** (`error: refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s)`;
exit 1, no output). The check in `src/backend/{x86,aarch64}/fixup.cyr` gates on a new `_allow_undef`
global (default 0 = error) instead of `_strict_mode`. `--allow-undef` (CLI) / `--al` downgrades back
to the warn+ud2+exit-0 behavior described below. **Dead-host (DCE-unreachable) refs STAY
warn+ud2+exit-0** (filtered out of the reachable count). So the asymmetry table below now describes
(a) any undef under `--allow-undef`, or (b) a genuinely dead-host ref — NOT the default reachable
case, which is now symmetric with the constant case (both hard-error). The flip's repo-wide blast
radius (forks, cross-arch builds, aspirational tools — not just the tcyr corpus) was cleaned with
ZERO `--allow-undef` via stubs/guards; see project memory `project_v632_undef_hard_error`.

**The asymmetry** (pre-v6.3.2 default; now the `--allow-undef` / dead-host case):

| unresolved symbol | cycc behavior |
|---|---|
| **function call** | `warning: undefined function 'f'` + emit `ud2;ud2;nop` at the call site; **exit 0, valid binary** |
| **constant / enum value** | `error: undefined variable 'X'`; **exit 1, no output** |

So `return f(x)` where `f` is defined nowhere COMPILES (and SIGILLs at
runtime if reached); `return X` where `X` is an undefined enum value does
NOT. `CYRIUS_STRICT=1` does not change the function case.

**Why** (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr ~170-194): for a function-call fixup
whose target is the undef sentinel (<0), cycc overwrites the 5-byte
`e8 XX XX XX XX` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` = `ud2; ud2; nop`.
Intentional — a SIGILL at the exact call site beats the disp32 resolving
to an arbitrary low address (the sandhi M2 2026-04-24 jump-into-entry bug).
Runtime confirmation: such a binary exits 132 (128+SIGILL) at the call.

**Where it bit (v6.2.35, the M6 persistence chain on agnos):**
`lib/sync.cyr` defined `mutex_new/lock/unlock` only under
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_{WIN,MACOS,LINUX}` — no agnos branch. patra includes
`sync.cyr` (not `thread.cyr`/`thread_agnos.cyr`), so on `--agnos` those
calls were undefined → ud2 stubs, compiling GREEN. `patra_init()` calls
`mutex_new()` UNCONDITIONALLY, so patra_init itself SIGILLs at runtime on
agnos. The filed fix ("add inert `SYS_FUTEX`/`FUTEX_*` to the agnos peer")
was a misdiagnosis: those constants are only referenced by stale patra
≤1.11.x's `_patra_lock`, which used `SYS_FUTEX` as a RAW CONSTANT (→ hard
error → that's the error the reporter actually saw). patra 1.12.x moved to
`mutex_lock()` (a FUNCTION → silent ud2), so once 1.12.3 is folded nothing
references the constants. Real fix: an agnos `#ifdef` no-op mutex backend in
sync.cyr (single-core cooperative-iron — no contender, lock is a no-op).

**Recurring class — `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`-only stdlib helpers / raw
Linux syscall numbers.** This is the same shape, hit THREE times now:
v6.2.33 (io.cyr's `xopen`/`xstat`/… substrate), v6.2.35 (sync.cyr mutex), and
**v6.2.36 (io.cyr's `file_lock`/`file_unlock`/`file_trylock`/`file_lock_shared`/
`file_append_locked` — Linux-only + raw `syscall(73)`, so undefined `ud2` stubs
on agnos+macOS and the wrong number on aarch64; descent's `persist_init` →
libro FileStore → `file_append_locked` trapped at BOOT).** The fix pattern is
always the same: **route through the portable `x*` wrapper** that centralizes
the per-target syscall number (`xflock` → #59 agnos / #92 macOS / #73 x86 / #32
aarch64), and where a target lacks a feature the others have, branch it (agnos
has no kernel `AO_APPEND` yet → `xlseek` SEEK_END under the lock instead of
`O_APPEND`). PREVENTION: periodically grep `lib/` for `#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX` blocks that define `fn` (without sibling agnos/macОS
branches) and for raw `syscall(<small Linux num>)` outside the peer files —
each is a latent off-Linux `ud2`.

**How to recognize / guard it:**
- A consumer "compiles clean for `--agnos`" (or any target) but crashes
  early at runtime → grep the build stderr for `warning: undefined function`.
  Those are live landmines, not noise.
- A regression GATE for this class must NOT just check `exit 0` — it must
  assert the symbols are DEFINED (compile a probe that references them and
  FAIL if stderr contains `undefined function '<name>`). See
  scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh probe 1f.
- Mirror of the macОS-rot placebo: self-host + check.sh never exercise
  agnos codegen, so undefined-fn stubs ship "found by ports."
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_arch_x86_predefined_even_on_agnos"
description = "The compiler predefines `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` on EVERY x86-hosted build — the AGNOS ring-3 target included (main.cyr:851, unconditional, before the target predefines). So a stdlib file that selects syscall numbers on `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` / `CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` ALONE silently takes the Linux x86 branch on agnos and emits Linux syscall numbers — which compiles clean and corrupts at runtime (agnos's numbers differ). The fix is a precedence `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` branch; surfaced v6.2.33 by sakshi's logger."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "agnos", "cross-target", "CYRIUS_ARCH_X86", "CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS", "ifdef", "v6.2.33"]
content = """
**The trap**: `PP_PREDEFINE(S, "CYRIUS_ARCH_X86")` fires unconditionally
near the top of main.cyr (the x86-hosted compiler), BEFORE the per-target
`CYRIUS_TARGET_{AGNOS,WIN,MACOS,LINUX}` predefines. agnos is an x86_64
emit target, so on `env CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1 cycc` BOTH `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`
AND `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` are defined. A stdlib module that dispatches
syscall numbers purely on the ARCH macro —

    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    var SYS_OPEN = 2; var SYS_CLOSE = 3; var SYS_EXIT = 60;   # LINUX numbers
    #endif

— therefore takes the **Linux** branch on agnos. It compiles with zero
errors and then calls the wrong kernel: on agnos #2=getpid, #3=spawn,
#60=undefined, so a file logger silently corrupts (sakshi, v6.2.33).
This is the cross-target sibling of the macOS-rot placebo: nothing in
the self-host or check.sh path exercises agnos codegen, so it ships
"found by ports."

**The fix**: put the agnos branch FIRST and exclude the arch branches
under it —

    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS
    var SYS_OPEN = 7; var SYS_CLOSE = 6; var SYS_EXIT = 0;     # agnos numbers
    #endif
    #ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    ...linux x86...
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    ...linux aarch64...
    #endif
    #endif

cyrius's preprocessor supports **nested `#ifdef`/`#ifndef`** (verified
4-deep) but has **no `#else`** — so a per-target split is always
"agnos/win/macos guards FIRST, then `#ifndef AGNOS { #ifdef ARCH... }`".

**Recognize it again**: any per-target `var SYS_* = <linux number>` or
raw `syscall(<linux-num>,…)` reachable on agnos that is guarded only on
an ARCH macro. cyrlint flags raw `sys_open(<expr>,<literal>,…)` and raw
getdents, but NOT a clean Linux-number `var` taken via the arch branch —
so this class needs a real `env CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1 cycc` build +
emit-inspect (the `_agnos_xsys_gate` / agnos-crossbuild gate), never just
self-host. Numbers that ARE carried in every peer (SYS_LSEEK,
SYS_GETRANDOM) are safe to use bare; flock is peer-carried ONLY on agnos,
so io.cyr's `xflock` centralizes its per-target number.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "element_typed_array_slot_spelling_v621"
description = "Bare `var a[N]` is byte-sized in a fn / slot-sized at top level — an accidental divergence that silently corrupts any address-taken local written with the `store64(&a + i*8)` slot idiom (the daimon route-404 class). v6.2.1 adds `var a: T[N]` (N elements of T) as the unambiguous slot/typed spelling; the audit found the class live in cycc itself (PARSE_SWITCH/PARSE_MATCH jump tables) + stdlib + folded libs."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "var-array", "byte-vs-slot", "0x12A000", "daimon", "v6.2.1"]
content = """
**The trap**: the SAME syntax `var a[N]` reserves different sizes by
scope. In a function (`PARSE_ARRAY`, parse_decl.cyr) it's `(N+7)&~7`
**bytes**; at top level (`PARSE_GVAR_ARR`) it's `N*8` (N i64 **slots**).
Both register into the one var-size table at `0x12A000`. A
function-local slot array written with `store64(&a + i*8, …)` therefore
runs off its byte-sized backing — `var parts[4]` is 8 bytes (1 slot),
so `store64(&parts + 24, …)` corrupts the next static object. Layout-
sensitive, so it stays dormant until an unrelated change shifts the
layout (daimon: deleting dead code moved `parts` 24 B before sandhi's
`" "` literal → every HTTP route 404'd).

**The fix (v6.2.1)**: `var a: T[N]` = N elements of T, sized
`N*sizeof(T)` identically in BOTH scopes. `i64[N]` = N slots (the slot
spelling), `u8[N]` = N bytes, widths i8..i64/u8..u64/u128. Pure
**frontend** change — the element width threads through `scalar_type`
(in-fn, `PARSE_ARRAY(S,noff,ew)`) / `ann_scalar` (top-level,
`PARSE_GVAR_ARR(S,noff,sti,ew)`) into the `0x12A000` byte size, and
every backend (x86/aarch64/macho/pe/cx) inherits it by SUMMING that
table — no backend edits. Bare `var a[N]` keeps its legacy per-scope
meaning (best reserved for byte buffers).

**Two-step bootstrap REQUIRED when migrating cycc's OWN arrays**:
because the OLD build/cycc byte-sizes the new `i64[N]` annotation,
`build/cycc → stage_a` gives stage_a byte-sized internal tables;
`stage_a → stage_b` (stage_a has the feature) gives slot-sized tables.
So `stage_a != stage_b`; the fixpoint is `stage_b == stage_c`. cycc's
own source had no >32-arm switch/match, so the under-sized `ends[256]`
(32 slots) self-hosted by luck for years — the classic latent-until-
layout-shift signature.

**Audit lesson**: run the audit against the lib SOURCE repos, not the
vendored fold — the fold-copy scan missed sigil `snp_report_verify`'s
`parsed[4]` that the source check caught (same `memset(&x,0,32)`-on-an-
8-byte-buffer bug as its sibling `inters[4]`). The ecosystem libs
(sigil/sakshi/agnosys) are the language's own stdlibs: patch each
lib's source, hold for release, then re-fold — never hand-edit the
`# Do not edit` vendored copy.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack"
description = "A large function-local `var buf[N]` in cycc is allocated in `.bss` static storage, NOT the stack frame — and a `var[]` sitting in a statically-dead region (e.g. after a `#ifdef`-gated early `return`) STILL reserves its `.bss` even on the path that never reaches it. Diagnose via section deltas, not `sub rsp` scanning."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "bss", "var-array", "agnos", "stack", "v6.1.12"]
content = """
**The trap**: `lib/io.cyr` `getenv()` had an 8 KB `var buf[8192]`
*after* a `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` early `return`. The agnos
build never touches `buf`, but the agnoshi consumer #PF'd and the
filed issue blamed an "8 KB stack frame reserved past the early
return." Plausible — but WRONG about the mechanism.

**What actually happens**: in cycc a function-local `var[]` of
this size is emitted into **`.bss`** (static storage), not as a
stack-frame allocation. Verified on the real `--agnos` build by
diffing section sizes before/after guarding the buffer behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`:

  .bss   0x2f18 -> 0xf08   (-8208 B = buf + align)
  .text  -928 B            (the now-dead environ reader)
  max stack frame: unchanged at 0xd0 — no `sub rsp, 0x2000`
  ever existed.

So scanning the disassembly for a big `sub rsp` finds nothing and
can make you think "the buffer isn't there" — it IS, just in
`.bss`. Always check `llvm-objdump -h` section deltas, not just
prologue `sub rsp`, when chasing a "phantom buffer" on a
constrained target.

**Why it still faulted on agnos** (mechanism the issue got wrong):
an oversized `.bss` grows the PT_LOAD memsz the loader must
zero-map; the agnos #PF is `.bss`-image / loader-mapping related,
NOT init-stack overflow. A "widen the ring-3 init stack" fix
aims a layer off.

**The fix**: guard the whole reader (buffer + parse loop) behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` so the dead region contributes
nothing to the agnos image. The broader compiler-side want —
elide storage for a `var[]` that's provably never live on the
taken path — stays a (low-priority) follow-on; the preprocessor
guard fully resolves the case. Related: the v6.0.79 `cyml.cyr`
oversized-`var[]`-on-constrained-target overflow.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "stale_fixed_cap_drift"
description = "Cap checks drift out of sync with the actual buffer size. The buffer gets expanded; the cap check / comment / docs DON'T. Bug sits latent until a downstream workload crosses the OLD threshold."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["caps", "drift", "diagnostics", "v3.4.20", "recurring"]
content = """
**The trap pattern**: cyrius uses fixed-size heap regions for
nearly every compiler data structure. Each region has an
explicit byte count (e.g., tok_names is 64 KB, preprocess_out
is 2 MB at v5.6.40). Most regions have a "capacity check"
somewhere in the code that silently caps or errors out when
the region fills.

Multiple times now, a capacity check has drifted out of sync
with the actual buffer size. The pattern:

1. Buffer gets expanded in one place (the actual allocation).
2. Cap check / comment / docs DON'T get updated in the other
   places.
3. Bug sits latent for N releases.
4. Some downstream workload crosses the OLD threshold.
5. Confusing error fires that doesn't point at the real
   buffer.
6. Delayed debugging session to figure out the stale cap is
   lying.

**Concrete instances**:

- v3.3.17 (Bug #33): LEXHEX read hex literals from the raw
  input buffer instead of the preprocessed buffer. Masked for
  years.
- v3.4.19: input_buf read loop capped at 131,072 bytes (the
  original 128 KB) silently — mabda's 141 KB bundle triggered
  the latent bug.
- v3.4.20 (lex.cyr:1077): preprocess_out cap checked
  `op > 524288` (512 KB), but the buffer was expanded to 1 MB
  in v3.4.5. Stale for FIVE releases.
- v3.4.20 (cyrius.toml roadmap): documented Input buffer as
  512 KB while actual code was 128 KB then 256 KB. Three
  different numbers across three files.

**The proactive sweep** (do at every minor closeout):

For each fixed-size buffer in cyrius, grep for EVERY
occurrence of the capacity constant (allocation AND cap
check) and confirm they agree. Also check comments, heap map,
docs, roadmap, and Open Limits table — all four need to match.

The Open Limits table in `docs/development/roadmap.md` is
worth special attention — it's the table downstream projects
read to decide how much headroom they have.

**The diagnostic discipline** (every fixed-size buffer):

Every fixed-size buffer should have an EXPLICIT overflow check
with a CLEAR error message pointing at the exact location to
fix:

```cyr
if (op > 1048576) {
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, 2,
        "error: expanded source exceeds 1MB (", 36);
    PRNUM(op);
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, 2,
        " bytes; raise preprocess_out cap in src/main.cyr)\\n", 50);
    syscall(SYS_EXIT, 1);
}
```

Silent truncation is never acceptable — it moves the symptom
arbitrarily far from the cause.

**Recognize again**: any error message reporting a numeric
threshold that doesn't match the comment / heap map / Open
Limits table.

Cross-ref: patterns.cyml's
`heap_cap_growth_compiler_grows_to_fit_language` (the dual —
when to RAISE caps vs when to discover stale caps).

Full backstory: retros/v3.cyml `stale_fixed_cap_pattern` +
`silent_stdin_truncation_saga`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_alloc_grow_step_must_cover_request"
description = "Bump-allocator grow logic must cover the requested allocation, not a fixed delta. Fixed step `0x100000` regardless of requested size returned pointers past the brk/mmap for any alloc(>1MB)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["alloc", "stdlib", "v5.6.x"]
content = """
**The trap**: bump allocator (`lib/alloc.cyr`) must grow its
backing region by AT LEAST the requested size, not a fixed
delta. Pre-fix, the grow logic was:

```cyr
if (alloc_used + n > brk_top) {
    sys_brk(brk_top + 0x100000);   # grow by 1 MB
    brk_top = brk_top + 0x100000;
}
return alloc_used; alloc_used = alloc_used + n;
```

For `alloc(1500000)` (1.5 MB request), the grow step adds 1 MB
but the requested size is 1.5 MB → returned pointer is past
the new brk_top. Subsequent stores write into unmapped memory
→ SIGSEGV.

**Fix**: grow by max(requested, fixed_step):

```cyr
if (alloc_used + n > brk_top) {
    var grow = 0x100000;
    if (n > grow) { grow = n + 0x100000; }
    sys_brk(brk_top + grow);
    brk_top = brk_top + grow;
}
```

**Recognize again**: any "alloc(N) for large N silently
SIGSEGVs in subsequent store" shape. Single-store SIGSEGVs
right after an alloc are almost always bump-allocator
boundary issues; print `alloc_used` and `brk_top` at the
crash site to confirm.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's
`stdlib_alloc_grow_step_vs_requested_size`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open"
description = "libssl's OPENSSL_init_ssl uses a recursive pthread mutex whose __kind field lives in the glibc TCB. cyrius's dynlib_bootstrap_tls zeroed TCB reads __kind=0 (non-recursive); same-thread re-entry deadlocks on futex. Use lib/fdlopen.cyr (foreign-dlopen) instead."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["dynlib", "tls-loader", "fdlopen", "v5.6.37"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius has two ways to load shared libraries at
runtime:

1. `lib/dynlib.cyr` — pure-cyrius ELF .so loader via mmap +
   GNU hash table walk. No libc, no glibc dlopen. Bypasses
   glibc's TLS/TCB setup entirely.

2. `lib/fdlopen.cyr` — foreign-dlopen wrapper. Calls into
   glibc's actual dlopen via the AT_BASE+offset trick, which
   means the loaded library gets glibc's full TLS/TCB setup
   including pthread_*.

For libssl specifically, dynlib_open works for getting symbol
addresses — but calling `OPENSSL_init_ssl()` deadlocks on a
futex inside libcrypto's pthread_mutex_lock. Why: libssl's
init uses `PTHREAD_RECURSIVE_MUTEX_INITIALIZER_NP`. The mutex
struct has a `__kind` field in glibc's TCB. cyrius's
dynlib_bootstrap_tls (the minimal TLS setup that dynlib_open
provides) zeros the TCB. `__kind == 0` means "non-recursive"
in pthread's encoding. Same-thread re-entry to a mutex that
the same thread already holds → futex wait forever (because
non-recursive).

**Rule**: if a library's init function uses pthread mutexes
internally, you NEED fdlopen (real glibc dlopen → real glibc
TLS/TCB), not dynlib (cyrius minimal loader). The list of
libraries that hit this includes:

- libssl (OPENSSL_init_ssl)
- libpthread (everything)
- libgomp (OpenMP runtime)
- libc-internal locking primitives (rare in pure cyrius use,
  but anything that touches TLS-cancellation hooks)

Conversely, dynlib is fine for:

- Pure C libraries with no pthread internals (libz, libcrypto's
  primitive routines if you skip OPENSSL_init_*)
- Libraries you call only for symbol addresses (dlsym then
  call manually)

**Recognize again**: any "dlopen succeeds, dlsym returns valid
pointer, calling that pointer hangs forever" shape on a
shared library that uses pthread mutexes.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment"
description = "SysV ABI requires rsp 16-aligned at every CALL (so callee sees rsp+8 16-aligned at entry). Cyrius's ECALLPOPS for n>6 args used `add rsp, 48` unconditionally; for odd nextra (N=7,9,11) this left rsp 8-aligned at CALL, propagating misalignment."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "sysv", "alignment", "v5.6.41"]
content = """
**The trap**: SysV AMD64 ABI requires rsp 16-byte aligned at
every CALL instruction (so the callee sees rsp+8 16-byte
aligned at entry, with the saved rip below it). Cyrius's
codegen for fn calls with >6 args uses ECALLPOPS to clean up
stack arguments after the call:

```cyr
# n > 6 args, n_extra = n - 6
# Push n_extra extra args
# call helper
# add rsp, n_extra * 8  ← cleanup
```

Pre-v5.6.41, the cleanup was `add rsp, n_extra * 8`
unconditionally. For odd `n_extra` (N = 7, 9, 11 args), this
leaves rsp 8-byte-aligned at the next CALL. Misalignment
propagates downstream until something does SSE on a stack-
saved value (libssl/libc prologs commonly do `movdqa xmm,
[rsp+N]`) — SIGSEGV at first instruction.

**Fix**: align rsp to 16 BEFORE pushing extra args:

```cyr
if ((n_extra & 1) == 1) {
    # Pre-pad rsp by 8 to absorb odd n_extra count
    push rax  # any value; we sub rsp, 8 effectively
}
push n_extra args
call helper
add rsp, (n_extra + (n_extra_odd ? 1 : 0)) * 8
```

Win64 path was already correct via framesize-mod-15 alignment
branch; SysV path missed it.

**Recognize again**: any "SIGSEGV on the first SSE instruction
of a libc / libssl entry" shape. Common triggers: 7-arg fn
calls, OpenSSL's EVP_DecryptInit_ex (8 args), recvmmsg
(7 args). Always check rsp alignment via `gdb p $rsp & 0xF`
at the failing instruction.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment_violation`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "fn_keyword_in_param_position"
description = "`fn` is a reserved keyword and cannot be used as a parameter name even in helper fns. Cyrius surfaces this with 'expected identifier, got reserved keyword fn' at the NEXT fn definition, not the offending one."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "diagnostics", "v5.7.46"]
content = """
**The trap**: writing `fn helper(fn, arg)` declares a
parameter named `fn`. Cyrius's parser treats `fn` as a
reserved keyword (token type 32), so this fails — but the
error message points at the WRONG location.

The parser first sees `fn helper(`, accepts it, then sees the
parameter list. When it tries to read `fn` as a parameter
name, it errors with "expected identifier, got reserved
keyword fn" — but the PARSE_TOKEN cursor is at the NEXT
function definition's `fn` keyword, not the one in the param
list. So the error reports the line of the next fn, not the
broken param.

Diagnosis is "the fn at line X is broken" → user inspects
line X → finds nothing wrong → confused.

**Fix at v5.7.46** (cyrius v5.5.26+ ERR_EXPECT enhancement):
when the expected token is type 2 (identifier) and the got
token is a keyword (IS_KEYWORD_TOK returns 1), the diagnostic
explicitly names the keyword:

```
error: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'fn'
       (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/
       field/fn)
```

The error fires at the keyword's actual line, not the next
fn. The "rename" hint helps users figure out the fix without
having to know which keywords are reserved.

**The reserved-keyword list** that triggers this: `fn`, `if`,
`else`, `elif`, `while`, `for`, `return`, `var`, `struct`,
`enum`, `union`, `match`, `defer`, `include`, `asm`, `kernel`,
`object`, `secret`, `in`, `shared`, `default`, `as`,
`continue`, `break`. Any of these as a param/local/struct-
field name surfaces the same error.

**Recognize again**: any "compile error 'expected
identifier' on a line that looks fine" shape. Check the
PREVIOUS fn's param list for keyword reuse.

Full backstory: v5_7.cyml's `fn_keyword_in_param_position`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions"
description = "Cyrius requires top-level `var X` to be declared before any fn body that references X. Applies to programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr's symbol-table backing buffers too."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "lsp", "global-init-order", "v5.7.39"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius's parser does a single-pass top-down
walk of source. When a fn body references a top-level
`var X`, that `var X` must already be declared at parse time.
Forward fn calls work (the parser pre-scans fn signatures);
forward GLOBAL VAR references DO NOT work.

This is the v5.7.32 cyrlint warning class
(`global-init-order forward-ref`) that mabda surfaced. The
compiler ALSO doesn't catch every shape — the warning is
opt-in via `cyrius lint`.

**The cyrius-lsp specific instance** (v5.7.39): the LSP
extension added cross-file go-to-def + documentSymbol via a
symbol-table backing buffer. The buffer was a top-level `var
symbol_table[N]`. The fns that read it (`SYMBOL_LOOKUP`,
`SYMBOL_INSERT`) were defined before the `var` line in the
source layout.

Pre-fix: the parser saw the fn defs first, registered them,
parsed their bodies → reached `symbol_table` reference →
"undefined variable." Post-fix: moved `var symbol_table[N]`
above all fn defs that reference it.

**Recognize again**: any "undefined variable" error on a top-
level var that VISUALLY appears in the source somewhere.
Check it appears BEFORE the fns that read it.

**Generalize**: any new top-level `var X` declared in
cyrius's own source (or any cyrius project) MUST go above the
fn bodies that reference it. The parser is single-pass for
globals.

Full backstory: v5_7.cyml's `lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions_v5_7_39`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "snapshot_ping_pong_lib_corruption"
description = "lib/*.cyr edits silently revert via install-snapshot copy-back during cyrius deps"
category = "cyrius"
---
Surfaced 2026-05-03 mid-v5.8.23 bite #2 when `lib/tagged.cyr`
edits reverted between Edit calls during the sum-type migration.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

Two-step ping-pong loop:

  1. `version-bump.sh` runs `install.sh --refresh-only` which
     copies `lib/*.cyr` from the repo INTO
     `~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/lib/` and `~/.cyrius/lib`.
  2. Subsequent `cyrius deps` resolution (e.g., during
     `check.sh`) copies the snapshot version BACK into the
     repo.

If you edit a `lib/*.cyr` file AFTER a `version-bump.sh` ran
(snapshot is now stale), the next `check.sh` invocation will
overwrite your edit with the prior snapshot.

== SYMPTOM ==

You make an Edit. You verify the change. You run `check.sh`.
Tests pass. But the next time you read the file, your edit is
GONE. cycc is also gone — the snapshot's version was pulled back
in along with the lib file, AND the snapshot's lib is what's
actually in `~/.cyrius/lib`.

== MITIGATION ==

Documented in CLAUDE.md "Snapshot-ping-pong protection
(cyrius-side `lib/*.cyr` edits)" subsection (added v5.8.26).
3-step recipe:

  1. Edit `lib/<file>.cyr`.
  2. Manually refresh the install snapshot:
     ```sh
     cp lib/<file>.cyr ~/.cyrius/versions/$(cat VERSION)/lib/<file>.cyr
     cp lib/<file>.cyr ~/.cyrius/lib/<file>.cyr
     ```
     OR run `sh scripts/version-bump.sh "$(cat VERSION)"` (same-
     version regenerate path).
  3. Run `sh scripts/check.sh` — file should now stick.

== RELATED ECOSYSTEM RULE ==

CLAUDE.md "Downstream repo setup (ecosystem rule)" forbids
downstream repos (mabda, sigil, sakshi, yukti, etc.) from
symlinking their `lib/` to `~/.cyrius/lib` because writes
through the symlink propagate up the chain into this repo.

Sakshi was found violating this at v5.8.23 (its `lib/` was a
directory-level symlink to `~/.cyrius/lib`); fixed via
`rm sakshi/lib && (cd sakshi && cyrius deps)`.

Audit script (also added to CLAUDE.md v5.8.26):
    find ~/Repos -maxdepth 2 -type l -name lib

Returns nothing now; sakshi was the only directory-level
violation. Other repos have only single-file `lib/<dep>.cyr`
symlinks (legitimate `cyrius deps` artifacts).

[[entries]]
name = "foldin_symlink_vs_regular_file"
description = "When folding a sibling distfile into cyrius's lib/ via the sandhi pattern, the vendored file MUST be a regular file (mode 100644), not the absolute-path symlink that `cyrius deps` creates by default. Symlinks point into the user's local cache (/home/<user>/.cyrius/...) and dangle on any other machine — including CI."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["foldin", "deps", "ci", "sandhi-pattern", "v5.8.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius deps` resolves a `[deps.<name>]` entry by
populating `~/.cyrius/deps/<name>/<tag>/` and then creating
`lib/<name>.cyr` as a SYMLINK to that cache path:
```
lib/sakshi.cyr -> /home/<user>/.cyrius/deps/sakshi/2.2.3/dist/sakshi.cyr
```
The symlink target is an ABSOLUTE PATH in the resolving user's
home. On the user's local machine this works because the cache
is populated. On any OTHER machine — including GitHub Actions
CI runners (whose home is `/home/runner/...`) — the symlink
dangles.

When folding a dep into stdlib (sandhi pattern), naive `git
add lib/<name>.cyr` commits the symlink. Git records mode 120000
(symlink). On checkout elsewhere, the symlink resolves against
a non-existent path → file effectively missing.

**Surfaces as**: tcyrs that `include "lib/<name>.cyr"` fail to
compile with "file not found" → cycc exits non-zero → under
`bash -e` (GHA's default shell mode), the test loop terminates
silently mid-loop. No `FAIL:` print, no summary, just exit 1.
Easy to misdiagnose as "test crashed the runner."

**The fix at v5.8.65** (the slot that surfaced this):
1. Convert the symlink to a regular file:
   ```
   cp --remove-destination "$(readlink lib/<name>.cyr)" lib/<name>.cyr
   ```
   `--remove-destination` is essential — `cp` without it follows
   the symlink and writes through to the cache, not the repo.
2. Commit the type change. `git diff` shows mode 120000 → 100644.
3. The cyrius distlib walker (`scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`) skips
   files matching `^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$` in the first
   5 lines — those byte-snapshot bundles must not be reformatted.
   See also `distlib_marker_styles_v5865`.

**Recognize again**: any time you fold a `[deps.X]` into `lib/`
under the sandhi pattern, audit `git ls-tree HEAD lib/<X>.cyr`
for mode 120000 BEFORE pushing. The v5.7.0 sandhi precedent
(`lib/sandhi.cyr`) is the canonical example — mode 100644,
never a symlink.

**Why it wasn't caught locally**: the user's local machine
HAD the symlink target populated. `check.sh` self-host green,
all 127 tcyrs pass via `cyrius test`. CI is the first place
the absolute path doesn't resolve.

The fix is one shell loop, but the diagnosis took multiple
re-pushes (audit-walk marker fix, then ci.yml inline marker
fan-out, then sakshi-specific bundle.sh marker style). Saved
here so the next foldin slot starts with the de-symlink step
in the playbook.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "heap_layout_relocation_option_a_vs_b"
description = "When growing a heap region 8x in-place collides with neighboring scratch state, the choice is Option A (in-place grow + shift downstream — monotonic, ~200+ edits) or Option B (relocate to fresh high block — non-monotonic, ~50 edits). v5.8.46 (tok arrays) + v5.8.59 (str_data) both took Option B; v5.8.61 reverted .59 back to monotonic once the gap was free."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap", "layout", "two-step-bootstrap", "v5.8.46", "v5.8.59", "v5.8.61"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius's heap regions have hardcoded offset
constants scattered across many files. Growing any region 8x
to absorb new data collides with whatever sits immediately
above. Two paradigms emerge:

**Option A — in-place grow + shift downstream**:
- Region stays at its original offset; size grows.
- Every region above shifts up by the size delta.
- Layout stays MONOTONIC (small-offset → big-offset reads
  cleanly).
- Cost: every offset literal in the affected band updates.
  v5.8.59 estimated this at ~200+ edits across ~20 files for
  the str_data 256 KB → 2 MB grow. Byte-identity risk grows
  linearly with edit count.

**Option B — relocate to fresh high block**:
- Region moves to a new offset above the highest-used heap.
- Size grows in the new location; old slot becomes empty.
- Layout is NON-MONOTONIC (small-offset reads jump past the
  vacant slot to the high block).
- Cost: only the offset literal changes (~50 edits).
- Precedent: v5.8.46 tok-array relocation moved
  tok_types/values/lines from `0x94A000`/`0xD4A000`/`0xF4A000`
  (tangled with output_buf) to a clean post-TS-region high
  block at `0x368C000`/`0x3E8C000`/`0x468C000`.

**The pattern that emerged**: take Option B during the
expansion slot to get the new capacity shipped fast and
audited cheaply. Pin a follow-up "monotonic reorg" slot for
when the layout-non-monotonicity becomes a maintenance
liability. v5.8.59 took Option B to ship NFKC/NFKD via the
str_data bump (placed at `0x4E8C000`); v5.8.61 reverted to
monotonic once the var_enum_id..preprocess_out gap was sized
right (str_data now at `0x21A000`). Two-step bootstrap
required at both: cycc (old layout) compiles cc5b (new layout);
cc5b compiles cc5b'; cc5b == cc5b' byte-identical at the new
size.

**When to use which**:
- New region or major cap raise → Option B initially.
- Cycle wind-down or pre-major-bump → fold back to Option A
  (monotonic) when the receiving gap is the right size.
- DO NOT skip the two-step bootstrap audit for either path —
  heap layout changes are byte-identity-critical.

**Trade-off note**: Option B's non-monotonicity makes future
heap-map readers do mental math (str_data jumped to where?).
The follow-up monotonic reorg pays this back, but only if the
slot pin holds (don't let the "we'll reorg next minor" become
"we'll reorg next major" — pre-v6.0 hardening pin exists for
exactly this drift, see `_aw_is_distlib_bundle` mention in
the heap-map block of src/main.cyr).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "preprocess_out_vs_str_data_caps"
description = "Two distinct heap caps gate compilation. str_data caps the bytes the COMPILER writes for tracked string literals (raised to 2 MB at v5.8.59). preprocess_out caps the bytes of EXPANDED SOURCE the preprocessor produces from include resolution (still 2 MB since v5.6.40). Hitting one isn't the same as hitting the other, and the workarounds are different."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap", "preprocessor", "str_data", "v5.8.60"]
content = """
**The trap**: at v5.8.60 (NFKC/NFKD), the original design
used 116-char fixed-width compat-decomp records. 3833 records
× 116 chars = ~445 KB hex-encoded source — well under the
v5.8.59-bumped str_data 2 MB cap. Compiled fine in isolation.

But every consumer tcyr that includes lib/unicode/normalize.cyr
(transitively pulling in _normalize_data.cyr's ~445 KB blob)
also includes the rest of stdlib via auto-prepend. The
*expanded source* — the post-preprocessor concatenation of
all includes — overflowed `preprocess_out` (the 2 MB buffer at
S+0x44A000):
```
error: expanded source exceeds 2MB (2114828 bytes)
```
str_data has plenty of room. preprocess_out is the wall.

**These are two DIFFERENT walls**:
- `str_data` (`0x21A000`, 2 MB at v5.8.61): bytes the compiler
  writes for tracked string literals during codegen. Cap
  applies per-translation-unit.
- `preprocess_out` (`0x44A000`, 2 MB since v5.6.40): bytes of
  the source after include resolution. Cap applies BEFORE the
  lexer even runs.

A consumer that auto-prepends 14 stdlib modules + 6 sibling
distlib modules can sit at 1.6 MB expanded source even before
tcyr-specific code. Adding a 445 KB data table easily blows
the cap.

**The fix at v5.8.60**: switch the data-table encoding from
fixed-width 116-char records (445 KB total) to a 2-table
split — sorted IDX (12 chars/record × 3833 = 46 KB) +
variable-width DATA blob (count + cps, ~41 KB). Total ~87 KB,
80% reduction. Same lookup semantics (binary search IDX → data
offset → read count + cps), but consumer-side preprocess_out
stays well under cap.

**Recognize again**: any new fold-target that adds large
data blobs to lib/ should be sized against BOTH caps:
- Single-file size vs 64 KB string-literal cap (token
  encoding's u16 length field).
- Aggregate across ALL auto-prepended stdlib + the consumer's
  own source vs preprocess_out 2 MB cap.

The 2 MB preprocess_out cap is the easier one to forget
because it's not in the compiler's diagnostic surface during
single-file edits — only manifests when consumers compile.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "relocs_stripped_vs_ovmf_v51149"
description = "OVMF firmware treats COFF Characteristics RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001) as 'load at exact ImageBase or fail'. UEFI Application binaries must NEVER set this bit, even with zero actual fixups. Standard UEFI loaders (rEFInd, systemd-boot, GRUB-EFI) all clear it"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "uefi", "ovmf", "efi-application", "relocs-stripped", "coff-characteristics", "v5.11.49", "gnoboot", "silent-rejection"]
---
**THE TRAP.** OVMF firmware treats the COFF Characteristics bit
`RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001)` as *"load at exact ImageBase or fail"*. A UEFI
Application must **NEVER** set it — even when the image genuinely has zero fixups
and setting it looks accurate. Every standard loader (rEFInd, systemd-boot,
GRUB-EFI) clears it.

**RECOGNIZE AGAIN**: a flag that is *descriptively true* about your output but is
read by the consumer as a *demand*. The structural gate passed — Subsystem 0xA,
NX_COMPAT, Data Dirs [1]/[12] zeroed, a valid `.text` — because it checked what the
bits SAY, not what a firmware DOES with them. Boot it.

⟳ Full narrative in `retros/v511x.cyml`.
[[entries]]
name = "macho_syscall_number_dual_definition_61_vs_217"
description = "SYS_GETDENTS64 is defined as 217 (x86/macos) AND 61 (aarch64-Linux) in different stdlib files; the wrapper binds 61 by include order, and the macho ESYSXLAT translated only 217→344, missing 61→344 — so arm64-macOS dir-walk silently EBADF'd. 'Works in a probe, fails in the wrapper' = a duplicate global bound differently by include order, NOT codegen."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "syscall", "getdirentries", "include-order", "v6.0.63"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius lib sync` (and any dir-walk — is_dir,
dir_list) returned EBADF on arm64 macOS and reported "snapshot
lib not found" on a directory that demonstrably exists. The .60
fix had already added a macho ESYSXLAT entry translating x86
getdents64 217 → Darwin getdirentries 344, and a standalone
probe calling dir_list WORKED — so it looked fixed and was
mis-filed as an "arm64 arg-corruption codegen bug."

**Why the probe lied**: `SYS_GETDENTS64` is defined in MULTIPLE
files with DIFFERENT values:
  lib/fs.cyr                    : var SYS_GETDENTS64 = 217;  (x86)
  lib/syscalls_macos.cyr        : 217
  lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr: 61   (the aarch64 number)
cyrius is "last definition wins". The wrapper (cbt/cyrius.cyr)
includes fs.cyr (217) THEN syscalls.cyr (→ aarch64 → 61), so it
binds **61**. A small probe with a different include order bound
217. Same source line, opposite value.

**Why 61 failed**: the macho ESYSXLAT chain translated x86
217→344 but had NO 61→344 entry — even though the wrapper/tools
pull the aarch64 stdlib (61). Untranslated 61 ran with a stale
x16 → garbage syscall → EBADF (-9). Instrumenting is_dir in the
wrapper showed `fd=3` (open OK) but `var sg = SYS_GETDENTS64`
loaded **61**, not 217.

**Fix** (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr, `_TARGET_MACHO==2`
ESYSXLAT block): add `cmp x8,#61; b.ne; movz x16,#344`
(0xF100F51F / 0x54000041 / 0xD2802B10, llvm-mc-verified) beside
the 217→344 entry. Both numbers now translate, robust to
include order.

**Recognize it again**: "works in a minimal probe, fails in the
wrapper/tools" → DON'T reach for a codegen hypothesis. Print the
actual VALUE of any global the failing path reads
(`var x = THE_GLOBAL;` + write it). A duplicate global bound
differently by include order is far more likely than
context-sensitive codegen. The WRAPPER (full include set), not a
probe, is the real test. See retros/v60x.cyml + the .63 CHANGELOG.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_atomic_on_global_needs_8_aligned_var_base"
description = "aarch64 ldxr/stxr (what atomic_cas/atomic_fetch_add compile to) MANDATORILY fault with SIGBUS on a non-8-aligned address, even where plain access is permitted. cyrius globals were only 4-byte aligned because the aarch64 ELF var-area base inherited the 4-alignment of the code size — so atomic_cas on a GLOBAL SIGBUS'd. x86 already rounds code to 8; aarch64 rounded to 4. Fix: round code size to 8 in src/backend/aarch64/fixup.cyr."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "atomic", "alignment", "ldxr", "sigbus", "fixup", "var-base", "v6.0.64"]
content = """
**The trap**: `atomic_cas(&someGlobal, ...)` SIGBUSes on
aarch64 (exit 135), while the SAME call on a heap pointer
(alloc returns 8-aligned) or a stack local works fine. The
v6.0.64 thread-safe allocator's `var _alloc_lock = 0;` was
the FIRST-ever atomic op on a global, so it was the first to
trip this — every prior `atomic_cas` user (thread.cyr mutex,
atomics.tcyr) used heap/stack cells.

**Why**: aarch64 `ldxr`/`stxr` (load/store-EXCLUSIVE — what
`atomic_cas` / `atomic_fetch_add` in lib/atomic.cyr compile
to) require natural (8-byte) alignment of the address and
fault on a misaligned one EVEN when SCTLR.A=0 lets ordinary
loads be unaligned. Exclusive accesses are special-cased by
the architecture; there is no unaligned `ldxr`.

**Root cause (the layout, NOT atomic.cyr)**: cyrius globals
live in the ELF var area whose RUNTIME base is
`0x400000 + 120 + cp` (`EMITELF`) / `entry + preamble + acp`
(the fixup `dbase`), where `cp` = code size. aarch64
instructions are 4 bytes, so `cp` is only 4-aligned; when
`cp ≡ 4 (mod 8)` the whole var area is 4-aligned and EVERY
global lands at a 4-aligned address. x86's fixup rounds code
to 8 (`cp = (cp+7)&~7`) precisely so its data base is
8-aligned; aarch64's fixup rounded to 4 (`(cp+3)&~4`).

**The tell**: `&g mod 8 == 4` on aarch64 but `== 0` on x86
for the SAME source. The layout is NOT arch-shared — the
byte OFFSET of a global is identical (shared frontend) but
the BASE alignment differs between backends.

**Fix** (v6.0.64, one line, `src/backend/aarch64/fixup.cyr`):
change the post-emit `cp = (cp + 3) & (0 - 4); SCP(S, cp);`
to `(cp + 7) & (0 - 8)`. `SCP` stores it, so `EMITELF`,
`EMITELF_KERNEL`, `EMITMACHO_ARM64`, and the `dbase` global
fixups all read one consistent size via `GCP` — no
inconsistency between where a global is addressed and where
it's written. ≤4 extra zero pad bytes in `.text` (never
executed). Mach-O is unaffected (its `__DATA` is page-aligned,
independent of `cp`). This makes atomic-on-ANY-global work on
aarch64, matching x86 — not just the allocator lock.

**Recognize it again**: any new aarch64 SIGBUS (exit 135) in
a program that does an atomic on a `var`-declared global, or
a "self-host failed on pi (rc=135)" when a stale
`build/cycc_aarch64` cross-compiler emits the old 4-aligned
base. Rebuild `build/cycc_aarch64` after the fixup change.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_atomic_cas_is_not_a_full_barrier"
description = "lib/atomic.cyr's atomic_cas is documented as 'a full barrier on both arches' — TRUE on x86 (lock cmpxchg) but FALSE on aarch64, where it's plain ldxr/stxr with NO dmb. A spinlock/mutex built on it needs an EXPLICIT post-CAS atomic_fence() for the acquire barrier (thread.cyr mutex_lock has it; the v6.0.64 alloc lock initially forgot it)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "atomic", "memory-ordering", "acquire-barrier", "spinlock", "weak-memory", "v6.0.64"]
content = """
**The trap**: building a lock as `while (atomic_cas(m,0,1)==0){}`
and assuming the CAS gives you the acquire barrier. It does on
x86 (`lock cmpxchg` is a full barrier) but NOT on aarch64:
lib/atomic.cyr's aarch64 `atomic_cas` is plain `ldxr`/`stxr`
with NO `dmb ish` (not the `ldaxr`/`stlxr` acquire/release
forms). So critical-section loads (e.g. `_heap_ptr`) can be
observed BEFORE the lock is actually held → stale read →
corruption that only manifests under real contention on a weak
memory model (invisible on x86, invisible single-threaded).

**The canonical pattern** (lib/thread.cyr `mutex_lock`):
spin-CAS to acquire, THEN `atomic_fence()` — the comment there
notes the fence is "a cheap no-op on x86" (cmpxchg already a
barrier) but REQUIRED on aarch64. Release mirrors `mutex_unlock`:
`atomic_fence(); atomic_store(m, 0);`.

**What bit us** (v6.0.64): `_alloc_lock_acquire` trusted
atomic.cyr's "full barrier on both arches" comment and omitted
the post-CAS fence. The release side was correct (it copied
mutex_unlock). Adversarial review caught the missing acquire
fence before ship; a 4-thread contended run on real aarch64
(pi) validated the fix. atomic.cyr's "full barrier" doc is
aspirational for aarch64 — treat `atomic_cas`/`atomic_fetch_add`
as ATOMIC but UNORDERED there, and fence explicitly.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "portable_sleep_no_raw_nanosleep_off_linux"
description = "Raw syscall(35) nanosleep faults off Linux: Darwin has NO plain nanosleep BSD syscall (and on aarch64 macho, 35 COLLIDES with unlinkat), and PE never routed 35. Portable sleep = poll(NULL,0,ms) on Linux+macOS (poll 7→230 is routed on both Mach-O backends) + a kernel32 Sleep reroute on Windows. WaitForSingleObject on a process/thread pseudo-handle does NOT block as a sleep."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "windows", "syscall", "nanosleep", "poll", "sleep", "esysxlat", "v6.0.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `syscall(35, &timespec, 0)` (Linux nanosleep) in a
stdlib/consumer sleep helper works on Linux and faults or
misbehaves on every other target:
- **macOS**: XNU has no plain `nanosleep` BSD syscall (libsystem
  builds it over semaphore primitives). Worse, on aarch64-macho
  the number 35 COLLIDES — ESYSXLAT maps aarch64 `35` to
  `unlinkat`→`unlink`, so `syscall(35, ts, 0)` tries to unlink a
  garbage path. (The x86-nanosleep-35 vs aarch64-unlinkat-35
  clash is the same multiply-defined-number swamp as the .63
  getdents 61-vs-217 bug.)
- **Windows (PE)**: 35 is not a routed reroute → fault.

**The portable fix** (v6.0.65, `lib/chrono.cyr` `sleep_ms`):
- Linux + macOS: `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` — `poll` (x86 7 → Darwin 230)
  is ALREADY routed on both Mach-O backends (the .59 socket port),
  and `poll` with 0 fds + a timeout is the portable ms-granular
  sleep. Verified ≈ms-accurate on Linux + ecb + ach (the constant
  ~300ms "overhead" the first time on macOS is one-shot
  codesign/AMFI launch, not poll — baseline-subtract to confirm).
- Windows: a real kernel32 `Sleep(ms)` reroute (0xF00F), 1-arg,
  mirroring the SRWLock reroutes.
- agnos/cx: no-op (no sleep syscall / single-threaded VM).

**Windows dead-end worth knowing**: `WaitForSingleObject(handle,
ms)` on the current-PROCESS (-1) or current-THREAD (-2)
pseudo-handle returns IMMEDIATELY — it does NOT block for the
timeout (verified on cass: 0 ms). It only blocks on a genuinely
non-signaled object. So there's no "sleep via the already-routed
WaitForSingleObject" shortcut; you need a real `Sleep` reroute.

**Measurement gotcha (cmd.exe)**: `cmd /c "echo %TIME% & prog &
echo %TIME%"` prints the SAME time twice — `%TIME%` expands at
parse time (once), the `%errorlevel%` gotcha applied to TIME. Use
`cmd /v:on /c "... !TIME! ..."` or PowerShell `Measure-Command`.

**Recognize again**: any consumer doing raw `syscall(35)` for a
sleep on macOS/Windows; or a new "syscall not routed on Mach-O"
warning naming 35. The fix is always: route through
`lib/chrono.cyr sleep_ms`, never hardcode nanosleep.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "callptr_indirect_call_frame_slot_spill_survives_win64_shadow"
description = "v6.0.70: compiler-emitted indirect calls (callptr → IR_CALL_INDIRECT, x86 `call [rbp-disp]` / aarch64 `ldr x6;blr x6`). The crux is callee-survival across ECALLPOPS: the obvious 'push callee, pop after ECALLPOPS' is WRONG on Win64 (ECALLPOPS does `sub rsp,0x20` for the shadow home → buries a stack-pushed callee) and on SysV n>6. The correct general fix is to spill the callee to a FRESH frame local before the args (rbp-relative → unaffected by ECALLPOPS's rsp moves, re-entrant for nested callptr, any arg count). aarch64 must load into x6 (x0-x5 are arg regs, x9 is the >6-arg shuffle scratch). RESOLVED at v6.0.86: on PE the callee is pushed on the stack rather than spilled to a frame slot, so nothing aliases an &-taken out-param; a real Win64 COM callee (dxgi_vram_bytes()=128 MB) verified on real cass hardware — see the next entry, callptr_callee_spill_aliases_addr_taken_local."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["callptr", "indirect-call", "ir_call_indirect", "com", "vtable", "win64", "shadow-space", "frame-slot", "ffi", "v6.0.70"]
content = """
**Compiler-emitted indirect call (callptr/IR_CALL_INDIRECT) — the
callee-survival problem is the whole game.** To call through a computed
pointer (a COM vtable method, a fn-ptr value), the callee value must be
LIVE in a register at the `call`/`blr` — but it has to survive (a) the
arg evaluation (which uses the rax/x0 accumulator) and (b) ECALLPOPS
(which marshals the pushed args into arg regs and, on Win64, `sub rsp,0x20`
for the shadow home).

**The wrong design (the understand-workflow proposed it, it's WRONG for
Win64):** push the callee FIRST (deepest), push args, ECALLPOPS, then POP
the callee into rax and `call rax`. This only works on SysV n<=6. On Win64,
ECALLPOPS does `sub rsp,0x20` AFTER draining the args, so the callee
(pushed before the args) is now BURIED 0x20 below rsp — popping gets the
shadow, not the callee. SysV n>6 also shuffles/drops slots. Verify against
ECALLPOPS (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr) before trusting any callee-survival
scheme — the shadow `sub rsp` is the trap.

**The right design (frame-slot spill):** in the callptr parser arm,
allocate a FRESH frame local (`GFLC` → `EFLSTORE` → `SFLC+1`), spill the
evaluated callee there BEFORE pushing args, then after ECALLPOPS emit
ECALLIND = `call [rbp-disp]` (x86, FF /2 mod=10 rm=101) / `ldr x6,[x29-disp]
; blr x6` (aarch64). rbp/x29-relative → immune to ECALLPOPS's rsp moves;
re-entrant (nested callptr → distinct slots, since each grabs the next GFLC
slot); works for any arg count + ABI. The frame auto-grows (EPATCHFRAME
sizes from GFLC at fn-end). IR_CALL_INDIRECT carries the spill idx (arg1);
its DCE predicates: clobbers_rax (return), def_rcx_any (caller-save),
uses_rax conservatively (matches the call cohort).

**aarch64 register: x6, not x0.** ECALLPOPS loads arg0 into x0 and uses x9
as the >6-arg memory shuffle. x6 is outside the 6 arg regs (x0-x5) and
isn't x9 → it survives ECALLPOPS for any argc. (lib/fnptr.cyr's asm uses
x9, but that's the boilerplate being replaced — don't copy x9.)

**callptr needs a function frame.** The spill is a frame local, so callptr
at TOP LEVEL is a loud compile error (top-level vars are globals → no rbp
frame; `_cur_fn_ix < 0` detects it). cx has no indirect-call bytecode op →
loud error there too. Both fail loud, never silent-wrong.

**Open gotcha (v6.0.71):** callptr to a TRIVIAL cyrius callee works on all
4 hosts (incl. Win64, loaded-callee 2/3-arg). But callptr to a REAL Win64
COM callee (a DXGI vtable method) corrupts the cyrius caller frame on cass
(RC=5 / access-violation) even when the COM call returns S_OK — NOT a slot
error (slot 7 and 12 both fail) and NOT the primitive (verified). A subtle
real-Win64-callee interaction; needs windbg on cass to pin. See
issues/2026-06-05-windows-com-vtable-real-callee-frame-corruption.md.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macos_owns_thread_pointer_register"
description = "macOS owns TPIDR_EL0 (arm64) / %fs (x86) — Darwin saves+restores it as thread state and overwrites any userspace `msr TPIDR_EL0` / arch_prctl on the FIRST preemption, so cyrius cannot durably own the thread-pointer register for thread-local storage on macOS."
detail = """
v6.0.84. Symptom: sigil's crypto-scratch bank (`cbank()` → `thread_local_get`,
lib/thread_local.cyr) SIGSEGV'd on real ecb (Apple Silicon) AFTER an ECDSA
sign — but every ingredient (sha256, hmac, fl_alloc, the secret vars, the
rfc6979 reject loop) ran clean in isolation. The .76 issue chased it to a
`&_fl_heads` freelist codegen bug via an early lldb; THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG —
the `&global` adrp+add path is provably correct (EVADDR emits a fresh
adrp+add per site, FIXUP_ADRP_ADD masks 0x9F00001F so it can't corrupt the
opcode, var-address resolution is deterministic per index).

Real cause: thread_local.cyr installs a per-thread block via `msr TPIDR_EL0`
(arm64) and reads it back via `mrs TPIDR_EL0`. On Linux the kernel lets EL0
own TPIDR_EL0. On Darwin the kernel uses it for its own thread self-data and
RESTORES it on every context switch. Decisive repro on ecb: install the
block (TPIDR=0x1xxxxxxxx), run a 2-billion-iteration pure-compute loop with
ZERO syscalls, read TPIDR again → 0x200d (a Darwin thread value). Pure
preemption, no syscall, clobbers it. `cbank()` lazy-inits ONCE
(`_crypto_tls_inited` guard), so the first preemption during crypto poisons
every later `thread_local_get`. This is why it looked "context-dependent,
after a preceding svc" — it's preemption, and a long crypto op always gets
preempted.

How to recognize: lldb shows the fault at `ldr xN, [x1, x0, lsl #3]` right
after `mrs x1, TPIDR_EL0`, with x1 a tiny value (~0x2010), NOT a heap addr.

Fix: a macOS process-global slot array (no TPIDR), like the Windows TlsAlloc
path. Safe because cyrius threads don't run on macOS at all — thread.cyr is
clone(2)-only (no Darwin bsdthread path) — so crypto-scratch is
single-threaded. Since preemption (not a syscall) is the trigger, NO
"re-msr before access" scheme is reliable; you must not use the register.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_socketpair_dual_number_199_vs_53"
description = "socketpair's syscall number differs by macho arch: arm-macho pulls syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr → 199; x86-macho pulls syscalls_macos.cyr → 53. The ESYSXLAT must map BOTH to Darwin 135 (in their respective backends). And on the arm enum, 53 is fchmodat (already mapped) — reusing 53 there collides."
detail = """
v6.0.84 (cf. the v6.0.63 getdents64 61-vs-217 dual-number trap). There is no
syscalls_aarch64_macos.cyr: lib/syscalls.cyr routes arm-macho to
syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr (aarch64-Linux numbers) and x86-macho to
syscalls_macos.cyr (x86 numbers). So a socket added only as the x86 number is
DEAD on arm-macho. socketpair was the last untranslated one (the .59 socket
surface added socket/connect/accept/bind/listen but not socketpair): the
fork+socketpair TLS e2e (tls12_handshake.tcyr) ran svc with a stale x16 →
garbage fds → TLS_ERR_IO on every read/write. Symptom of a totally
untranslated number on Darwin: SIGSYS (exit 140 = 128+12).

Fix: aarch64-macho ESYSXLAT `cmp x8,#199; movz x16,#135` (NOT 53 — 53 is
fchmodat on the aarch64-Linux enum, already mapped to chmod with an arg-shift,
so a 53 entry both misses real socketpair AND corrupts fchmodat). x86-macho
EMACHO_SYSXLAT `_msx(S, 53, 0x2000087)` (53 is socketpair on the x86 enum,
no collision). Keep `_macho_arm_routes` in lockstep (it whitelists the
unrouted-syscall warning). Encodings: `as -arch arm64` on ecb.

RESOLVED (v6.2.24): the socket FAMILY (socket/connect/bind/listen/setsockopt)
is now mapped in the aarch64-macho ESYSXLAT block too (socket 198→97, connect
203→98, bind 200→104, listen 201→106, setsockopt 208→105 — the "aarch64-Linux
socket numbers → Darwin" branch in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr). Real-network
sockets on arm-macho are no longer x86-only-mapped; the .84 STILL-LATENT note
is closed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "callptr_callee_spill_aliases_addr_taken_local"
description = "The callptr indirect-call callee-spill frame slot (v6.0.70) can ALIAS a `&`-taken local. When a function passes `&local` as a COM/FFI out-param and the callptr spills its callee to a GFLC frame slot, the two can land on the SAME rbp offset → the out-param write and the spilled callee fight → frame/regalloc corruption → AV on the NEXT indirect dispatch."
detail = """
v6.0.86. Symptom: lib/dxgi.cyr dxgi_vram_bytes() AV'd (0xC0000005) on real DXGI
hardware (cass) — factory + EnumAdapters1 worked, then the GetDesc1 dispatch
crashed. Looked GPU-specific + windbg-needed; it was neither.

cdb on cass (C:\\dbg\\cdb.exe — the debugger was present; a Program-Files-only +
minimal-ssh-PATH `where` search wrongly reported it absent) gave the fault:
  mov eax,0x50 ; pop rcx ; add rax,rcx ; mov rax,[rax]   with rcx(avtbl)=0
i.e. load64(avtbl + 10*8) [GetDesc1] with avtbl=0. avtbl = load64(pAdapter);
pAdapter pointed at zeroed mem. llvm-objdump of the PE showed the smoking gun:
  0x1843  mov [rbp-0x50], rax    ; EnumAdapters1 callptr callee-spill (cp_idx)
  0x1855  lea rax, [rbp-0x50]    ; &pAdapter (EnumAdapters1's out-param)
SAME slot. The v6.0.70 callptr spills the callee to `cp_idx = GFLC` and calls
`[rbp+disp(cp_idx)]`; here GFLC handed it the slot a `&`-taken local already
owned. The collision corrupted the frame/regalloc enough that a later
regalloc'd var (avtbl in r12) read stale 0.

Why it hid: frame-LAYOUT-dependent (which callptr collides shifts as you add/
remove locals — adding one local moved the AV from GetDesc1 to EnumAdapters1);
real-Win64-callee-only (a cyrius callee writes near rbp, a system DLL writes
near rsp + uses the out-param pointer hard); and INVISIBLE to self-host because
cycc has no callptr (callptr is a consumer/FFI feature) — so the bug shipped
green through every self-host gate. The v6.0.71 ECALLPTR_PE force-16-align was a
SEPARATE, real fix (SSE-spill alignment), not this.

Fix: on PE, don't spill the callee to a frame slot at all — push it on the
STACK (parser pushes it FIRST, below the args), then in ECALLPTR_PE pop the args
into rcx/rdx/r8/r9, `pop rax` (the callee, now on top), align + 32B shadow,
`call rax`. No frame slot → nothing can alias it. The non-PE path keeps the
frame-slot spill + ECALLIND (its ECALLPOPS shadow would bury a stack callee) —
unchanged, byte-identical. Verified: dxgi_vram_bytes()=128MB on cass UHD 600 +
callptr_real_win64 kernel32 regression=42 + 4-host self-host byte-identical.

Lesson echo: don't take a "needs a debugger we don't have / needs special
hardware" verdict as gospel — the debugger was on the box and the integrated GPU
reproduced it fine. See methodology / the hardware-directly field note.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "getenv_on_agnos_and_local_kernel_testability"
description = "getenv() on agnos walks the exec-stack envp from _agnos_init_rsp (agnos has no /proc); and the agnos kernel is locally testable under QEMU — rebuild + boot it and run a ring-3 cyrius binary, don't claim 'no agnos to test on'."
detail = """
v6.0.87. Two linked notes.

(1) getenv() on agnos. agnos has no /proc/self/environ (the Linux source). The
agnos kernel (1.43.2) stages a default envp (HOME=/, PWD=/) on the SysV exec
init stack. cyrius getenv() delegates under CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS to _agnos_getenv
in lib/args_agnos.cyr, which reads the envp from the entry init-rsp captured at
startup (the same _agnos_init_rsp that argc/argv use):
    envp[j] = load64(_agnos_init_rsp + 8 + (argc+1+j)*8)   # NUL-terminated
and matches "KEY=". The delegation lives in args_agnos.cyr (not io.cyr) because
io precedes args in the stdlib include order, so io.cyr calling _agnos_getenv is
a forward FUNCTION ref (REGFN resolves it) — a forward GLOBAL ref to
_agnos_init_rsp from io.cyr would not resolve. Every agnos program already links
args_agnos (the cycc entry emits `call _agnos_capture_rsp` for _TARGET_AGNOS), so
the global is always present.

(2) The agnos kernel is LOCALLY TESTABLE — do not claim "no agnos to run on."
   - rebuild current:  cd agnos && sh scripts/build.sh   (-> build/agnos, 1.43.2)
   - boot + run a ring-3 program under QEMU: agnos/scripts/agnsh-smoke.sh boots a
     production kernel with an ext2 rootfs whose /bin/agnsh it execs in ring 3;
     AGNSH_BIN=<your.agnos> overrides that program. exec-smoke.sh is the
     EXEC_SELFTEST disk-exec path. Both capture the serial output.
   - I verified cyrius getenv on the real kernel this way: built a probe
     (cyrius build --agnos) that prints getenv("HOME"/"PWD"/"NOPE"), ran it as
     AGNSH_BIN -> serial showed HOME=/ PWD=/ NOPE=(null). Requires
     qemu-system-x86_64 + OVMF + gnoboot built at ../gnoboot/build/.
   The kernel needs to be CURRENT (rebuild from source) — an older build/agnos
   predates the 1.43.2 envp staging and getenv would read an empty envp.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "emit_fmt_macho_before_pe_precedence"
description = "When deriving an output-format selector (`_emit_fmt`) from the _TARGET_* flags, test Mach-O BEFORE PE — matching the pre-existing EMITELF ladder's order. _TARGET_MACHO and _TARGET_PE come from INDEPENDENT, unguarded env vars (CYRIUS_MACHO / CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN; main.cyr has no mutual-exclusion guard), so the combined state is reachable. The old code let Mach-O win; flip the order and you silently change the conflicting-combo output (Mach-O → PE)."
detail = """
v6.0.89 (the _TARGET_* consolidation first bite: collapse the format fan-out +
entry-base ladder, duplicated in BOTH x86/fixup.cyr and aarch64/fixup.cyr, into
_emit_fmt(S)→{0=ELF,1=Mach-O-x86,2=Mach-O-ARM,3=PE} + _entry_base(S)).

An adversarial-review workflow caught a DRAFT _emit_fmt that checked `_TARGET_PE==1`
first: for `CYRIUS_MACHO=1 CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1` the old EMITELF (macho-first) emitted
Mach-O 8192B, the draft emitted PE 1536B — a real divergence, not a false alarm,
even though no sane build sets both. Fix: `if(_TARGET_MACHO==1)…;if(_TARGET_MACHO==2)
…;if(_TARGET_PE==1)…` so Mach-O wins, byte-identical for that combo too.

VERIFICATION SHAPE (this is the most fixup-fragile code — entry feeds every absolute
fixup): build .PREV-vs-.NEW compilers and diff EMITTED binaries across EVERY mode —
ELF-user, kernel ELF32+ELF64, shared, object, EFI (real source), AGNOS, PE, Mach-O
x86, Mach-O ARM, AND the Mach-O+PE conflict. A single-format self-host run only
exercises ELF; the macho/pe/kernel paths need the explicit matrix + ecb/ach/cass.
TEST-HARNESS TRAP that wasted a cycle: zsh does NOT word-split an unquoted `$var`, so
`env $combo sh -c …` with combo="A=1 B=1" set ONE malformed var and silently tested
the no-flags case — the output's ELF magic (not Mach-O) gave it away. Use literal
`env A=1 B=1 …`, and ALWAYS check the actual output bytes, not just the exit code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "new_pe_emitter_needs_aarch64_strict_stub"
description = "Any NEW PE syscall-reroute emitter `E*_PE` (added to backend/x86/emit.cyr) must also get a `fn E*_PE(S) { return 0; }` stub in backend/aarch64/emit.cyr. parse_expr.cyr references the emitter unconditionally inside its statically-dead `if (_TARGET_PE == 1)` block, so cycc's symbol resolution needs the symbol to exist when the aarch64 backend builds — a missing stub fires `warning: undefined function 'E*_PE'`, which is FATAL under `--strict`. v6.1.17."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pe", "aarch64", "stub", "symbol-resolution", "strict", "syscall-reroute", "v6.1.17"]
content = """
**The trap**: v6.1.17 added `ENANOSLEEP_PE` to `backend/x86/emit.cyr`
(routes `nanosleep(35)` → `kernel32!Sleep`) and wired it into
`parse_expr.cyr`'s `_TARGET_PE == 1` block — both the literal
`sc_num == 35` case and the `EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC` argc==3 candidate.
The x86 build + local self-host + cass all passed. But the
**aarch64** cross-build fired `warning: undefined function
'ENANOSLEEP_PE'` (twice). It only *warns* (non-fatal) on a plain
build — so a forgotten stub sails through unless you build `--strict`
(CI / `cyrius build --strict`), where it's a hard error.

**Why**: `parse_expr.cyr` calls every `E*_PE` emitter unconditionally
inside `if (_TARGET_PE == 1)`. On aarch64 `_TARGET_PE` is always 0 so
the branch is statically dead at runtime, but cycc resolves symbols
before that's known, so the fn must exist. `backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`
therefore carries a `return 0` stub for the ENTIRE PE cohort
(EWRITE_PE, EGETTICKS_PE, ECREATEDIR_PE, EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC, …). A
new emitter is the one piece you forget.

**The fix**: add `fn ENANOSLEEP_PE(S): i64 { return 0; }` to the
aarch64 cohort. (The `cx` backend does NOT stub these — it already
emits the undefined-fn warning for the whole cohort and builds anyway,
because it isn't a `--strict` target; matching that means you leave cx
alone, you don't stub only your one new fn there.)

Recognize it: a new `E*_PE` lands, x86 + Windows are green, then a
`--strict` aarch64/macOS build (or CI) breaks on "undefined function."
Grep `backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` for the existing `_PE) { return 0; }`
cohort and add yours next to `EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "per_fn_local_tables_indexed_by_slot_not_var"
description = "The four per-fn local tables (fn_local_names / fn_local_depths / fn_local_types / fn_local_slice) are indexed by SFLC, which counts stack-frame SLOTS, NOT vars — a `stack var buf[N]` registers N/8 filler slots — so any frame larger than ~2 KB silently overran the old 256/576-entry tables (CVE-24). v6.1.40 relocated all four to the heap top at 16384 slots each + added a loud SFLC cap."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "local-tables", "sflc", "heap-cap", "overflow", "cve-24", "v6.1.40"]
content = """
**The trap (CVE-24)**: cyrius's four per-function local tables —
`fn_local_names`, `fn_local_depths`, `fn_local_types`,
`fn_local_slice` — are indexed by `SFLC` (the stack-frame local
count). `SFLC` counts stack-frame **SLOTS**, not user-visible
variables. A `stack var buf[N]` (a byte array on the frame) doesn't
register as one entry — it registers **N/8 filler slots** (one per
8-byte slot the array occupies). So a single `stack var buf[2048]`
burns 256 table entries by itself.

The old tables were sized 256 / 576 entries. Any function whose
frame exceeded ~2 KB of slot-arrays silently ran `SFLC` past the
table end and wrote past the region — a heap overrun with no
diagnostic. Self-host never tripped it (cycc's own fns stay small),
so it was invisible until a consumer with a large stack buffer (the
daimon report) hit it.

**The fix (v6.1.40)**: relocate all four tables to the heap top,
each sized **16384 slots**:

  - fn_local_names   @ 0x5D9D000
  - fn_local_depths  @ 0x5DBD000
  - fn_local_types   @ 0x5DDD000
  - fn_local_slice   @ 0x5DFD000

plus a LOUD `SFLC` cap check (error + exit, not silent truncation)
so a future overrun reports the slot count and the file to raise,
instead of corrupting the heap.

**The footgun behind the report — local vs global array sizing**:
the array-size convention differs by scope, which is exactly what
let the frame grow past expectation:

  - **local** `var a[N]` = **N BYTES**, rounded UP to 8 (so
    `var a[5]` still consumes one 8-byte slot; `var a[2048]`
    consumes 256 slots).
  - **global** `var a[N]` = **N i64 SLOTS** (N*8 bytes).

So a buffer that looks identical in source (`var a[2048]`) is 2 KB
on the stack but 16 KB in `.bss` as a global. Size local arrays by
BYTE count; size globals by slot count. Mixing the two mental
models is how the daimon frame quietly grew past the old cap.

**Recognize again**: any "large stack buffer corrupts compiler
state / heap" shape, or a consumer whose fn has a multi-KB
`stack var buf[N]`. Check `SFLC` against the table cap; the slot
count is `frame_bytes / 8`, not the var count. Related:
`large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack` (the dual — big
fn-local arrays land in `.bss`, not the stack frame).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "phase0_growable_grow_path_self_host_invisible"
description = "The v6.2.0 growable pressure tables (fixup_tbl, fn-tables, codebuf) never grow during cycc's own self-compile — cycc is at 6486/8192 fns and ~1 MB of a 3 MB codebuf, so the realloc / rehash / relocation code is dead on the self-host path AND on every check.sh test. A bug in the grow/rehash logic is therefore invisible to byte-identical self-host. The ONLY thing that catches it is a forced-grow test: shrink the initial cap, self-compile, and cmp against the no-grow reference."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["growable", "self-host-invisible", "fn-tables", "codebuf", "fixup", "testing", "v6.2.0"]
content = """
**The trap**: Phase 0 (v6.2.0) made three fixed tables growable —
`fixup_tbl` (`_fixup_grow`), the 16 fn-tables + 2 hashes
(`_fnt_grow`, with a name-hash rehash), and `codebuf`
(`_codebuf_grow`, off-heap relocate). All three GROW paths are
**cold on every path the test suite walks**: cycc self-compiles
with ~6486 functions (the cap starts at 8192) and ~1 MB of
generated code (the codebuf cap starts at 3 MB), so neither
`_fnt_grow` nor `_codebuf_grow` ever fires. A subtle bug — a wrong
copy length, a stale base pointer after relocation, a botched
rehash — produces a **byte-identical self-host and a green
check.sh** while silently corrupting any real consumer that does
cross the cap.

**The only test that exercises it**: artificially shrink the
initial cap so a NORMAL self-compile is forced through many
grows, then prove the output is unchanged:

  # fn-tables: set `_fnt_cap = 64` / `_fnt_hash_mask = 63` in the driver,
  # build cycc_small, then:
  cat src/main.cyr | ./cycc_small > out      # forces ~7 grows + rehashes
  cat src/main.cyr | ./build/cycc > ref      # no-grow reference (cap 8192)
  cmp out ref                                # MUST be identical

  # codebuf: `_codebuf_cap = 8192` → ~7 relocations; same cmp.

Run the forced-grow test on **both** x86 AND aarch64 — the
aarch64 start-hash build + `live[]` bitmap are mirrored code; the
x86 forced-grow does not cover them. The aarch64 variant: shrink
the cap in `main_aarch64.cyr` and run `cross-os-selfhost.sh pi`
(it self-hosts on real ARM, which forces the grow there).

**Recognize again**: ANY migration of a fixed cap/table to
growable, where the compiler's own usage stays under the cap.
"Self-host is byte-identical" proves the RELOCATION-equivalent
(no-grow) path; it says nothing about the grow path. Mandatory
forced-grow + differential-corpus before trusting it. Sibling
trap: `phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap"
description = "Two mechanics that bite when migrating cycc's fixed tables to growable: (1) a relocating buffer (codebuf) is only safe if every access re-derives from the base gvar and never holds a captured pointer across an EB — and the disp32 patch sites do an 8-byte read-modify-write that bypasses EB's cap-check, so the grow needs a guard band; (2) the 8192 fn-cap was a magic literal scattered across 6 subsystems and textually colliding with 5 UNRELATED 8192-capped tables, so the swap had to be surgical per-context, never a global replace."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["growable", "codebuf", "fn-tables", "relocation", "magic-number", "v6.2.0"]
content = """
**Relocation safety (codebuf)**: `_codebuf_grow` `alloc()`s a new
buffer and repoints `_codebuf_base`. That is only correct because
EVERY codebuf access re-derives `_codebuf_base + offset` fresh:
- the disp32 patch sites (`var a = _codebuf_base + GCP(S); S64(a, …)`)
  capture-and-store with NO `EB` in between, so no grow can run
  between the capture and the use;
- the DSE/LASE/regalloc peephole passes read+patch already-emitted
  code AFTER all emit, when the buffer is stable.
A pattern that captured `_codebuf_base + cp` and then called `EB`
(which can grow → relocate) before using it would dereference the
freed old buffer. There is none today — keep it that way.

The disp32 stores also do an 8-byte read-modify-write at `cp`
(`S64(a, (L64(a) >> 32 << 32) | disp)`), writing 4 bytes PAST the
4-byte slot, and they bypass `EB`'s cap-check entirely. So
`_codebuf_grow` triggers at `cp >= _codebuf_cap - 64` (a 64-byte
guard band), not at the exact cap, or the last RMW before a grow
would write past the buffer end. The cx bytecode backend has its
OWN codebuf at `0x54A000` (separate region, still fixed 512 KB) —
it is NOT `_codebuf_base`.

**The scattered magic number (fn-tables)**: `8192` (the fn-cap)
was hard-coded in ~6 independent places — `REGFN`'s cap-check,
the `fn_name_hash` + `fn_start_hash` probe/mask/clear bounds, the
`live[1024]` DCE bitmap, and the 85 %-full warning — with NO single
source of truth. Worse, the literal `8192` also appears in 5
UNRELATED capped tables (var table, IR pool, PE reloc, struct
field pool, the TS scratch stack). A blind `8192 → _fnt_cap` would
have corrupted those. The migration was surgical per-context:
`& 8191 → & _fnt_hash_mask` (hash masks only), `probes/sh_clr <
8192 → _fnt_cap` (hash bounds only), `live[1024] → live[4096]`
(the DCE bitmap, sized to the 32768 ceiling), REGFN's check → the
grow call. Everything now reads one `_fnt_cap`.

**Recognize again**: any "raise/remove a fixed cap" task where the
cap value is a bare literal. Grep first; a literal that doubles as
an unrelated table's size will silently break that table.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_target_stub_predicated_on_obsolete_kernel_surface"
description = "A `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS return 0;` stub written against an EARLIER kernel ABI silently rots once the kernel grows the missing syscall — the stub keeps 'working' (returns a deterministic 0) so nothing fails loud, while every consumer re-rolls a direct-syscall workaround. Re-check the live kernel ABI before trusting a frozen-surface comment."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["agnos", "stub", "syscall", "chrono", "stale", "v6.2.6"]
content = """
**The trap**: `lib/chrono.cyr`'s AGNOS branches for monotonic time
(`clock_now_ns()`) and `sleep_ms()` were fixed-`return 0` / no-op
stubs, with a comment justifying them — "agnos has no monotonic /
sleep syscall in the frozen 0-33 surface (228=clock_gettime,
35=nanosleep are out of range)." True when WRITTEN (v6.0.56). But
the agnos kernel grew `uptime_ms`#40 + `sleep_ms`#41 at 1.43.x (the
cyrius-doom frame clock / pacing primitives). The stubs never
faulted — `clock_now_ms()` just always returned 0 and `sleep_ms()`
busy-spun — so no gate, no smoke, no self-host caught it. It
surfaced only when the v6.2.5 `dig`/`yo` net-tool ports needed real
timing and had to call `syscall(40)`/`syscall(41)` DIRECTLY,
bypassing chrono. Classic "found by ports": the placebo that returns
a plausible value is more dangerous than one that crashes.

**The fix** (v6.2.6): add `sys_uptime_ms`#40 / `sys_sleep_ms`#41
wrappers (new `SysNrAgnosTimer` enum) to
`lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr`; point chrono's agnos branches at
them (`sleep_ms()` agnos branch calls `sys_sleep_ms(ms)`, guard
`ms<=0`).

⟳ **SUPERSEDED IN PART at v6.6.1.** This fix bound
`clock_now_ns()` = `sys_uptime_ms()*1e6` — syscall **#40**, backed by
`timer_ticks`, which is driven by the timer interrupt and therefore
**FROZEN whenever interrupts are cleared**. So the .6 fix replaced a
placebo that returned 0 with one that returned *a plausible constant*:
any elapsed-time measurement taken across a critical section or in
early boot read the same value twice and reported **zero elapsed
time** — a stopped clock, not a slow one, and strictly harder to spot
than the original stub. `clock_now_ns()` is now `sys_uptime_us()*1000`
(**#95**, `rdtsc`), which advances with interrupts disabled. Verified
at the instruction level: the AGNOS build emits `movl $0x5f, %eax`
(95) and the Linux path still issues `0xe4` (228). Gated by
`tests/gates/platform/agnos_monotonic_clock_rdtsc.sh`. Wall-clock#46 was
already correctly bound at v6.2.3 — this was the monotonic+sleep
counterpart it didn't cover. Extended `agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh`
with a chrono monotonic/sleep probe so a re-stub is now caught.

**Recognize again**: any `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS return 0;`
(or no-op) guarded by a comment naming a kernel version / "frozen
0-N surface." That comment is a timestamp, not an invariant — the
kernel ABI doc (`agnos/docs/development/agnos-userland-abi.md`) is
canonical and grows. Grep the doc for the capability before
trusting the stub; a stub that returns a deterministic 0 will not
fail loud when the assumption expires.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "mod_reserved_keyword_and_cve32_traversal_replication_v6250"
description = "Two traps from the v6.2.50 Phase C modular resolver: (1) `mod` is a RESERVED cyrius keyword — a var/param named `mod` fails to parse ('expected identifier, got reserved keyword'); use `submod`. (2) A NEW filesystem-write path that takes dep-controlled names MUST replicate the existing path-traversal guard — _dep_pull_submodule added sys_unlink+copy on `lib/<pkg>_<submod>.cyr` but the guard lived only in _dep_copy_file (CVE-04), and the unlink ran BEFORE it -> arbitrary .cyr deletion from a malicious index.cyml (CVE-32, fixed v6.2.51). Validate names at EVERY ingestion point before any fs op."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrius.cyml", "deps", "distlib", "modular", "reserved-keyword", "path-traversal", "CVE-32", "security", "v6.2.50", "v6.2.51"]
content = """
**Two traps from the Phase C modular resolver (v6.2.50/.51).**

(1) `mod` is a RESERVED cyrius keyword. Naming a variable or fn parameter `mod`
fails to compile: `expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'mod' (cannot be
used as an identifier)`. Hit while writing `_dep_pull_submodule(dep_src, pkg,
mod, ...)` in cbt/deps.cyr — renamed the param to `submod`. (Same class as
`secret`/`pub`/`shared`.) Grep the keyword set before naming dep/module vars.

(2) **Replicate the traversal guard at EVERY new fs-write ingestion point.**
The .50 `--modular` resolver added a fresh write path: `_dep_pull_submodule`
does `sys_unlink(dst)` + `_dep_copy_file(src, dst)` where
`dst = lib/<pkg>_<submod>.cyr` and `submod` comes from a dep-controlled
`dist/<pkg>/index.cyml` (the sibling-dep list). The project already had the
CVE-04 guard — but it lived INSIDE `_dep_copy_file`, and the new `sys_unlink`
ran BEFORE that call. So a malicious index sibling like `a/../../../x` escaped
lib/ and `sys_unlink`'d an arbitrary `.cyr` during automatic `cyrius deps`
(CVE-32, P1). This is the exact dep-resolver injection class hardened at
v6.1.33 (CVE-14/15/16) that the new path silently failed to replicate.

Fix (v6.2.51): a shared `_dep_reject_unsafe_name(name)` (reject any `/` or `..`)
called at ALL ingestion points — the sub-module name at the TOP of
`_dep_pull_submodule` (before any fs op), each index-returned `lib:<leaf>`, and
the producer-side `pkg_name` in `_distlib_modular_emit`. Regression:
`_deps_modular_traversal_gate`. LESSON: when you add a code path that turns
externally-controlled strings into filesystem paths, the guard goes at the new
path's first op — an existing guard one call deeper does not cover an earlier
unlink/open. Audit "every place dep-controlled data becomes a path," not "the
copy helper."
"""

[[entries]]
name = "wx_separate_pt_load_segments"
description = "v6.3.12 split every userland ELF from one RWE PT_LOAD into two — text R E (not writable) + data R W (not executable) — for segment-level W^X (agnos 1.50.6 loader is PF_X-aware). DEFAULT ON; CYRIUS_WX=0 opts out. The split is hard because the code's absolute/PC-relative var+string relocations are patched in FIXUP against dbase = entry + acp (the data base), so EMITELF_USER's 2nd PT_LOAD p_vaddr MUST equal that same dbase or the patched code reads the wrong addresses. Both derive it from a shared _wx_data_vaddr(base + text_foff, cp) that rounds the code-area end up to a fresh 2 MB page (agnos huge-page granularity). Adding a 2nd 56-byte program header pushes .text from file offset 120 to 176, so _entry_base + e_entry must shift to base+176 under W^X. Flipping the default to ON needs a TWO-STEP BOOTSTRAP because the old single-segment cycc cannot emit W^X."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["W^X", "ELF", "PT_LOAD", "cyrld", "FIXUP", "dbase", "two-step-bootstrap", "segments", "v6.3.12"]
body = """
**The lockstep invariant.** In cyrius, FIXUP patches every global-var and string
reference in the emitted code against `dbase = entry + acp` (data base = end of the
aligned code area): a string is at `dbase + totvar + idx`, a gvar at
`dbase + pfx[idx]`. EMITELF_USER then serializes the data at a matching vaddr. To
put data on its own permission-separated PT_LOAD, you MUST change `dbase` in BOTH
places identically — pad `acp` in FIXUP so `dbase` lands on a fresh page, and emit
the 2nd PT_LOAD's `p_vaddr` at the SAME value. The shared helper
`_wx_data_vaddr(S, entry, cp)` (called with `entry = _entry_base` in FIXUP and
`entry = base + text_foff` in EMITELF_USER — they're equal for userland) guarantees
agreement. Get this wrong and every string/gvar load in the program reads garbage.

**Why 2 MB.** The agnos loader maps 2 MB huge pages, so a text and data segment that
share a 2 MB page can't get distinct permissions. Round the data vaddr up to a 2 MB
boundary. The FILE offset only needs the ELF `p_align` (4 KB on x86, 64 KB on
aarch64): since the 2 MB vaddr is a multiple of `p_align`, `p_vaddr ≡ p_offset
(mod p_align)` holds with only a ≤`p_align` file pad — the ≤2 MB gap is *virtual*,
near-zero file bytes (the "p_offset sharing" trick).

**The .text shift.** A 2nd program header (56 bytes) pushes `.text` from file offset
120 (64-byte ELF header + 1 PH) to 176 (+ a 2nd PH). `_entry_base` and the ELF
`e_entry` MUST move to `base + 176` under W^X, or every absolute fn / var / string
fixup (which is `entry + offset`) lands 56 bytes off the real code. Make
`_entry_base` call `_wx_active` (forward-ref resolves via pass-1 fn registration).

**Two-step bootstrap to flip default-on.** The existing single-segment cycc CANNOT
emit W^X (its `_wx_active` doesn't exist / defaults off). So `old-cycc compiles
new-src` → cc1: a single-segment binary whose *code* defaults W^X-on (a transition
intermediate). `cc1 compiles src` → cc2: W^X (cc1 emits W^X). `cc2 compiles src` →
cc3: W^X. `cc2 == cc3` is the stable fixpoint — install that as build/cycc. The
seed chain works the same way: cybs (single-segment) emits gen1 (cycc code,
single-seg), gen1 emits gen2 == build/cycc (W^X). Once build/cycc is cc2,
version-bump's single self-host step reproduces W^X with no further two-step.

**Guard.** W^X is userland-ELF only: `_wx_active` requires `_emit_fmt(S) == 0` (ELF,
not Mach-O 1/2 or PE 3) AND `kmode == 0` (not kernel / shared `.so` / object). So
Mach-O, PE, kernel, `.so`, and `.o` outputs are untouched. x86 and aarch64 are
separate backends, so `_wx_active`/`_wx_data_vaddr` are duplicated in each (aarch64
`p_align` is 0x10000, x86 0x1000). Verified: readelf -lW shows text R E / data RW on
x86, aarch64, and CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS; pi (real aarch64) self-hosts the W^X cycc;
CYRIUS_WX=0 is differential byte-identical to the pre-split release.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific"
description = "The Mach-O syscall-number translation is ARCH-SPECIFIC in its SOURCE numbers, not just its target: x86-macho feeds syscalls_macos.cyr (BSD-numbered — getpid 39, getppid 110) but aarch64-macho feeds syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr (getpid 172, getppid 173) via lib/syscalls.cyr:64-70. So a getpid/getppid/syscall fix that reasons ONLY about the x86 EMACHO_SYSXLAT table (39/110) misses the aarch64-macho stdlib ENTIRELY — the aarch64 numbers (172/173) also need their own ESYSXLAT entries. getpid returned positive GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS (an untranslated 172 ran a raw svc) until v6.3.44, and a callable-only smoke PASSED on it (a garbage-positive value is still 'callable, stable'). Found only by tightening vr01_process_smoke.tcyr to assert a POSITIVE PID and running it on REAL ecb (aarch64-macOS) hardware."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["macho", "syscall", "ESYSXLAT", "aarch64", "getpid", "getppid", "cross-os", "found-by-ports", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**Two macho stdlibs, two number spaces.** `lib/syscalls.cyr` (lines
~64-70) routes the Mach-O syscall SOURCE numbers by ARCH, not just OS:
  - **x86-macho** includes `syscalls_macos.cyr` — the BSD numbers
    (getpid **39**, getppid **110**).
  - **aarch64-macho** includes `syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` — the Linux
    aarch64 numbers (getpid **172**, getppid **173**).
The BACKEND translation table (`EMACHO_SYSXLAT` on x86, `ESYSXLAT` on
aarch64) then maps those source numbers to the real Darwin syscall
(getpid → 20, getppid → 39). The trap: these are TWO SEPARATE tables in
TWO SEPARATE backends. A fix that edits only the x86 table (39→20, 110→…)
leaves aarch64-macho (172/173) untranslated — the stdlib picks a totally
different source number there.

**How it shipped garbage.** Pre-v6.3.44 the aarch64-macho ESYSXLAT had no
entry for 172, so `getpid()` on aarch64-macOS emitted a raw `svc` with
x8=172 (a Darwin number that isn't getpid) and returned a positive but
GARBAGE value. getppid (173) returned 0. The x86 getpid (39→20) had
worked since v6.0.02, which is exactly why reasoning "getpid works on
macOS" was wrong — it worked on x86-macho only. Fix: aarch64 ESYSXLAT
`172→20` (getpid) + `173→39` (getppid); x86 `110→39` getppid; and the
`_macho_arm_routes` whitelist gained 172/173.

**Why the old test missed it.** The smoke only checked that getpid was
CALLABLE and returned a stable value — a garbage-positive integer passes
that. Tightening the test to assert a POSITIVE, plausible PID *and running
it on real ecb hardware* is what surfaced it. A green CI check and a
disasm of the x86 table would never have caught it. (Same lesson class as
the v6.3.43 Windows file-I/O std-handle bug.) See
`cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "emit_gvar_inits_parallel_copy_of_parse_struct_init"
description = "EMIT_GVAR_INITS (the pass-2 replay of global var initializers, src/frontend/parse_decl.cyr) is a HAND-DUPLICATED PARALLEL COPY of PARSE_STRUCT_INIT's positional struct-literal loop. A struct field-shape fix that lands in one but not the other makes the global-scope init path silently DIVERGE from the in-fn path. The v6.3.44 bug: EMIT_GVAR_INITS never got the v5.10.7 IS_STR_FIELD single-8-byte-slot handling, so a global `var g = T { .., <str> };` with a Str field flattened the Str into a nested-struct 2-field embedding, PCMPE'd a nonexistent `len` expression, and hit `}` (`unexpected '}'`) — while the same literal inside a fn (which goes through PARSE_STRUCT_INIT, already fixed) compiled fine. There is a documented refactor backlog of ~5 such 'parallel copy' duplications in the struct/gvar-init path; until they're merged, ANY field-shape or width-correctness fix must be applied to BOTH loops."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["EMIT_GVAR_INITS", "PARSE_STRUCT_INIT", "parse_decl", "struct-init", "global-scope", "parallel-copy", "IS_STR_FIELD", "refactor-backlog", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**Two loops, one shape.** cyrius emits a struct literal's positional
field stores in TWO places:
  - `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT` — the in-fn path (`var g = T { … };` inside a fn).
  - `EMIT_GVAR_INITS` — the pass-2 replay for GLOBAL-scope initializers
    (deferred to after all decls register).
They are hand-duplicated copies of the same positional loop, NOT a shared
helper. Every per-field-shape refinement has had to be applied to both,
and historically hasn't been: the v5.9.36 narrow-width initializer fix and
the v5.10.7 single-slot Str handling both had to be mirrored. When only
one loop gets a fix, the global-scope path silently DIVERGES.

**The v6.3.44 divergence.** A Str field (field-type id > 0) in
`EMIT_GVAR_INITS` fell into the nested-struct FLATTEN branch — which treats
a struct-typed field as a 2-field by-value embedding and `PCMPE`s a second
expression for the embedded struct's `len` field. A Str is a single 8-byte
handle, not a 2-field embed, so the loop consumed a nonexistent expression
and ran into the closing `}` → `unexpected '}'`. Diagnosis was masked by
its being FILED as a `#derive(Serialize)` bug — but it is
`#derive`-INDEPENDENT (a plain global struct literal with a Str field
reproduces it). Fix: mirror the `IS_STR_FIELD` single-8-byte-slot branch
into `EMIT_GVAR_INITS`, exactly as `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT` already had it.

**Recognize it again.** A struct-literal shape that works inside `fn main`
but errors at global scope (or vice-versa) is the fingerprint of a
one-sided fix to this parallel-copy pair. There is a refactor backlog of
~5 such duplications in the struct/gvar-init path — until they collapse
into one shared emitter, patch BOTH loops for any field-shape change.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_mutex_single_owner_sync_cyr"
description = "As of v6.3.44 the stdlib mutex has a SINGLE OWNER: lib/sync.cyr. mutex_new/lock/unlock + MUTEX_SIZE used to be defined in BOTH lib/thread.cyr AND lib/sync.cyr (and in each platform sibling: thread_win ⇄ sync_windows SRWLOCK, thread_agnos ⇄ sync agnos) — so any consumer linking both thread and sync (sync arrives transitively via alloc/atomic/patra) saw 3 `duplicate fn 'mutex_*'` warnings and a silent link-order winner if the impls ever drifted. Now sync.cyr owns them, thread.cyr includes sync.cyr once at the top, all thread*.cyr drop their inline defs, and cyrius's include-once dedup collapses the both-included case to one definition. sync.cyr's own #ifdef routing picks the backend (Linux futex / Windows SRWLOCK / macOS spinlock / agnos no-op). SECOND gotcha: the api-surface snapshot is MODULE-QUALIFIED (thread::mutex_lock vs sync::mutex_lock), so RELOCATING a fn from one module to another shows up as REMOVALS in the snapshot diff — regenerate it (build/cyrius_api_surface --update) and verify the removals are relocations, not real deletions."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["mutex", "sync.cyr", "thread.cyr", "single-owner", "include-once", "duplicate-fn", "api-surface", "module-qualified", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**Single owner.** `mutex_new` / `mutex_lock` / `mutex_unlock` + `MUTEX_SIZE`
now live in exactly one file: `lib/sync.cyr`. Before v6.3.44 they were
ALSO defined in `lib/thread.cyr` (Linux futex) and in each platform sibling
(`thread_win.cyr` ⇄ `sync_windows.cyr` SRWLOCK; `thread_agnos.cyr` ⇄ the
sync agnos branch). Because `sync` arrives transitively (alloc → atomic →
patra all pull it in), any consumer linking `thread` too got THREE
`duplicate fn 'mutex_*'` warnings on every build — with a silent
link-order winner if the two impls ever drifted. Fix: `sync.cyr` is the
single owner; `thread.cyr` includes it once at the top and all three
`thread*.cyr` drop their inline defs. `sync`'s own `#ifdef` routing selects
the backend (Linux futex / Windows SRWLOCK / macOS spinlock / agnos no-op),
and include-once dedup collapses the both-included case. Behavior-preserving
on Linux/Windows/agnos (the bodies were byte-identical) and a NET FIX on
macOS (a working spinlock instead of the never-routed futex).

**The api-surface snapshot is MODULE-QUALIFIED.** Symbols are recorded as
`module::fn` (e.g. `thread::mutex_lock`, `sync::mutex_lock`), so MOVING a
fn between modules registers as REMOVALS (`thread::mutex_*` gone) even
though the consumer-visible unqualified `mutex_*` is unchanged (it stays
via `sync`/`sync_macos`/`sync_windows`). Regenerate the snapshot
(`build/cyrius_api_surface --update`) and confirm the removals are
RELOCATIONS, not deletions — the v6.3.44 fold was 9 relocations / 0 real
additions. (Same regenerate-and-verify discipline as any folded lib that
adds/moves public fns — the check.sh api-surface gate goes RED on a stale
snapshot's exact-count line.)
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable"
description = "Cross-OS self-host on REAL hardware — ecb (macOS arm64), cass (Windows PE), pi (aarch64 Linux), one ssh away each — is a STANDING release gate, not a closeout-only or CI-only check. A GREEN CI CHECK IS NOT VERIFICATION. Two v6.3.x bugs shipped SILENTLY behind green smoke and were caught ONLY by running on the hardware: (1) v6.3.43 — Windows sys_write/sys_read on FILE handles (EWRITE_PE always translated the fd → GetStdHandle, so only std handles 0/1/2 ever worked; a CreateFileW handle wrote to garbage) — never caught because no test had ever written to a FILE on Windows. (2) v6.3.44 — getpid returned positive GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS (untranslated syscall 172) — a callable-only smoke passed on it. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only smoke is a PLACEBO; the actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware IS the test. The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently ~9 minors behind a CI job that only ran hello-world."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["cross-os", "self-host", "ecb", "cass", "pi", "release-gate", "found-by-ports", "smoke-is-placebo", "v6.3.43", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**A checkmark is not the compiler running on the hardware.** After ANY
compiler-backend or stdlib change, cycc must self-host BYTE-IDENTICAL and
the platform tests must PASS with real assertions on real macOS (ecb,
arm64), real Windows (cass, PE), and real aarch64 Linux (pi) — each one
`ssh` away via `~/.ssh/config`. This is a STANDING per-release gate (the
LIBTEST `vr01_` glob + `SELFHOST_OK`/`LIBTEST_OK` on ecb/cass/pi), not a
closeout-only or CI-only step.

**Two silent-rot cases from v6.3.x, both found only by running:**
  - **v6.3.43, Windows file I/O.** `EWRITE_PE` (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr)
    ALWAYS translated the fd into a *standard handle* via GetStdHandle
    (`neg;sub 10`), so `sys_write`/`sys_read` only ever worked for fd
    0/1/2 — a real `CreateFileW` handle (a large value) mistranslated into
    a bogus std-handle index → WriteFile to an invalid handle → the whole
    file round-trip silently failed. Never caught because NO test had ever
    written to a FILE on Windows (tests/win/ was all stdout). Fix: an
    unsigned `cmp rax,2; ja` fd-branch (fd>2 = handle direct), mirroring
    the fix EREAD_PE already had since v6.0.45.
  - **v6.3.44, aarch64-macOS getpid.** getpid returned positive GARBAGE
    (an untranslated syscall 172) — see
    `macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific`. A callable-only
    smoke passed on the garbage.

**The precedent.** The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently for ~9
minors (v5.3.13 → v6.0.32, 400+ patches) behind a CI job named "Mach-O
ARM64 Native ✓" that only ran hello-world / exit-code programs and never
once built or self-hosted cycc. It surfaced only when a human installed on
a Mac and got a broken toolchain. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only
smoke is a PLACEBO — the compiler self-hosting on the target, and the
actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware, IS the test.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "vex_encoding_vvvv_ones_complement_and_fma_cpuid_leaf"
description = "The v6.4.8/.9 SIMD arc emitted the FIRST VEX/AVX instructions the toolchain has ever produced (f32v8 256-bit: 2-byte C5 VEX for vaddps/vsubps/vmulps at v6.4.8; 3-byte C4 VEX for vfmadd231ps + vextractf128 at v6.4.9). Two traps bit here that hand-rolled hex-literal encoders on the SSE side never had. (1) THE VEX vvvv FIELD IS THE 1's-COMPLEMENT OF THE src1 REGISTER — the non-destructive 3-operand form encodes src1 in the inverted vvvv nibble, so a wrong-polarity vvvv assembles to a VALID instruction that silently reads the WRONG register (no crash, no decode error — just wrong math). It is INVISIBLE to a size/self-host check and can even pass a coarse value test if the mis-read register happens to alias. The only defense is to round-trip EVERY new VEX op through a real disassembler (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump) and diff the mnemonic+operands against intent — a disasm gate, not an eyeball. (2) FMA NEEDS A DIFFERENT CPUID LEAF/BIT THAN AVX2 — the runtime feature probe for vfmadd231ps is CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12 (simd_has_fma), NOT the AVX2 probe of leaf 7, EBX bit 5 (simd_has_avx2). They are genuinely separate capabilities (a CPU can have AVX2 without FMA in principle), so a wrapper that gates FMA on the AVX2 bit is wrong; each 256-bit op checks the RIGHT bit and falls back (2×SSE, or scalar for FMA). AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline — the CPUID probe + fallback is mandatory, not optional. Corollary from the same arc: decode.cyr's VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided with a pre-existing SYSCALL/CPUID mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (filed P3) — the disassembler gate uses external llvm tooling, not the in-tree decoder."
category = "cyrius"
---
The v6.4.4–.9 SIMD compute arc took x86 codegen from SSE-only into
**AVX/VEX** territory for the first time: v6.4.8 emits 2-byte (C5) VEX
for the f32v8 elementwise ops (vaddps/vsubps/vmulps on ymm), v6.4.9 adds
3-byte (C4) VEX for `vfmadd231ps` + the 8-lane `vextractf128` reduce.
Two traps here have NO analogue in the older hand-rolled SSE hex-literal
emitters (EMIT_F32V_LOOP etc. in src/backend/x86/float.cyr).

**TRAP 1 — the VEX `vvvv` field is the 1's-COMPLEMENT of src1.** The
non-destructive 3-operand VEX form encodes the first source register in
the `vvvv` nibble **inverted** (`~reg & 0xF`). Get the polarity wrong
and you STILL assemble a valid instruction — it just reads a DIFFERENT
register. No SIGILL, no decode failure, no assembler complaint: silently
wrong math. It is invisible to a size/self-host check (the bytes are a
legal encoding) and can even sneak past a coarse value test if the
mis-read register happens to hold a compatible value. **The only defense
is a disassembler gate:** round-trip every new VEX op through real
external tooling (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump), diff the decoded
mnemonic+operands against intent. Eyeballing the hex is not enough — the
whole point of the trap is that the wrong bytes look plausible.

**TRAP 2 — FMA and AVX2 are DIFFERENT CPUID capabilities.** The runtime
probe for `vfmadd231ps` is **CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12**
(`simd_has_fma`), which is a DIFFERENT leaf AND bit from the AVX2 probe
**CPUID leaf 7 (ECX=0), EBX bit 5** (`simd_has_avx2`). They are not
interchangeable — a part can advertise AVX2 without FMA — so gating the
FMA path on the AVX2 bit is a real bug. AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline;
each 256-bit wrapper MUST probe the correct bit and branch to a fallback
(2×SSE for elementwise, scalar for FMA) when absent. This is mandatory,
not a nicety — a plain `-march=x86-64` box will `#UD` on an unguarded
`vaddps ymm`.

**Corollary — the in-tree decoder was NOT extended for VEX.** During the
arc, `decode.cyr`'s VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided
with a PRE-EXISTING mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (SYSCALL/CPUID),
filed P3 (issues/2026-07-05-decode-len-mislengths-no-modrm-0f-opcodes.md).
So the disasm gate deliberately uses EXTERNAL llvm tooling, not the
in-tree `decode.cyr` — don't reach for the in-tree decoder to validate
VEX bytes; it can't, and fixing it is a separate tracked item.

**Recognize again.** Any first-of-a-new-encoding-family codegen
(VEX→EVEX, a new prefix, a new 3-operand form) where the encoding packs
an operand in a non-obvious way (inverted, split across bytes): assume a
plausible-but-wrong encoding will assemble cleanly and pass self-host,
and add a disasm round-trip gate BEFORE trusting value tests. And any new
ISA-extension op: find its EXACT CPUID leaf+bit (they are not grouped by
"AVX-ness") and wire a runtime fallback.

[[entries]]
name = "two_step_bootstrap_for_toplevel_array_sizing_and_legit_codegen_diff"
description = "Two paired differential-testing / bootstrap gotchas from the v6.4.x SIMD-and-fixes minor. (1) A LAYOUT change to how cycc sizes its OWN data needs a TWO-STEP BOOTSTRAP, same as a heap-map or W^X change. v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level `var X[N]` was silently 8× UNDER-sized (N bytes, the fn-local default) instead of N×8 (the PARSE_GVAR_ARR slot size) when the array was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement — because pass-2 PARSE_ARRAY fell through to the fn-local N-byte path. Fixed in parse_decl.cyr (~61): a bare NON-fn-local array gets N×8. Because cycc's OWN top-level arrays were mis-sized under the old compiler, flipping the sizing needs the two-step (old cycc → cc5b, then cc5b compiles itself; cycc==cc5b) — a plain one-step fixpoint can bake in the old layout. This joins W^X-default-flip and array-convention as the recurring 'cycc's own storage layout changed → two-step' class. (2) A BUG FIX legitimately shows differential codegen-diff>0 WITH status-diff=0 — the opposite of a pure refactor (which must be codegen-diff=0). When a fix CHANGES emitted bytes for the affected inputs (e.g. correct array sizing, a sign-extension fix, a correct register), the differential corpus SHOULD show codegen-diff>0 on exactly the affected programs while status-diff stays 0 (nothing that passed now fails); a codegen-diff=0 on a supposed behavior fix means the fix didn't actually fire. Read the two axes separately: status-diff guards regressions, codegen-diff measures blast radius."
category = "cyrius"
---
Two paired gotchas about READING differential/bootstrap results, from the
v6.4.x minor's fixes (not the SIMD codegen itself).

**(1) A change to how cycc sizes its OWN top-level data needs a TWO-STEP
BOOTSTRAP.** v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level
`var X[N]` was silently **8× under-sized** — it got N BYTES (the
fn-local array default) instead of N×8 (the `PARSE_GVAR_ARR` slot size)
**whenever it was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement**,
because at that point pass-2 `PARSE_ARRAY` fell through to the fn-local
N-byte path instead of the global slot path. Fix (parse_decl.cyr ~61): a
bare **non-fn-local** array is sized N×8. The catch: **cycc's own
top-level arrays were affected**, so the sizing flip cannot be validated
by a one-step fixpoint alone — the old (mis-sizing) cycc could bake the
old layout into the new binary. Run the **two-step**: old cycc → `cc5b`,
then `cc5b` compiles itself, assert `cycc == cc5b` (+ seed-derive). This
is the SAME class as the W^X default-on flip and the local-vs-global
array-convention gotcha: **whenever a change alters cycc's own storage
layout / data sizing, it's a two-step, not a one-step.**

**(2) A real behavior fix SHOULD show differential codegen-diff > 0 with
status-diff = 0** — and that is CORRECT, not a red flag. The differential
harness reports two independent axes over the corpus:
  - **status-diff** — did any program's pass/fail status change? For a
    fix this MUST stay 0 (nothing that compiled+ran now breaks).
  - **codegen-diff** — did the emitted BYTES change, and for which
    programs? For a fix that changes what's emitted (array sizing, a
    sign-extension, a corrected register) this SHOULD be > 0, on exactly
    the affected programs.

The trap is conflating the two. A pure REFACTOR must be
codegen-diff = 0 (identical bytes). A BUG FIX is the opposite: if a
supposed behavior fix shows codegen-diff = 0, the fix **did not fire** —
you changed a path nothing exercises, or guarded it wrong. So read the
axes separately: status-diff is the regression guard; codegen-diff is the
blast-radius measurement. `codegen-diff > 0 && status-diff = 0` on the
programs you expected is exactly the signature of a landed, contained fix.

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_transcendental_polyfill_dispatch_and_dekker_dd_range_reduction"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
aarch64 has no native transcendentals — x86 gets them from x87 (fsin/fcos,
f2xm1/fscale for exp/exp2, fyl2x for ln/log2, fpatan for atan). Every f64
transcendental on aarch64 is therefore a lib/math.cyr POLYFILL that the aarch64
backend dispatches to. The dispatch pattern (v5.7.31 exp/ln → v6.4.16 sin/cos →
v6.4.25 exp2/atan) is identical: EF64_<OP>(S) in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr does
`var pf = _FINDFN_CSTR(S, "_f64_<op>_polyfill", <len>); if (pf<0) ERR_MSG(...requires
include lib/math.cyr...); EPUSHR(S); ECALLPOPS(S,1); ECALLFIX(S,pf);` — i.e. it emits
a 1-arg call into the stdlib fn resolved by name. The x86 EF64_<OP> in
src/backend/x86/emit.cyr keeps emitting native x87 bytes and is NOT touched. THREE
consequences that trip you up:

(1) x86 cycc stays BYTE-IDENTICAL across a pure-aarch64 transcendental change. The
x86 fork (src/main.cyr) includes ONLY backend/x86/emit.cyr — not the aarch64 emit —
and cycc does NOT include lib/math.cyr (it's a stdlib, not compiler source). So
adding a polyfill + rerouting the aarch64 EF64_* changes ONLY the three aarch64 forks
(main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}.cyr) and lib/math.cyr. The x86 self-host fixpoint and
the differential (343 inputs, codegen-diff=0) are trivially green — that is CORRECT,
not a missing test. The real verification is the aarch64 self-host fixpoint (build the
x86-hosted aarch64-emitting cross-compiler `main_aarch64.cyr | cycc`, then `| itself`,
cmp — byte-identical under qemu) PLUS running the actual polyfill on real hardware
(pi + ecb) via a vr01_ tcyr. A green x86 run proves nothing about the aarch64 path.

(2) The polyfill must NOT recurse into the builtin it backs. _f64_exp2_polyfill uses
round/sub/mul/add/to (NOT f64_exp2); _f64_atan_polyfill uses gt/div/sub/add/mul/neg +
bit ops (NOT f64_atan). Each polyfill compiles on x86 too (arch-independent body) but
is only INVOKED on aarch64 — x86 keeps the x87 path.

(3) Large-argument trig range reduction on a machine with only f64 add/sub/mul (no
scalar FMA builtin) requires a Dekker double-double. The single-constant reduction
`r = x - round(x*2/pi)*pi_2` catastrophically cancels for large |x| (rel err ~5e-2 at
1e15). The fix is a NO-FMA Dekker TwoProduct: split via (2^27+1), form x*(2/pi) and
frac*(pi/2) as hi+lo words with the constants pre-split into _SH/_SL halves so the
product is exact without fma. Validated numerically (Python float64 == IEEE754) BEFORE
touching the compiler — double-double is exact to ~1e-16 through |x|≈1e15; triple-double
is NOT needed. GATE it (|x| >= 8192) so the cheap small-angle path is untouched and
stays BYTE-IDENTICAL — the issue's "small path unchanged" is a byte-identity
requirement, not a vibe. The dd quadrant k must come from the dd sum (round(ph+plo)),
NOT a naive round(x*2/pi) which is off-by-one for large x — so the dd block writes k_f
locally, and (no multi-return in this compiler) the ~14-line block is inlined verbatim
in both sin and cos, same as their small-path reduction already is. Keep each emitted
Horner/dd line under 120 chars up front (cyrlint gate) by accumulating into a stepwise
var, not one deep f64_add(f64_add(...)) nest — cyrfmt won't split it for you.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "new_pe_reroute_fn_needs_return0_stubs_in_aarch64_and_cx_backends_for_symbol_resolution"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
Adding a new kernel32/PE reroute emitter (an `E<NAME>_PE(S)` fn — TerminateProcess,
GetCurrentProcessId, ProcessPrng, etc.) is THREE edits, not one, and the third is
easy to forget because local testing never exercises it:

(1) the REAL impl in src/backend/x86/emit.cyr (emits the call bytes; often just
    `_pe_ensure_<x>(S); return _pe_call_Narg_aligned(S, _pe_<x>_get());`),
(2) the kernel32 IAT import registration in src/backend/pe/emit.cyr
    (`var _pe_<x>_ready/_idx; fn _pe_ensure_<x>(S){ _pe_register_kernel32(S,&_idx,"<Name>"); }`),
(3) the parse_expr.cyr dispatch (`if (sc_num == 0xF0NN) { if (argc==K) { E<NAME>_PE(S); ... } }`)
    AND the routed-range warning string.

The TRAP: parse_expr.cyr is compiled into EVERY fork (main.cyr, main_win.cyr,
main_x86_macho.cyr, main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}.cyr, main_cx.cyr). The dispatch's
CALL to E<NAME>_PE is emitted on every fork (the `if (_TARGET_PE==1)` is a runtime
constant, so the call is still COMPILED, just dead). cycc requires the symbol to
RESOLVE at compile time. But the real impl lives ONLY in backend/x86/emit.cyr,
which is included by exactly THREE forks (x86-Linux, PE, x86-macho). The aarch64
forks include backend/aarch64/emit.cyr and the cx fork includes backend/cx/emit.cyr
— NEITHER pulls in the x86 backend. So those forks need a `return-0` STUB:
`fn E<NAME>_PE(S){ return 0; }` in backend/aarch64/emit.cyr AND
`fn E<NAME>_PE(S): i64 { return 0; }` in backend/cx/emit.cyr (note the aarch64
stubs omit the `: i64`, the cx stubs keep it — match each file's local convention).
There is already a big block of these stubs (EWAIT_PE, EGETEXIT_PE, ESETHANDLE_PE,
EGETCURPID_PE, EPROCPRNG_PE, …) — add the new one next to them.

WHY IT BITES SILENTLY: local Linux self-host + `cat prog | CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1 cc_win`
+ wine all use forks that include backend/x86/emit.cyr, so they compile fine and pass.
The stub gap is invisible until a fork WITHOUT the x86 backend is built — which the
cross-OS gate does when it builds cycc_cx (the cx compiler) on cass. v6.4.26 shipped
ETERMINATE_PE with the x86 impl + pe import + parse dispatch, all green locally +
wine (terminate test 3/3, self-host byte-identical), then cass SELFHOST_FAILED with
`error: refusing to emit binary with 1 reachable undefined function(s): ETERMINATE_PE`
— the cycc_cx build. Adding the two stubs fixed it. LESSON: after adding an E*_PE
reroute, immediately build the aarch64 AND cx cross-compilers locally
(`cat src/main_aarch64.cyr | build/cycc >/dev/null` and `cat src/main_cx.cyr |
build/cycc >/dev/null`) — an undefined-function error there is the missing stub, and
it's far cheaper to catch than a cass round-trip. This is the same class as the
`_macho_arm_routes` stub-for-symbol-resolution note already in backend/x86/emit.cyr.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_ao_access_mode_bits_are_1to1_with_linux_o_flags_pass_through_dont_fold_verify_under_mirshi"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
The agnos file ABI reuses Linux's low-2-bit access-mode encoding EXACTLY:
AO_RDONLY=0x0, AO_WRONLY=0x1, AO_RDWR=0x2 — identical to O_RDONLY/WRONLY/RDWR
(lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr). So in `file_open`'s `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`
flag-translation, the access mode PASSES THROUGH: `ao = ao | (flags & 3);`. Do NOT
write `if ((flags & 3) != 0) { ao = ao | 0x1; }` — that folds BOTH WRONLY(1) and
RDWR(2) to WRONLY(1), so an O_RDWR open becomes write-only on agnos and every later
read on that fd returns -1 (v6.4.27 P1: `io.cyr:80`; patra "cannot read header"
reopening its B-tree O_RDWR under sit/mirshi). The higher bits are NOT 1:1 and DO
need per-bit mapping (O_CREAT 64→AO_CREAT 0x100, O_TRUNC 512→0x200, O_APPEND
1024→0x400) — only the access-mode nibble is a straight pass-through.

TWO meta-lessons: (1) an agnos flag/ABI-translation bug is INVISIBLE to Linux runs
and to a bare `--agnos` COMPILE — both pass. The only thing that catches it is
running the agnos binary under **mirshi** (~/Repos/mirshi/build/mirshi <bin>, the
agnos→Linux ptrace supervisor) and asserting real I/O behavior (here: create+write,
close, reopen O_RDWR, read → exit 42; the buggy fold exits 4 on the read). Build a
minimal exit-code program (NOT the assert framework — it uses raw syscall(1,…) writes
that aren't agnos-portable) and check $? under mirshi. (2) When the same helper is
COPIED into a vendored stdlib (here lib/sakshi.cyr:88 had the identical fold), fix the
cyrius-native site in a cyrius release but fix the fold at its SOURCE repo
(~/Repos/sakshi) + patch-bump + re-vendor — never patch lib/sakshi.cyr directly (it's
materialized; the edit evaporates on the next `cyrius deps`).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pe_kernel32_reroutes_fixed_sub_rsp_only_aligns_on_right_parity_closehandle_faults_on_misalign_wine_cannot_repro"
description = "Windows PE kernel32 reroutes must DYNAMICALLY 16-align rsp before the call — a fixed `sub rsp, 0x28` is a latent bug. Win64 requires rsp = 0 (mod 16) immediately before a `call`; a fixed sub-rsp achieves that only when the caller's rsp lands on the exact parity the offset assumes, which depends on the function's frame size + expr-stack depth + call chain — so it silently works or faults by context. CloseHandle (and any kernel32 fn with an internal aligned SSE access, e.g. movaps) faults 0xC0000005 on an 8-misaligned stack; GetExitCodeProcess/WaitForSingleObject happen to tolerate it, which is why the crash localizes to CloseHandle though its siblings share the bug. EXPOSED at v6.4.44 by the async subprocess resume (lib/async_win.cyr _async_process_task): main -> task_join -> _async_pump -> _async_step -> fncall1 -> resume -> CloseHandle is a deep stack, and TWO sequential async_run_process calls crashed on real cass while a SINGLE call and the BATCHED (spawn-all-then-join) form worked — pure alignment-parity luck. CreateProcessW's ECREATEPROC_PE was already rbx-anchor-aligned at v6.0.51, but its own comment asserted 'the <=4-arg reroutes tolerate a misalign this 10-arg call does not' — that generalization was FALSE. FIX: route each of the 8 fixed-offset reroutes (ECLOSE_PE/EWAIT_PE/EGETEXIT_PE/ESETHANDLE_PE/ECREATEPIPE_PE/EGETCMDLINE_PE/EGETTICKS_PE/EMMAP_PE) through the existing rbx-anchored helpers (_pe_call_0/2/3/4arg_aligned, _pe_srw_call_1arg: push rbx; mov rbx,rsp; and rsp,-16; sub rsp,0x20; call; mov rsp,rbx; pop rbx). cycc SHRANK ~4 KB (inline reroutes -> shared helpers). HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a real-Windows-ONLY 0xC0000005 that WINE cannot reproduce (WINE aligns leniently -> returned 42 throughout) AND flips crash<->pass with call-depth / caller-frame changes -> suspect a fixed-`sub rsp` reroute, not the callee. Localize with a WriteFile (syscall(1,1,...)) trace bracketing each op; bisect step-0 vs resume vs the post-fncall1 loop code with manual GQCS + fncall1 replays. Self-host + seed-derive stay byte-identical (PE-emit-only; cycc's Linux codegen never calls these). See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_offbyone_prologue_postloop_missing_pp_shift_f32v8_make_lanes_567"
description = "On Win64 PE a by-value SIMD/struct return uses a HIDDEN RETPTR in RCX, so every user arg shifts down one ABI slot (a,b,c -> rdx/r8/r9; d,e,f,g,h -> stack). The callee prologue homes params in TWO places and they must agree on that retptr shift: the IN-loop homing (parse_fn.cyr ~3321) computes `_pp_shift = (_cur_fn_ret_stash>0 && !aarch64) ? 1 : 0` and calls ESTOREPARM(int_pc+_pp_shift, li) -- pidx shifted for the source Win64 slot, lidx contiguous for the dest local slot. But the POST-loop that homes the DEEP stack params (pidx>=6, parse_fn.cyr ~3404) did `ESTOREPARM(spi, spi, pc)` -- using spi as BOTH pidx AND lidx with NO shift. Correct only with no retptr. With one, it homed each deep stack param ONE LOCAL SLOT TOO DEEP (dest lidx = true+1) and stopped one param short (spi<pc never reached the last source idx), so the first excluded stack param was never homed, the rest got the previous param's value, and the last param's incoming bytes were ignored. The body reads each param at the contiguous -0x30-lidx*8 slot via FINDLOCAL, so it diverged. SYMPTOM: f32v8_make(a..h) returned lanes 0-4 correct but lanes 5,6,7 = garbage/shifted on real cass; Linux/SysV all 8 lanes correct (SIMD returns via the xmm PAIR there, no retptr, so _pl_shift==0 and the emit is textually the old code -> self-host byte-identical). FIX: mirror _pp_shift in the post-loop -- `pidx = spi + _pl_shift` (shifted source), `lidx = spi` (contiguous dest), start `spi = 6 - _pl_shift`, guard `pc + _pl_shift > 6` (also catches the exact-6-param retptr case pc>6 dropped). PRE-EXISTING since v6.4.31 (Win64 value-form SIMD params/returns); latent for many minors because vr01_f32v8_ctor.tcyr carried a stale comment saying it 'skips cass', but v6.4.31 made CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS true on PE so the native cass cycc actually RAN the f32v8 assertions -- surfaced only when the v6.4.44 permanent Windows async gate ran the full vr01_ corpus on cass. HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a value-form-SIMD/struct-returning fn with >6 params whose DEEP args (3rd+ stack arg) are wrong on Win64 but correct on Linux -> suspect the retptr-shift being applied inconsistently between the two prologue-homing sites. Confirm by disassembling BOTH the prologue homing (`mov rax,[rbp+src]; mov [rbp-dst],rax`) and the body read (FINDLOCAL disp = -((lidx+1)*8)-regalloc*8-ret_stash) -- they must land on the same local slot. The caller (ECALLPOPS / parse_decl retptr push) was CORRECT; the bug is callee-side. Same '_cur_fn_ret_stash disp<->idx off-by-one' class as project_v6431_win64_valueform_simd, here the one remaining site. See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "shift_right_split_gtgt_stays_logical_gtgtgt_is_arithmetic_reverse_of_js_never_flip_gtgt"
description = "v6.4.46 added `>>>` as the ARITHMETIC (sign-preserving) right shift and KEPT `>>` LOGICAL (zero-fill) — the REVERSE of JS/Java (there `>>` is arithmetic, `>>>` logical). The split is NOT arbitrary: `>>` MUST stay logical because two load-bearing sites depend on it. (1) CRYPTO ROTATES: keccak/sha/chacha rotate via `(x << n) | (x >> (64-n))` — logical `>>` is required or the high bits sign-extend and the rotate is wrong; flipping `>>` to arithmetic silently miscompiles SHA-3, cascading into TLS (tls_native_alpn went red). (2) THE COMPILER'S OWN UNION FLAG: `ISUNION()` is `fcount >> 63` — logical `>>` gives 1 for a tagged (high-bit-set) fcount, arithmetic `>>` gives -1 and the union sizeof/layout miscompiles (structs went red). BOTH were caught ONLY by check.sh's corpus — the self-host fixpoint AND seed-derive stayed GREEN because cycc's own source didn't exercise the exact miscompiled path until the corpus ran (a reminder that green self-host is necessary, not sufficient). LESSON: do NOT 'fix' `>>` to arithmetic to match JS/Java — it is a deliberate, load-bearing choice; route signed-shift needs through the NEW `>>>` (IR_ASHR=26; x86 `sar rax,cl` = 48 D3 F8; aarch64 `asrv` = 0x9AC12800; cx op 37). The drishti bug that motivated it was a signed value silently zero-filled by `>>`; the fix is `>>>`, never a `>>` semantics flip."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["shift", "logical-shift", "arithmetic-shift", "operator", "crypto", "rotate", "union", "isunion", "sign-extension", "green-selfhost-not-sufficient", "v6.4.46"]

[[entries]]
name = "codebuf_is_growable_soft_warning_grow_not_exercised_by_selfhost_force_it_with_a_low_cap_build"
description = "The compiler's code buffer (generated machine code) has been GROWABLE since v6.2.0 — do NOT panic at a 'code buffer at N%' warning or treat the initial cap as a hard wall (a consumer, thoth, hit this: '91% of the ~3.14 MB cap' was a SOFT warning against the small initial allocation; the build succeeds and relocates transparently). v6.4.49 tuned it: initial cap 3 MiB → 8 MiB, and the grow went from ×2 doubling to +8 MiB LINEAR steps (8→16→…→64, dividing evenly into the 64 MiB ceiling — no doubling overshoot). THREE traps for anyone touching it: (1) the INITIAL cap can't just be bumped in-heap — the old 3 MiB region at S+0x41A000 is boxed in by file_map at 0x71A000, so the 8 MiB initial is an off-heap alloc(8388608) (the fixed region is now vestigial; alloc is proven at that init point — _vsgn_base already allocs 8 MiB there, so it's cybs/seed-safe). (2) the cx bytecode backend keeps a SEPARATE fixed 512 KiB region at 0x54A000 (cx/emit.cyr checks cp >= 524288, never _codebuf_base/_codebuf_grow) — cx's _codebuf_base/_codebuf_cap are vestigial; leave them. (3) — the big one — the self-host fixpoint AND check.sh do NOT exercise the grow: cycc emits ~1.09 MB, far under the 8 MiB initial, so no grow ever fires during self-compile or the test corpus. A change to _codebuf_grow (util.cyr) will pass all normal gates while being broken. To actually test the grow: build a throwaway cycc with a tiny initial cap (sed _codebuf_cap = 65536 + _codebuf_base = alloc(65536) into main.cyr, build, RESTORE main.cyr), have it compile the full cycc source (forces a grow), and cmp its output to a normal build — byte-identical proves the grow emits correct code. The warning lives only in main.cyr + main_win.cyr (the x86/Win drivers with an end-of-compile summary); aarch64/macho drivers have no summary block, so no codebuf warning there (pre-existing)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codebuf", "grow", "growable", "off-heap", "alloc", "self-host-doesnt-cover", "force-grow-test", "cx-separate-region", "warning", "v6.4.49"]

[[entries]]
name = "cfo_const_fold_rewind_class_four_occurrences_grep_the_shape_not_the_operator_and_every_tier_that_reenters_the_fold"
description = "THE most-repeated codegen defect of the v6.x line — four occurrences, each fix scoped to the tier its repro landed in and each declared complete. MECHANISM: the constant folder keeps `_cfo` (fold armed), `_cfv` (folded value) and `_cfp` (the code pointer captured at the START of the foldable region). A parse path clears `_cfo = 0` BEFORE calling a lower tier that RE-ARMS it — `PARSE_FACTOR` sets `_cfp = GCP(S)` then `_cfv = val; _cfo = 1` for a bare NUM — so the flag comes back on with `_cfp` pointing PAST code that was already emitted. The next operator then takes the const-fold branch with a live-but-stale fold and `SCP(S, _cfp)` rewinds the code pointer over that already-emitted code, replacing it with a constant. The symptom is always a silently wrong constant: no error, no warning, valid binary. OCCURRENCES: (1) 2026-06-11, cyrius-doom, `A * B * 4` -> 800 (the EIMUL path). (2) v6.4.74 — 17 sites in the `PARSE_TERM` tier (`/ % << >> >>>`): `100 >>> 1 + 1` == 2 (want 51), `100 >> 0 + 1` == 1 (want 101). `>>` and `<<` mostly escaped because their own fold path handles a literal RHS, so it bit exactly where that fold could not apply (shift count 0, or a value outside the 0x10000 window); a parenthesised RHS also masked it, since the paren path never re-arms. (3) v6.4.80 — 16 sites one tier UP in `PEXPR` (`+ - & | ^`): `1 - 2 + 3` == **5**, `2 - 5 + 10` == 15, `3 + 1 - 5 + 2` == 7. The emitted code is the expression with the leading `a -` deleted. Trigger is the `cfr >= 0` test in the subtract fold — a NEGATIVE intermediate fails it and takes the runtime fallback, and the fallback was the buggy one, which is exactly why non-negative intermediates were always right (`5 - 3 + 1` == 3) and it hid. Measured blast radius: 40 of 400 (10 %) systematic 3-term constant expressions wrong. (4) v6.4.81 — one tier DOWN in `EMIT_OP_DISPATCH`, struct operator overloading: it parses its RHS with `PARSE_TERM` (re-arms) and ends with `SESTYPE(S, 0)`, so the following `+`/`-` rewound over the emitted operator CALL — the overload silently deleted and replaced by a constant. `p * 3 + 1` == 4 (want 1004). The tell was a 2-of-4 asymmetry in four structurally identical lines: `add` (parse_expr.cyr:2809) and `sub` (:2837) already cleared `_cfo`; `mul` (:2550) and `div` (:2610) never did. THE LESSON: **grep the SHAPE, not the operator — and grep EVERY TIER that calls back into the fold, not just the tier the repro landed in.** A single `grep -c \"_cfo = 0; E.*PARSE_TERM\"` at .74 would have found the .80 sites eight releases earlier; at .80 the sweep recorded 'zero occurrences of the shape remain in parse_expr.cyr' and that was true only of PEXPR — the dispatch helper one level down was untouched. THE OTHER LESSON: **251/251 tcyr were byte-identical across the .80 fix, and that is the finding, not reassurance** — the corpus contained zero expressions of the failing shape, so 10 % of constant arithmetic was wrong with every gate green (self-host fixpoint, seed-derive, check.sh, cross-OS on four hosts). A byte-identical corpus proves a change did not perturb what the corpus covers; it says nothing about what it does not. COVERAGE TRAPS recorded while building the regression (`tests/tcyr/const_chained_multiply_fold.tcyr`, now 39 assertions and the single home of this one mechanism): an operator-overload helper whose body ALIASES the folded answer (`OpV_mul` returning `a * b`) passes while broken — make helpers return values plain integer arithmetic cannot produce; and getting a mutation to fail took three rounds, because `1 - 2 & 3` fires the SUB fallback not the AND one, AND needs a left operand folding to 0 (`0 & 1 & 1`), OR/XOR need a ZERO right-hand operand (`1 | 0 ^ 1`), and ADD is reachable only via i64 overflow (`9223372036854775807 + 1 + 1`) since a literal `crv` is never negative. Before that work 8 of the 16 .80 sites had no coverage at all. When a mutation PASSES, fuzz good-vs-mutant to find a discriminator rather than concluding the site is dead. Found at .80 by an adversarial doc-sweep verifier RUNNING the compiler to re-check an unrelated vidya claim about precedence — not by reading code. See CHANGELOG [6.4.74], [6.4.80], [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["_cfo", "_cfp", "const-fold", "code-pointer-rewind", "SCP", "PARSE_TERM", "PEXPR", "PARSE_FACTOR", "EMIT_OP_DISPATCH", "operator-overload", "grep-the-shape-not-the-operator", "byte-identical-is-not-coverage", "mutation-proof", "aliasing-helper", "v6.4.74", "v6.4.80", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt_the_cycc_fixpoint_does_not_substitute_for_seed_derive"
description = "v6.4.74's constant folder first spelled its arithmetic shift `v >>> _CF_FACTOR(S)`. It compiled, self-hosted, and FIXPOINTED byte-identical on `build/cycc` — then `scripts/seed-derive-cycc.sh` failed at step [3/5] with a bare `syntax error` and nothing else. Cause: **cybs, the hand-assembly bootstrap compiler the 29 KB seed assembles, cannot lex `>>>`** — `bootstrap/cybs.cyr`'s `lexer_shr` knows only `>`, `>=`, `>>`, so it tokenised `>>` `>`. The `>>>` operator has existed since v6.4.46; cybs never learned it. Fixed by writing `_CF_ASR`, which sign-extends using only `>>`/`<<` and masks the count to 6 bits to match the backend's `sar`. This is the cleanest available demonstration of Release Gate item 2: **the cycc self-host fixpoint does NOT cover the seed chain.** `build/cycc` is a full v6.x compiler; cybs is far more limited and fails SILENTLY, or with an unattributed one-line error, on constructs `build/cycc` compiles fine — too many global/string-literal references in one function (the ceiling that forced `_var_grow` into its `_grow_g1..g7` tail-call chain, that made v6.4.77 split the 67-entry `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` out of `TOKNAME`, and that made v6.4.81 split `PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG` out of an already-heavy `PREPROCESS`), tail calls, and now newer operators. PRACTICAL RULES: run `seed-derive-cycc.sh` for ANY `src/` change on EVERY release, not only at closeouts (a doc/lib-only bump is the sole exception); when writing compiler source prefer the OLDEST spelling that works; when a new helper carries a large literal table, expect the ceiling and split the fn before the gate tells you to; and note that gen1 (cybs's output) being ~72 KB smaller than `build/cycc` is NORMAL — only `gen2 == build/cycc` matters, do not chase it. RECOGNITION: a bare `syntax error` from step [3/5] with no file, no line and no token is the signature — read it as cybs hitting a ceiling, not as a defect in your source. See CHANGELOG [6.4.74]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cybs", "seed-derive", "bootstrap-chain", "gtgtgt", "arithmetic-shift", "_CF_ASR", "release-gate-item-2", "silent-failure", "global-reference-ceiling", "fixpoint-is-not-enough", "v6.4.74"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_reserved_token_count_cannot_be_grepped_and_every_number_written_down_has_gone_stale"
description = "⛔ COUNT CORRECTED v6.6.1: it is **76**, not the 67 this entry asserted (and not the 51 a `util.cyr` comment asserted) — THREE live values for one set, in three files, which is the entry's own lesson turned on itself. DERIVE it: `sed -n '/^fn TOKNAME_BUILTIN/,/^}/p' src/common/util.cyr | grep -cE 'if \(typ == [0-9]+\)'` → 76, disjoint from the 26 statement keywords in IS_KEYWORD_TOK, so 102 reserved tokens total. v6.4.77 fixed `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` and `TOKNAME` (`src/common/util.cyr`) both stopping at token 111, which made those reserved builtin/intrinsic names report `expected identifier, got unknown` — a diagnostic pointing at a syntactically clean identifier and calling it unknown. It cost the consumer that filed it (hisab) a whole 312-assertion suite, findable only by bisection. The consumer named three intrinsics; the real surface was 67 — a filing enumerates what it hit, not the class. **THE DURABLE PART IS THE COUNTING TRAP: a first pass finds only 51.** Two independent reasons any regex sweep under-counts. (1) The lexer has TWO keyword paths — `LEXKW_EXT`, which `return`s a token, and the inline `ADDTOK` chain — and a sweep over one silently misses the other. (2) A name longer than 8 characters cannot fit one u64 immediate, so it is matched by a u64 compare on the first 8 bytes PLUS per-byte `load8` tails, and never appears as a single decodable literal: `f64_floor`, `f64_round`, `f64v_sqrt`, `f64v_fmadd`, `f64v_scale`, `f64v_axpy`, `f32v_fmadd` and the whole `f32v8_*` family — 16 names invisible to a hex-literal grep. THE ONLY SOUND METHOD is to round-trip through the compiler: for each candidate spelling, compile `var <name> = 1;` and check the diagnostic names THAT EXACT spelling. That validates every hex decode at once and cannot be fooled by either lexer path. SHAPE OF THE FIX worth copying: the names live in a new `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` and **`IS_KEYWORD_TOK` DERIVES from it** (`if (TOKNAME_BUILTIN(typ) != 0) { return 1; }`) rather than repeating the list, so the 'is it reserved?' set and the 'what is it called?' set cannot drift, and a future intrinsic that gets a name is reserved automatically. CONSEQUENCE FOR DOCS: reserved words are a CLASS, not a list to be hand-extended in prose — CLAUDE.md carried THREE names for the 67-name class until v6.4.80 corrected it, structurally the same partial-observation-as-language-rule error as the retired '<=6 args' rule. And when gating a diagnostic, carry a NEGATIVE control: a gate that only greps for the substring `reserved keyword` passes happily on `reserved keyword 'unknown'`. See CHANGELOG [6.4.77]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["reserved-words", "TOKNAME_BUILTIN", "IS_KEYWORD_TOK", "LEXKW_EXT", "ADDTOK", "u64-compare", "load8-tail", "regex-undercount", "round-trip-through-the-compiler", "negative-control", "derives-cannot-drift", "v6.4.77"]

[[entries]]
name = "lexer_tokens_79_and_111_are_double_assigned_so_a_token_type_cannot_recover_the_spelling"
description = "**Token 79 is BOTH `object` and `f64_sqrt`; token 111 is BOTH `stack` and `callptr`** (`src/frontend/lex.cyr`; 111's two producers are the `LEXKW_EXT` path and the inline `ADDTOK` chain). Both spellings compile CORRECTLY, because the grammar disambiguates by POSITION — `object;` is a top-level mode declaration and `f64_sqrt(x)` an expression intrinsic; `stack var b[N];` and `callptr(...)` cannot occur in the same place — so the collision is invisible at parse time. It is very much not invisible in diagnostics: anything mapping a token type back to a name has lost the information, and before v6.4.77 `var f64_sqrt = 1;` reported `reserved keyword 'object'` and `var callptr = 1;` reported `reserved keyword 'stack'` — a diagnostic that actively named the WRONG keyword and sent the reader looking for a variable they never wrote. Current handling: `TOKNAME` returns both spellings (`object'/'f64_sqrt`, `stack'/'callptr`) and the v6.4.60 caret excerpt shows which one the reader actually typed. DO NOT 'fix' this by picking one, and do not assume anywhere else in the compiler that a token type identifies a spelling. Genuinely resolving it requires token RENUMBERING, which touches codegen — if you attempt it, every `typ ==` comparison across the lexer, the parser, `TOKNAME`, `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` and `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` moves together, which is why it is documented at the site rather than repaired. See CHANGELOG [6.4.77]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "token-numbers", "double-assigned", "token-79", "token-111", "object", "f64_sqrt", "stack", "callptr", "TOKNAME", "diagnostics-lie", "token-renumbering", "v6.4.77"]

[[entries]]
name = "any_fn_indexed_side_table_must_reach_32768_six_were_left_at_8192_and_index_8192_aliased_the_neighbour"
description = "v6.2.0 made the 17-table `_fnt_*` family growable — the fn table starts at cap **8192** and doubles to a hard ceiling of **32768** — but SIX other tables keyed by the same fn index `fi` were left at fixed 8192-slot heap bands, packed back-to-back: `fn_deprecated_msg` (0x100000), `fn_regalloc` (0x14A000), `fn_ret_sid` (0x15A000), `fn_variadic` (0x16A000), `fn_flags` (0x17A000), `fn_var_bytes` (0x1C8000). Once `_fnt_grow` let the count past 8192, `PARSE_FN`'s per-fn metadata reset for fn `fi` wrote into slot `(fi - 8192)` of the **NEIGHBOURING table** — silent cross-table corruption, no diagnostic. Fixed as a P0 at v6.4.75. WHY IT SURVIVED TWO MIGRATIONS AND EVERY GATE: **cycc has ~1135 fns**, so the compiler compiling itself never comes within 7000 fns of the cliff — the self-host fixpoint, seed-derive and the entire 251-file corpus are structurally incapable of reaching it. Only a large consumer approaches it; stiva 3.0.6 sat at 7443/8192 (91 %), about 750 fns away. Worse, the capacity WARNING went silent at exactly the dangerous point: it divided by the LIVE `_fnt_cap`, which doubles as the table grows, so it screamed 'fn_table at 99 %' at 8191 fns and reported a comfortable 54 % at 9000 — the silence coincided precisely with the corruption. It now reports against the fixed 32768 ceiling, which does not move. RULES. (1) Any new table indexed by `fi` allocates for **32768**, never for the current cap. The sanctioned shape is lazy-alloc-at-max-cap — `if (_base == 0) { _base = alloc(32768 * 8); }` inside the SETTER, `return 0` in the getter while unallocated — already used by `_fnt_tparams`/`_fnt_defstart`/`_fnt_is_async` and by `_vsgn_base` on the var side. It needs NO per-fork driver edit, cannot desync the cybs-fragile grow chain, and `alloc()` hands back kernel-zeroed pages so an unwritten slot reads 0 exactly like the old BSS band. Cost is a predictable growth tax: the `if (_base == 0)` guard on six hot accessors measured +1.0 % self_compile at .75. (2) When a table becomes relocatable, GREP FOR OTHER WRITERS — `REGFN`'s forward-overload registration alone still wrote `S + 0x97A000 + …` for `_fnt_ovstr`/`_ovint`/`_ovcstr` while every reader used the grown pointer, so an overload registered after a grow silently vanished. (3) A capacity meter must key on the ceiling that does not move, or it goes quiet exactly when it matters. (4) Watch bare arrays sized in BYTES: the DCE reachability bitmap `var live[4096]` is 4096 BYTES = 32768 bits (which the `t3idx < 32768` root-seed bound already assumed), but the clear loop stopped at 1024 — bits 8192..32767 read uninitialised stack, so unreachable-fn counts varied run to run. Gate: `_fn_grow_gate` (`programs/checks/selfhost.cyr`) generates an 8300-fn program with `#must_use` on fn 58 and a reachable fn 8250, mutation-proven both directions. NOTE FOR DOC SWEEPS: most `8192` references in the tree are CORRECT — it is still the live INITIAL cap — so a blanket 8192->32768 replace introduces errors. See CHANGELOG [6.2.0], [6.4.75]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["fn-table", "fn-indexed-side-tables", "8192", "32768", "lazy-alloc-at-max-cap", "_fnt_grow", "REGFN", "overload-tables", "dce-live-bitmap", "capacity-warning-goes-silent", "self-host-cannot-reach-it", "_fn_grow_gate", "v6.2.0", "v6.4.75"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_heap_map_is_machine_read_prose_on_the_map_line_is_parsed_as_the_size_and_unit_suffixes_were_invisible"
description = "`src/main.cyr`'s heap map is NOT documentation — `tests/heapmap.sh` parses it with awk and it IS the overlap gate. Two parser holes made that gate blind for years, both fixed at v6.4.81, and both are things to know BEFORE editing a map line. (a) **Unit suffixes did not exist.** The size pattern was `/\\[([0-9]+)\\]/` — a bare integer only — so every entry written `[16 MB]`-style was skipped ENTIRELY and the region did not exist as far as the auditor was concerned. That hid **20.02 MB of live heap**: `ir_nodes [16 MB]`, `ir_cp [4 MB]`, the lex_pp file scratch `[16KB]` and three PP `#derive` scratch regions. The failure mode is worse than a missing row — the auditor then reported a **phantom ~21.4 MB free gap** above `ir_live_out` that is in fact fully occupied, so a future region placed 'in the free space' would land inside `ir_nodes` and the gate would report PASS. The pattern now accepts `KB/MB/K/M`. (b) **It took the LAST bracketed number on the line**, so prose after the size won. The `fn_param_struct_mask` line ends `(v6.3.36 issue [5]: bit N = …)` and was parsed as **FIVE BYTES** — off by 13,107x. The same trap immediately bit the v6.4.81 `include_fname` correction: the corrected line carried the old `0x190500 [256]` in an explanatory parenthetical and the region was re-parsed as 256 B. The parser now takes the FIRST bracketed size after the name. **DURABLE RULE: a map line carries offset, name, size and a short label — keep every explanatory parenthetical, historical address and issue reference on CONTINUATION lines that contain no `0x… [N]` pattern.** This is the third instance of the class: v5.5.40 fixed a whitespace pattern that had silently dropped every region past 0xFC000, because 7-char hex offsets left only ONE space before the name. A heap auditor that cannot see a region cannot guard it, and it reports green either way. State after the fix: **100 regions, 0 overlaps** — numerically the same as through v6.4.74 but by a different route (94 at .75, when six fn-indexed side tables went lazy-alloc and their fixed bands were freed; back to 100 at .81 when the parser learned to see unit-suffixed entries). Do not read the recurring '100' as the old number having been right all along. Mutation-prove any change here by planting a probe region inside an existing one and requiring exit 1. See CHANGELOG [5.5.40], [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap-map", "heapmap.sh", "awk-parser", "unit-suffix", "prose-on-the-map-line", "continuation-lines", "phantom-free-gap", "ir_nodes", "fn_param_struct_mask", "region-count-100", "mutation-proof", "v5.5.40", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_heap_map_entry_can_document_a_region_no_code_has_ever_written_and_the_auditor_will_validate_the_fiction"
description = "The preprocessor include / `#ref` filename scratch was recorded in the heap map as `0x190500  include_fname [256]`. **No code has ever written 0x190500.** Every real use is `0x190400`, and it was UNBOUNDED — three separate copy loops (`include`, post-macro-expansion `include`, `#ref`) with no length check. So `tests/heapmap.sh` spent three minors proving non-overlap for a region that exists only in a comment, one page above a live region it therefore never checked. The live one could be walked straight through the include-count cell at `S+0x197F00`: `include \"zzz/<31490 A's>.cyr\"` **SIGSEGV'd cycc (exit 139)**, while 31480 bytes still gave a clean error — an exact boundary, with everything below it corrupting preprocessor state silently. That is CVE-32 (High), fixed at v6.4.81 by bounding all three loops at 4096 B through a shared `PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG` helper — split into its own fn because `PREPROCESS` was already near cybs's per-fn literal/global ceiling and seed-derive is the gate that would have caught the overflow. THE GENERALISATION: **the heap map is a machine-read CLAIM ABOUT the code, and nothing cross-checks the two directions.** A gate over the map proves the MAP is self-consistent; it says nothing about whether the code obeys it. So when auditing the map, do not read down the list checking arithmetic — **grep the tree for each base address, confirm a writer exists at exactly that address, and confirm its writes are bounded.** An entry with no writer is either dead (delete it) or a decoy sitting one page away from a live unbounded region (fix the code first, then the map). Companion instances from the very same audit, all three passing the gate: the map claimed a 13.3 MB TS frontend reservation that does not exist at all; `src/main.cyr` and `src/frontend/ts/lex.cyr` both quoted a stale TS base of `0x1D0B000` that lands inside `fixup_tbl`; and the live TS base overlapped `tok_types` by 10 MB. Sibling CVEs found the same way and worth the same treatment — `READFILE`'s `CYRIUS_HOME/lib/` fallback composed into `var fbuf[512]` with NEITHER copy loop bounded (CVE-33), and `var _cyrius_lib[256]` is a BARE TOP-LEVEL array so it is 256x8 = 2048 B per the v6.4.10 contract, with a `HOME=` copy bounded only by `elen` and ~32 further bytes appended unconditionally afterwards (CVE-34). cycc compiles untrusted source by design; every one of these is on the wrong side of that boundary. See CHANGELOG [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap-map", "include_fname", "CVE-32", "CVE-33", "CVE-34", "unbounded-copy", "preprocessor", "PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG", "gate-validates-fiction", "grep-for-the-writer", "cybs-ceiling", "untrusted-source", "bare-toplevel-array-is-Nx8", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_can_pass_because_of_the_bug_it_guards_and_ascii_collisions_make_byte_pattern_gates_vacuous"
description = "Two instances from the same period of the same failure — a green gate that proves nothing — with different mechanisms. (1) **THE GATE DEPENDED ON THE DEFECT.** `programs/checks/platform_efi.cyr` proves kmode emits top-level asm before the deferred gvar-init block, by searching the binary for a `48 b9` movabs. Its marker was `var marker_var = 0xDEADBEE0 | 0xF;` — a deliberately COMPUTED initializer, chosen so it would land in the runtime-store path. That path existed only because computed module-scope initializers were BROKEN, which is precisely the headline bug v6.4.74 fixed. The moment the new constant folder baked the marker into the image the deferred store vanished, `_find_bytes` silently matched an unrelated `48 b9` in the kmode boilerplate, the binary shrank 408 B -> 368 B, two `48 b9` became one — **and the gate stayed green.** Marker changed to `seed_var | 0xF` (an IDENT, which `_CF_TRY` refuses to fold); and because a comment telling the next person 'keep this non-foldable' is not a gate, it now proves its own NON-VACUITY by compiling a control variant with the marker gvar removed and requiring the real variant to contain strictly more `48 b9`. (2) **ASCII COLLISION.** `agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh` checked emitted code with `grep 'mov eax,0x52'`-style patterns. **0x52-0x55 are ASCII `R`,`S`,`T`,`U`**, so an ordinary string-literal byte store (`mov eax,0x54; pop rcx; mov BYTE PTR [rcx],al`) satisfied it — the gate reported PASS with a MUTATED syscall number (`#84 -> 99` still passed). Replaced with a precise extraction of the `mov eax,0xNN` immediately preceding each `syscall`, asserted per band member; this also silently repaired the identical latent hole in the v6.4.63 `#82`/`#83` checks. **THE RULE: mutation-prove every gate, in the direction that matters — break the thing it guards and require RED, restore and require GREEN.** A gate written at the same time as its fix is exactly when the dependency in (1) gets built in, because the author's model of 'the state before the fix' is the state the gate was authored against. Two more shapes of the same class from the same period: a gate asserting only `exit != 0` cannot distinguish 'the flag works and detects drift' from 'the flag is rejected as unknown' — that is how v6.4.63's `--check-lib-sync` shipped unreachable and a consumer, not the gate, found it; and a diagnostic gate greping for a substring passes on `reserved keyword 'unknown'` unless it also carries a negative control. See CHANGELOG [6.4.63], [6.4.70], [6.4.74], [6.4.77]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gate-vacuity", "mutation-proof", "placebo-gate", "ascii-collision", "kmode-marker", "_CF_TRY", "non-vacuity-control", "negative-control", "exit-code-only-assert", "agnos-gpu-band", "v6.4.63", "v6.4.70", "v6.4.74"]

[[entries]]
name = "win64_ecallpops_five_register_shuttle_broke_at_ten_args_and_the_bug_was_written_down_as_a_language_rule"
description = "**The Win64 caller-side stack-arg path corrupted argument 1 AND never wrote the stack args at all for any call with >=10 arguments** — silently, on a Tier-1 target, for about a year. Fixed v6.4.64. Win64 passes 4 args in `rcx/rdx/r8/r9` and the rest at `[rsp+32..]`; because cyrius pushes args left-to-right (aN on top) but Win64 wants a5 at the LOWEST stack slot, the caller must REVERSE the extras. The PE branch of `ECALLPOPS` did that by shuttling them through a FIXED 5-register table (`r10/r11/r14/r15/r12`) with `if (nextra >= 1..5)` pops and `if (nextra == 1..5)` write-backs. With `nextra = n - 4`, `n >= 10` fell off the end of its own table two ways at once: only 5 of the >=6 extras were popped, so the following `pop r9/r8/rdx/rcx` read SHIFTED slots and `rcx` (argument 1) got the wrong value; and no `nextra ==` branch matched, so the stack args were never emitted and the callee read garbage. Both silent — wrong answers, no error, no warning. Measured on real cass: arity 5-9 correct, 10/11/12 corrupt. SysV had been migrated off the identical register shuttle to rsp-relative addressing at v5.6.24/v6.0.57 for exactly this bug class — **the Win64 branch was never brought along**, which is the durable structural warning: when one ABI branch is rewritten to fix a class, open the sibling branch in the same commit. FIX SHAPE if you touch it again: load `rcx/rdx/r8/r9` IN PLACE via `_emov_rsp_disp` (disp8/disp32 auto-select, no pops); carve the frame BELOW the pushed args so the reversed copy's destination is strictly below its source and cannot overlap at ANY arity (an in-place reversal cannot be done by a single ascending or descending shift — every order clobbers a slot a later read still needs); reverse-copy through `r10`, which is CALLER-saved (the v5.6.24 lesson — r12-r15 are callee-saved and regalloc may pin caller locals there); and share one frame formula between `ECALLPOPS` and `ECALLCLEAN` (`_pe_call_frame`: 32 shadow + nextra*8, 16-rounded, +8 parity pad for odd `n` — the old path got alignment free by popping all `n` slots first, so an in-place path must pay for it explicitly). **WHY IT SURVIVED: it had been written into CLAUDE.md as a LANGUAGE RULE** — 'fns take <=6 args cleanly (register ABI); args 7+ go on the stack and have shown corruption — restructure instead.' Wrong in BOTH directions (7-9 were always fine; the cliff was 10, on one target), it read as settled so nobody re-measured, and on 2026-07-14 it was cited to file an issue **against sigil**, asking a stdlib to restructure around a defect in the compiler that compiles it. The release gate also could not see it structurally: sigil sits outside cycc's include closure and cycc's own PE calls happen to stay <=9 args. The live consumer was sigil 3.12.0 — `argon2id_into` takes 10 args, `argon2_hash_into` 17 — i.e. a password KDF returning a wrong-but-plausible hash, and sandhi's HTTP client (11 fns taking >=10 args, 15 call sites passing >=10) was miscompiled on Win64 in both 1.8.2 and 1.9.0. **THIS IS THE LANGUAGE REPO: when the compiler cannot compile valid cyrius, fix the compiler. If a rule here tells you to work around codegen, treat the rule as the bug report.** Gate: `tests/tcyr/crossos/win64_stack_args.tcyr` (arity 5/9/10/17 + nested), mutation-tested on real cass — old codegen 2 passed / 3 failed, fixed 5 / 0. See CHANGELOG [6.4.64]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["win64", "PE", "ECALLPOPS", "ECALLCLEAN", "stack-args", "nextra", "arity", "_pe_call_frame", "r10-caller-saved", "sysv-migrated-pe-was-not", "codegen-bug-as-language-rule", "sigil-argon2", "sandhi-http", "vr01_win64_stack_args", "v5.6.24", "v6.4.64"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_temporal_invariant_can_guard_a_spatial_collision_invisibly_ts_arena_overlapped_tok_types_by_10mb"
description = "The TS frontend arena was a FIXED base — `S + 0x298B000`, with `TS_HEAP_SIZE = 0xD81000` — so it ran `0x298B000..0x370C000` and **overlapped `tok_types` entirely plus 1.6 MB of `tok_values`: 10,027,008 bytes.** Nothing was ever corrupted, because all three TS modes (`--lex-ts`, `--parse-ts`, `--emit-js`) `SYS_EXIT` before `PREPROCESS` and `LEX`, so the token arrays are never populated while the arena is live. **A TEMPORAL invariant guarding a SPATIAL collision** — held by nothing but call ordering, written down nowhere, and one added 'lex the TS then keep going' path away from a 10 MB heap smash. TWO THINGS MADE IT UNDETECTABLE. `tests/heapmap.sh` structurally could not see it: the base was a **code literal at the call sites, not a map entry**, and the auditor only knows the map. And the map actively said something else — it claimed a 13.3 MB TS reservation between `fixup_tbl` end (`0x207B000`) and `tok_types` (`0x2D7C000`), which does not exist and is 524,288 B smaller than `TS_HEAP_SIZE` regardless. THE ORIGIN IS THE DURABLE PART: `0x298B000 - 0x207B000 = 9,502,720` — **exactly the 9.06 MB the v5.11.68 reorg shrank the heap by.** That reorg moved ~35 region bases and MISSED THIS ONE LITERAL, which had been the pre-reorg `fixup_tbl` end. Which is the entire argument for the v6.4.82 fix: the arena is now `alloc(TS_HEAP_SIZE)`, taken only when a TS mode is actually active, so a normal compile pays nothing and the brk carries no TS carve-out at all. `src/frontend/ts/lex.cyr` had documented `alloc()` as the contract the test drivers use all along — the fixed base was the deviation. **A literal is what the next reorg misses too.** Relocating to another fixed base was never really available anyway: it needs ~14.2 MB contiguous, which does not exist below the arena end, so a fixed-base fix would have been a brk/heap-LAYOUT change requiring the two-step bootstrap — which is why v6.4.81 deliberately filed it rather than bundling it into the tail of an already-gated release. LESSONS: when auditing the heap, grep for `S + 0x` literals in CALL ARGUMENTS, not just map lines; a subsystem reachable only in an early-exit mode is exactly where a spatial invariant rots unnoticed, because no test ever runs past the exit; and prefer `alloc()` to a new fixed base for anything not required to sit at a fixed offset. See CHANGELOG [5.11.68], [6.4.81], [6.4.82]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "ts-arena", "TS_HEAP_SIZE", "temporal-invariant", "spatial-collision", "tok_types", "heap-layout", "alloc-over-fixed-base", "v5.11.68-reorg", "missed-literal", "heapmap-blind-spot", "two-step-bootstrap", "early-exit-mode", "v6.4.82"]

[[entries]]
name = "toktyp_reads_past_gtcnt_as_token_type_0_never_12_eof_so_every_eof_test_fails_and_recovery_cannot_terminate"
description = "`TOKTYP` (`src/backend/common/tokens.cyr`) is an unchecked `L64`. Past `GTCNT` it reads zeroed heap and returns token type **`0`, never `12` (EOF)**. Once panic-mode multi-error recovery existed (v6.4.62) that had a compounding consequence: every `t == 12` EOF test in `_sync_skip` and in the block loops silently failed, recovery could never terminate normally, and `PARSE_STMT`'s forced-progress guard was disarmed too — it only advances while `GTI < GTCNT`, which is exactly the condition that is false out there. Input ending mid-construct — a clipped file, an unclosed `fn`, EOF mid-expression or mid-arg-list — produced **166,670 stderr lines** before the v6.4.62 watchdog aborted. Clamped to EOF inside `PEEKT` at v6.4.78: 166,670 -> 5 lines (unclosed `fn`: 3). **THE PERF OBJECTION THAT GOT IT DEFERRED DID NOT SURVIVE CONTACT WITH THE FIX.** It was filed as its own slot because `PEEKT` is THE hottest function in the parser and a compare+branch there is a hot-path change. But `PEEKT` ALREADY has an `if (_had_error == 1)` guard for the watchdog, and the runaway is exclusively a POST-ERROR phenomenon — LEX unconditionally appends an EOF token and every parse loop stops on type 12, so a well-formed parse never advances past it. Putting the clamp INSIDE that existing guard therefore costs nothing on a clean compile: measured **-0.07 %** (672.8 vs 673.3 ms, interleaved medians on one box), size-neutral, 251/251 tcyr byte-identical. Clamping unconditionally in `TOKTYP` would also work and would tax every lookahead for no benefit. LESSON: **before deferring a hot-path fix on cost, check whether an existing guard already gates the path** — the cheap placement often already exists, and the fix that was 'too expensive to bundle' turns out to be free. The v6.4.62 watchdog is kept deliberately alongside the clamp — a `_had_error`-gated stall detector in `PEEKT` (>500,000 reads with `GTI` unmoved, far above any legal recursive-descent depth so it never false-fires) that bounds ANY future desync the clamp does not anticipate, and it is what broke the VR-02 fuzz wall that had stopped two prior attempts at multi-error recovery. GATE THE SHAPE THAT MATTERS: a garbage-TOKEN input can happen to recover — the pre-existing case did, and missed this entirely — so the gate uses inputs ending MID-CONSTRUCT, asserts <=200 stderr lines, no hang, no SIGSEGV, no output, **and that the watchdog does NOT fire** (its firing is itself the regression signal). Independently fuzz-verified with 60 deterministic random truncations of `lib/str.cyr`: worst case 166,680 -> 18 lines, 0 hangs. See CHANGELOG [6.4.62], [6.4.78]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["TOKTYP", "PEEKT", "GTCNT", "EOF-token-12", "token-type-0", "panic-mode", "_sync_skip", "PARSE_STMT-progress-guard", "watchdog", "hot-path-objection", "existing-guard", "VR-02-fuzz", "truncated-input", "v6.4.62", "v6.4.78"]

[[entries]]
name = "cross_compile_arms_in_main_cyr_are_a_separate_fork_from_the_native_drivers_and_a_tcyr_can_never_see_them"
description = "Two v6.4.81 findings with one root shape: **the path that cross-compiles TO a target is a different code path from the one that runs natively ON that target, and the entire native test corpus exercises only the second.** (1) All six NATIVE drivers predefine `CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS` — `main_win.cyr` since v6.4.31, `main_cx.cyr` since .32, plus `main_x86_macho.cyr` and the `main_aarch64*` forks. `src/main.cyr`'s PE and Mach-O **cross** arms predefined only the target macro, so `lib/simd.cyr`'s whole value-form block vanished and **the same source produced two different programs depending on which host built it.** Half-shipped for the entire minor. A `.tcyr` structurally cannot catch this: it runs natively on each host and therefore exercises `main_win.cyr`, the fork that was always right, and would have stayed green through the whole bug. The new gate (`tests/valform_simd_crosstarget.sh`) checks all four emit paths **on the build host**, which is the only place the divergence is observable; mutation-proven by reverting the two predefines, which fails exactly the `pe` and `macho` legs. Eleven 'non-PE by design' claims in `lib/simd.cyr`, the guide and four `vr01_*` headers were wrong as a consequence — including headers telling readers those tests SKIP on cass when they in fact RUN. That same kind of stale header is what kept the v6.4.44 Win64 retptr bug latent, so treat a 'this test skips on X' comment as a claim to re-verify, not a fact. (2) Both Windows PE gates had been validating a **cycc 5.11.69** binary since 2026-05-19 — the whole of v6.0.x through v6.4.80. Both used an EXISTENCE-ONLY rebuild trigger on `build/cycc_win_cross`, and nothing else in the repo refreshes that artifact: it is not in `cyrius.cyml`'s `cross_bins`, so `cyrius pulsar` and `install.sh` never touch it. A green checkmark for a compiler nobody ships — structurally the same rot as the macOS incident that the cross-OS principle exists for. **An `if [ ! -f X ]` rebuild trigger is a staleness bug, not an optimisation:** rebuild unconditionally, or make the artifact's freshness a checked property. RECOGNITION for both: when a gate is green and the behaviour is broken, ask which FORK the gate actually built and which ARTIFACT it actually ran — not whether the assertions are right. See CHANGELOG [6.4.31], [6.4.44], [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cross-compile", "main.cyr-cross-arms", "predefines", "CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS", "fork-divergence", "tcyr-runs-natively", "cycc_win_cross", "existence-only-rebuild-trigger", "stale-artifact", "green-checkmark-rot", "stale-test-headers", "valform_simd_crosstarget", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "identifier_pool_raises_in_place_only_below_the_0x100000_physical_ceiling_above_it_the_compiler_hangs"
description = "The interned identifier pool `tok_names` sits at `S+0x60000` and was a hard 256 KB cap that stiva 3.0.6 was 7 % from (243737/262144 = 93 %), having already forced that consumer to split its test suite FOUR separate times. v6.4.76 raised it to **512 KB** as a **constants-only** change, verified against live code: the two LEX thresholds `261872 -> 524016` (= 524288 - 272 slack) in `NPOS_GUARD` and `LEXID` (`src/frontend/lex.cyr`), the capacity-warning divisor (`src/common/util.cyr`), the `CYRIUS_STATS` meter denominator (`main.cyr`, `main_win.cyr`), plus the error/warning strings and the heap-map size line in all five forks that carry it. WHAT MAKES IT SAFE IN PLACE, AND WHAT BOUNDS IT: the band `0xA0000-0x100000` (393216 B) is free of any occupant in **ALL SEVEN forks** — the constraint is the UNION of the forks, not the one you are building — so the pool can grow from ending at `0xA0000` to ending at `0xE0000`. **`0x100000` is a PHYSICAL CEILING**: it is `fn_name_hash`'s initial base, so the pool END must stay <= `0x100000`. The theoretical max is 640 KB (threshold 655088), and **above it the compiler HANGS rather than erroring**, because the pool overruns the hash and var tables. Going to 1 MB is a different project — it means relocating the `0x100000+` occupants. ALSO WORTH CARRYING: the pool is NESTED inside `input_buf` (`S+0..0x100000`) and that overlap is legal only as a **temporal** invariant — after `PREPROCESS` the real source lives at `preprocess_out` (`0x459D000`) and LEX writes the pool at `0x60000`, so `input_buf` is dead scratch by lex time. This is NOT a layout change (no region moves, no base changes, no brk/mmap change — lazily-mapped pages cost nothing), so no two-step bootstrap; and cycc's own compile uses ~43 K of identifiers, far under either cap, so the result is byte-identical (251/251 tcyr identical, fixpoint + seed-derive green). GATE IT the way `_idpool_gate` (`programs/checks/selfhost.cyr`) does — emit ~10,000 distinct 35-char globals (~339 KB, over the old cap and under the new) and read the last one back, which is mutation-proven non-vacuous because that exact program errors `identifier buffer full` on the prior release. And update `_cap_drift_gate` (v5.11.50), which cross-checks the heap-map comment against the inline threshold: it exists precisely to catch a raise that edits the code and forgets the map, and it caught this one. See CHANGELOG [6.4.76]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["identifier-pool", "tok_names", "LEXID", "NPOS_GUARD", "524016", "0x100000-physical-ceiling", "hang-not-error", "in-place-cap-raise", "all-seven-forks-union", "temporal-nesting-in-input_buf", "_idpool_gate", "_cap_drift_gate", "not-a-layout-change", "v6.4.76"]

[[entries]]
name = "textual_includes_mean_per_item_file_identity_must_be_stamped_at_preprocess_time_and_the_parser_is_two_pass"
description = "Per-item FILE IDENTITY is the whole substrate under `public`/`private` (v6.5.0), and cyrius includes are TEXTUAL — by parse time there is one flat expanded buffer and the origin file of each fn/var has to have been recorded on the way through. Three traps, all of which shipped green before being caught. (1) **The preprocessor must emit a RESUME marker after an include closes, not just an ENTER marker before it opens.** With enter-only markers every fn declared in the enclosing file AFTER an include is attributed to the INCLUDED file, so `alloc_init` and `atomic_cas` — different files — collided on id 19 and were mutually visible under `private`. **My Phase 1 gate passed anyway because the fixture put its includes first and declared nothing after them**, which is the shape that cannot observe the bug; the rebuilt gate uses a nested include AND code-before-include, and was run RED first to prove it. If you add file tracking to a textual-include preprocessor, the fixture must have code after an include or the gate is decorative. (2) **The parser is TWO-PASS and a pending-modifier flag leaks across the boundary.** `_pub_pending` was consumed in `PARSE_FN_DEF` (pass 2) but not in `_prescan_fn_sig` (pass 1), so the flag set by a `public` in pass 1 survived into the next file — `private` worked in a main source and SILENTLY did nothing when the file arrived via an include. Any per-item modifier needs consuming in BOTH passes; stamp the fileid in both too. (3) **A byte-identical binary is a TELL, not a reassurance.** The Phase 2b var stamp was inserted inside the annotated-only branch (`ann_scalar < 8`), so it fired only for `var x: i32` and never for a bare `var x = 7;`. Nothing failed — the rebuild just came back byte-identical when a real behaviour change had been made, which is the signal that the edit is on a path the compiler's own source never takes. Move the stamp before the width branch (`SVCNT`) and it changes. RECOGNITION for the class: when adding cross-file semantics to a language whose includes are textual, every question of the form 'which file did this come from' is answered at PREPROCESS time or not at all — the parser cannot recover it later. See CHANGELOG [6.5.0]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "parse_return_tail_path_is_the_third_place_found_missing_what_parse_fncall_has"
description = "The overload-suffix dispatch carried TWO independent defects from v5.10.25 to v6.5.1, and the pair is a lesson about WHERE to look rather than about overloads. (1) **The redirect never checked the TARGET's arity** — it decided on argument 1's type alone, so `base(oneArg)` could be rewritten into a call to a two-parameter `base_str`, surplus parameter bound to whatever occupied the register, reported as a WARNING with the binary emitted anyway. (2) **`PARSE_RETURN`'s tail-call fast path never consulted the overload tables at all** — it emits epilogue+jmp itself — so `var r = println(myStr)` routed to `println_str` while `return println(myStr)` silently called the cstr base. **The same call, spelled two ways, ran two different functions.** RECOGNITION: when a transformation lives in ONE call-parsing function, enumerate every path that emits a call without going through it — cyrius has at least three (normal `PARSE_FNCALL`, the `PARSE_RETURN` tail path, inline replay), and this is the THIRD time the tail path was found missing something the normal path had (v6.3.36 plain-struct params, v6.4.53 value-form SIMD params, now overload dispatch). The fix is the same each time and it is NOT to duplicate the logic: set `tc_has_addr = 1` to divert onto the normal path. **THE MEASUREMENT THAT MATTERS**: the arity fix changes **0 of 253** .tcyr while the tail divert changes **81** — attribute a differential per-fix instead of reporting one total, because '0 files changed' is not reassurance that the fix is inert, it is proof the CORPUS HAD ZERO COVERAGE OF THE SHAPE, which is exactly why the defect survived five minors. Identical lesson to v6.4.80 (251/251 byte-identical across a real wrong-answer fix). Also: escalating the arity check from warning to error was verified safe by measuring FIRST — the whole repo (253 .tcyr, all 7 main forks, programs/) emits zero such warnings — and it deliberately does NOT set `_panic`, since an arity mismatch is not a token-stream desync and all bad sites should report in one compile. See CHANGELOG [6.5.1]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "identical_binary_size_is_not_byte_identity"
description = "An UNCHANGED cycc byte-size after a real compiler edit proves nothing — ELF padding absorbs small additions. `cmp`, never `ls -l`. At v6.5.6 a one-line xlat entry left cycc at exactly 1,133,440 B while 872,790 bytes moved."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6.** The standing trap "a byte-identical rebuild after a real behaviour change is a TELL — the edit is on a path cycc's own source never takes" has a sibling that reads the same and means the opposite:

    old cycc  1,133,440 B
    new cycc  1,133,440 B      <- looks byte-identical
    cmp       differ at byte 97; 872,790 bytes differ

Adding one `_msx(S, 231, 0x2000001);` entry to `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` produced a compiler of **exactly** the same size. Section padding/alignment absorbed the added instructions, and a large fraction of the file shifted underneath. Size equality carried zero information.

WHY THIS IS DANGEROUS SPECIFICALLY HERE. The TELL heuristic is real and worth keeping — but it is stated in terms of *byte-identity*, and the cheapest way to check "did my change land" is `ls -l`. Those two are not the same test. Reading an unchanged size as "the edit is on a dead path" leads directly to the wrong conclusion: reverting a correct fix, or hunting for a reason the code is unreachable when it is reached fine.

THE RULE. Prove the change landed **two** ways, neither of them size:

1. `cmp old new` — do the bytes differ at all?
2. A *functional* probe of the changed path. For the 231 entry: compile `syscall(231, r)` with `CYRIUS_MACHO=1` and disassemble — the translation chain must now contain `cmpq $0xe7, %rax` / `movq $0x2000001, %rax`. The old compiler's chain has no `0xe7` compare anywhere.

Only step 2 distinguishes "the bytes moved" from "the bytes moved *for the reason I intended*". A rebuild is nondeterministic in neither direction here, so `cmp` differing is necessary but not sufficient.

COROLLARY for the release notes: "cycc size unchanged" is a legitimate and interesting thing to report (it means the compiler's own output did not grow), but it must never be offered as evidence that a change is inert. Those are different claims about different binaries.

[[entries]]
name = "esysxlat_is_a_linear_cmp_chain_so_an_entry_must_sit_below_every_entry_that_compares_the_number_it_produces"
description = "The aarch64 backend's `ESYSXLAT` (`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`) is a STRAIGHT-LINE chain of `cmp x8,#N / b.ne +8 / movz x8,#M` triples that rewrites `x8` IN PLACE and then keeps falling through. So an entry whose OUTPUT `M` equals a LATER entry's compare `N` is silently re-caught and translated a second time — a wrong syscall, no error, no warning. **Any new entry must sit BELOW every entry that compares the number it produces.** Five prior instances made this rule before v6.5.7 (newfstatat/utimensat last, v6.1.3; faccessat after shutdown, v6.2.x; fsync/fdatasync after rename, v6.2.14; epoll_wait after pipe, v6.4.42) and v6.5.7 added two more. **THE NEW THING AT v6.5.7 IS THE >=1000 PRIVATE-ALIAS BAND.** `fchownat` was unreachable from source on aarch64: its native number **54** is consumed by the x86-compat `setsockopt(54->208)` shim — load-bearing for **51 ecosystem repos that emit raw `syscall(54, ...)`** — and the usual escape (borrow the x86 number, **260**) is this peer's own native `SYS_WAIT4`. With BOTH candidates owned, the answer is a number **no OS mints**: source numbers **>= 1000 are cyrius-private aliases**, renumbered by ESYSXLAT, spelled `1000 + the native number` so the alias documents itself. `SYS_FCHOWNAT = 1054`, and `SYS_CHDIR = 1049` two bullets later for exactly the same reason (native 49 is eaten by `bind(49->200)`; the x86 number 80 is this peer's own `SYS_FSTAT`). ORDERING, CONCRETELY: `1054->54` must stay **LAST in the aarch64-Linux chain** because it produces `x8=54` and the setsockopt entry compares 54 — placed above it, every `fchownat` is silently reissued as `setsockopt`. Mutation-verified: the misordered build returns **ENOPROTOOPT**. `1049->49` must likewise stay below the `bind` entry. **THE MACH-O BRANCH OF THE SAME FUNCTION HAS NO ORDERING HAZARD** — it compares `x8` and writes `x16`, so an output can never be re-caught — but it still needs a row for EVERY alias, because macOS-arm64 resolves the aarch64-Linux peer (there is no `syscalls_aarch64_macos.cyr`), so the alias arrives there too and an absent row leaves a **stale x16**: `1054->468`, `1049->12`. And `_macho_arm_routes()`, the single source of truth behind `parse_expr.cyr`'s macho whitelist warning, must gain the alias too or every build touching it warns 'syscall not routed'. So one alias is FOUR coordinated edits: the enum in `lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr`, the aarch64-Linux chain, the Mach-O chain, and `_macho_arm_routes`. See CHANGELOG [6.5.7]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ESYSXLAT", "aarch64", "syscall-renumber", "private-alias-band", "1054", "1049", "SYS_FCHOWNAT", "SYS_CHDIR", "ordering-rule", "re-capture", "setsockopt-54", "bind-49", "_macho_arm_routes", "stale-x16", "x86-compat-shim", "v6.5.7"]

[[entries]]
name = "darwin_at_star_flags_diverge_from_linux_and_a_pure_renumber_xlat_passes_them_straight_through"
description = "`AT_FDCWD` is **-2** on Darwin, not Linux's -100 — that was found by ports and corrected at v6.2.40. **The FLAGS diverge exactly the same way and were left Linux-valued for three more minors**, fixed at v6.5.7 in `lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr`: `AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW` **0x20** (Linux 0x100), `AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW` **0x40** (Linux 0x400), `AT_REMOVEDIR` **0x80** (Linux 0x200) — values read off ecb's own `$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)/usr/include/sys/fcntl.h`. **WHY A RENUMBER DOES NOT SAVE YOU:** the at-family ESYSXLAT entries are PURE renumbers — they rewrite the syscall number and pass every argument, flags included, straight through — so a Linux flag value is simply an invalid flag on Darwin: **EINVAL, forever, silently.** Measured consequences on real ecb/ach: `sys_fchownat` returned **-22** the first time it was ever run there, and **`xrmdir` had never once worked on macOS-arm64 since the day it shipped at v6.5.2**. That second one had a compounding cause worth its own line — the macho branch mapped `unlinkat(35)` to Darwin `unlink(10)` **with an arg-shift that DROPPED the dirfd and the flag**. Right for `unlink`; fatal for `rmdir`, which is the SAME syscall distinguished only by `AT_REMOVEDIR`, so `sys_rmdir` ran `unlink(AT_FDCWD)` with the dirfd `-2` reinterpreted as the path pointer. Now a pure renumber to Darwin `unlinkat(472)`, which fixes `unlink` and `rmdir` together. `AT_EMPTY_PATH` (0x1000) has NO Darwin equivalent and is deliberately left unguarded, because no at-family wrapper here uses it on the macOS path — if you add one, that changes. **THE RECOGNITION RULE: whenever a constant is `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS`-corrected, the fix is not done until every SIBLING constant in the same header has been re-read off a real SDK.** A dirfd corrected without its flags is the same half-fix shape as `STAT_SIZE` corrected without the rest of its struct (see the sibling note) and `unlink` mapped without `rmdir` — five of the seven defects v6.5.7 found were this shape, each of which looked complete because the thing that prompted it started working. None of it is visible on the host suite; `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_wrappers.tcyr` on hardware is the thing that disagrees. See CHANGELOG [6.2.40], [6.5.2], [6.5.7]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["darwin", "macos", "AT_FDCWD", "AT_REMOVEDIR", "AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW", "AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW", "AT_EMPTY_PATH", "pure-renumber-passes-flags", "unlinkat-472", "xrmdir", "half-fix", "EINVAL-silently", "vr01_syscall_wrappers", "v6.5.7"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_macos_x86_stat_enum_was_a_mix_of_two_structs_because_only_the_field_that_prompted_the_fix_was_corrected"
description = "`lib/syscalls_macos.cyr`'s `Stat` enum described **two different structs at once**, and had since v6.0.41. The x86 backend maps `stat(4)` to Darwin **188** — the LEGACY, non-INODE64 `struct stat` — via `_msx(S, 4, 0x20000BC)` in `src/backend/x86/emit.cyr`, NOT to `stat64` (338). But the enum carried **stat64** offsets. Only `STAT_SIZE` was ever corrected, empirically, at v6.0.41 when something visibly broke; `MODE / NLINK / UID / GID / MTIME` kept stat64's layout. So on Intel-Mac **`STAT_MODE` read the low half of `st_ino`** — every mode test silently false, no error — and **`STAT_MTIME` read mtime's NANOSECONDS as its seconds**. Re-dumped against a real 0755 directory on ach at v6.5.7 and corrected to 188's layout: `mode@8`, `nlink@10`, `uid@12`, `gid@16`, `mtime@40`, `mtime_nsec@48`, `size@72`. **WHY THE HALF-FIX LOOKED FINISHED, AND WHY IT SURVIVED 4 MINORS:** the one consumer that mattered was `cbt/deps.cyr:_file_size`, which uses `STAT_SIZE` — the one field that HAD been fixed — so everything anyone actually ran on Intel-Mac worked, and nothing read `STAT_MODE` there until v6.5.7's `_xdir_exists` did. **THE DURABLE RULE: a struct-offset enum is a single artifact describing a single struct — when one offset in it is found wrong, the finding is that the WHOLE enum describes the wrong struct, and the repair is to re-dump every field against the real kernel on real hardware, not to patch the field that surfaced.** And check WHICH struct the backend actually routes to before trusting any offset table: on Darwin `stat`/`stat64`/`fstatat64` are three different layouts, and the number in the xlat table is the only thing that says which one you get. Same half-fix family as the `AT_*` flags corrected one dirfd at a time (sibling note). See CHANGELOG [6.0.41], [6.5.7]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macos-x86", "intel-mac", "ach", "Stat-enum", "struct-offsets", "darwin-188", "stat64-338", "STAT_MODE", "STAT_MTIME", "half-fix", "re-dump-the-whole-struct", "EMACHO_SYSXLAT", "v6.0.41", "v6.5.7"]

[[entries]]
name = "qemu_user_reports_a_false_thread_va_leak_because_proc_self_statm_measures_the_host_process"
description = "`tests/tcyr/crossos/thread_detach.tcyr` (v6.5.8) asserts that 100 detached threads grow virtual address space by **exactly 0** — before the fix they grew it by **210,124,800 B**, i.e. 100 x 2,101,248 (`THREAD_STACK_SIZE + THREAD_GUARD_SIZE`) to the byte, with `vm.max_map_count` (65530) as the ceiling, so a thread-per-request server degraded to `thread_create` returning 0 after ~65K spawns. **DO NOT TRUST `qemu-aarch64` FOR THIS TEST — IT REPORTS A FALSE LEAK.** Under qemu-user the file fails with ~1,665 residual pages per detached thread, which is not even the 513-page stack: qemu maps its OWN per-thread emulator state into the same address space, and `/proc/self/statm` measures the HOST process, not the guest. On real pi the identical binary reports a delta of exactly 0 and the file passes 115/0. Same class as the v6.4.42 aarch64 epoll bug that only real pi could surface — **if a VA/mapping assertion goes red under qemu, run it on pi before believing it.** THREE MORE MEASUREMENT TRAPS baked into that file, each of which cost a false reading while it was being written. (1) **VA, not RSS.** Thread stacks are touch-on-demand, so the leak showed as ~200 resident pages while VA grew 51,300 — an RSS-based test misses it entirely. (2) **Poll until it settles; never sample at a fixed instant.** A spin loop is not a synchronisation primitive: on a slow machine the children simply have not reached their `munmap` yet, which reads as a leak. The property that actually distinguishes fixed from broken is that VA comes BACK DOWN AT ALL — with the leak it never does, so a bounded retry loop (400 x 5 ms) separates 'slow' from 'broken' where a fixed sleep cannot. (3) **Let the warm-up threads finish BEFORE sampling the baseline**, or they unmap during the measurement and you get a NEGATIVE growth of -513 pages — exactly one thread — the measurement racing the thing it measures. **AND ASSERT ZERO, NOT 'LESS'**: the detached child unmaps its stack AND the TLS block carved from the top of that same mapping and allocates no handle at all, so 0 is the correct number and a threshold like '< 20 MiB' would still pass with the TLS or the handle leaking. See CHANGELOG [6.4.42], [6.5.8]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["qemu-user", "false-positive", "proc-self-statm", "host-vs-guest", "thread_create_detached", "VA-not-RSS", "poll-until-settled", "baseline-race", "assert-zero-not-less", "real-pi-hardware", "vr01_thread_detach", "v6.5.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_does_not_inline_so_a_one_load_accessor_on_a_dispatch_path_is_a_real_call_frame"
description = "**cyrius has no inliner for ordinary calls, so every accessor is a real frame — and an accessor whose entire body is one `load64` costs strictly more than the load it wraps.** `alloc_via` (`lib/alloc.cyr`), the hot path of the whole `_a` allocator convention, cost **15.1 ns** while the `arena_alloc` bump underneath it is about eight instructions: **5.1 ns of that was two accessor CALLS** — `allocator_alloc_fn(a)` to perform one `load64(a)` and `allocator_state(a)` to perform one `load64(a + 32)` — against ~6.0 ns for the bump itself. A third level was pure trampoline: `_arena_alloc`, whose entire body was `return arena_alloc(state, size)`. v6.5.10 reads the vtable inline in the four dispatch helpers (`alloc_via` / `realloc_via` / `free_via` / `reset_via`) and registers `&arena_alloc` / `&arena_reset` directly: **15-16 -> 12 ns** from the inlining, **-> 11 ns** with the trampolines gone. The accessor inlining is the large half. **THIS IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE `allocator_new` FIXES THE LAYOUT** — reading `a + 0` / `a + 32` directly is exactly as safe as calling the accessors, which remain as public API; only the hot path stopped calling them. **DO NOT GENERALISE THE INLINING INTO A DELETION.** `_bump_alloc` and `_bump_reset` look like the same trampoline shape and are NOT: they ADAPT ARITY (`alloc(size)` and `alloc_reset()` take fewer arguments than the vtable signature passes), so they genuinely translate rather than pass through, and removing them corrupts the call. The distinction — a frame that only forwards vs. a frame that changes the argument list — is the thing to check before deleting any wrapper, and `tests/alloc_via_no_plumbing.sh` asserts both directions. **WHY A FEW NANOSECONDS EARNED A RELEASE SLOT:** `_a` threading multiplies the cost by the size of the object graph, which is the entire point of the `_a` families. agnosai counted **112 allocations on one route — 32 % of the request** — with a counting allocator wrapped around the arena's own vtable, and that was AFTER a hoisting pass; `bayan_json_v_obj_new_a` alone is three `alloc_via` calls. See CHANGELOG [6.5.10]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["no-inlining", "alloc_via", "allocator-vtable", "dispatch-plumbing", "trampoline", "_bump_alloc-adapts-arity", "arity-adapter-vs-passthrough", "_a-convention", "allocator_new-fixes-layout", "alloc_via_no_plumbing", "v6.5.10"]

[[entries]]
name = "negating_at_i64_min_is_a_no_op_so_every_while_n_gt_0_decimal_formatter_printed_a_bare_minus"
description = "`n = 0 - n` is a **NO-OP at `i64::MIN`** — two's-complement absolute value overflows back to itself — so a formatter written as 'emit the sign, negate, then `while (n > 0)` peel digits' emits the sign byte **and nothing else**. `str_from_int(i64::MIN)` was the one-character string `\"-\"`, and bayan then serialised `{\"n\":-}`, which is not JSON, **with no error raised at any layer**. Both the `n == 0` special case and the digit loop are skipped, because the negated value is still negative. Fixed at v6.5.8 across **12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named**: four in `lib/fmt.cyr`, plus `string.cyr`, `log.cyr`, sakshi (fixed UPSTREAM at 2.4.8 and re-vendored, per 'fix the source repo, not the fold'), **two inside the compiler itself that the roadmap never listed** — `PRNUM` (`src/common/util.cyr:849`) and `_emit_decimal` (`src/main.cyr:888`) — and three shipped demos. **THE PART WORTH CARRYING IS THAT THIS EXACT BUG HAD ALREADY BEEN FIXED IN THE FUNCTION DIRECTLY ABOVE.** `fmt_hex` sits immediately below `fmt_int` in the same file and was corrected for the same `> 0` vs `!= 0` reason at v6.4.69 — **the decimal siblings were never brought along**, which is the same one-branch-at-a-time shape as the Darwin `AT_*` flags and the macOS `Stat` enum. When you fix a loop-termination predicate in one formatter, grep the whole tree for the predicate, not the function name. PREREQUISITE VERIFIED BEFORE RELYING ON IT: truncation toward zero for negative `/` and `%` was confirmed on x86_64, aarch64 **and** cx, since the digit-peel for a still-negative `n` depends on it. See CHANGELOG [6.4.69], [6.5.8]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["i64-min", "twos-complement", "0-minus-n-is-a-noop", "fmt_int", "fmt_hex", "PRNUM", "_emit_decimal", "gt-0-vs-ne-0", "sakshi-2.4.8", "fix-upstream-then-fold", "truncation-toward-zero", "grep-the-predicate-not-the-fn", "v6.4.69", "v6.5.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "an_allocator_returning_0_is_not_an_error_channel_when_the_type_system_has_no_option"
description = "**The arena primitives all handled exhaustion CORRECTLY and that was not the bug.** `arena_alloc` returns 0, `str_from_a` returns 0, `vec_push_a` returns -1. The bug is what the 0 does NEXT: a `Str` of 0 is **indistinguishable from a valid `Str`**, there is no option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so it flows on and the first thing that dereferences it faults. 'Arena too small' was observable as a **SIGSEGV several layers away, in a JSON walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.** **THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS WHAT MADE IT SERIOUS:** before threading a route onto an arena, an oversized response was a *leak* on the no-free global bump; after threading — a change whose entire purpose is to stop leaking — the same response was a *crash*. **Adopting the feature correctly made the failure worse and quieter**, and a 5,500-assertion suite missed it because allocation tests are small-fixture by nature: they assert `bytes == 0` over a handful of items and nothing about that shape ever probes the ceiling. v6.5.9 ships one **policy field** (`a + 24`) rather than three features: `ARENA_FULL_NULL` (0, today's behaviour and still the default, so no existing arena moves) / `ARENA_FULL_GROW` (1, chain another chunk — `arena_new_growable` / `arena_allocator_growable`) / `ARENA_FULL_SPILL` (2, serve overflow from the global allocator) / `ARENA_FULL_ABORT` (3, die loudly at the allocation instead of faulting three layers away). FOUR THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TOUCHING IT. (1) **GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`** — the bump allocator underneath has no `free()`, so releasing them is not expressible, and it is not what an arena wants anyway: reset rewinds to the first chunk and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on its high-water mark and then allocates nothing (verified: 500 allocations twice over produce identical total capacity). (2) `arena_used` cannot show the whole chain once it has grown — that is what `arena_capacity_total` is for. (3) `arena_free` previously cleared only base/ptr/end and **left the policy set**, so a 'freed' growable arena would take the grow path and **succeed**; it now clears the policy back to `ARENA_FULL_NULL`. (4) **MUTATION TESTING CHANGED THE CODE HERE, NOT JUST THE GATE**: the mutation 'grow ignores an oversize request' PASSED, because `arena_alloc` returned the post-grow pointer **without re-checking the bound** — a silent heap overflow, not a failed allocation — and the test could not see it either, asserting only `!= 0`. Both were fixed: a defensive bound re-check, and a first/last-byte write-and-read-back that only survives if the memory is really there. `lib/alloc.cyr` is inside cycc's include closure and on the seed path, so all of this carried the full fixpoint + seed-derive gate. See CHANGELOG [6.5.9]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arena", "exhaustion-policy", "ARENA_FULL_NULL", "ARENA_FULL_GROW", "ARENA_FULL_SPILL", "ARENA_FULL_ABORT", "zero-is-not-an-error-channel", "no-option-type", "regression-direction", "grow-retains-chunks", "arena_capacity_total", "arena_free-left-policy-set", "mutation-changed-the-code", "seed-path", "v6.5.9"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_distlib_deps_sidecar_scanned_includes_not_the_declaration_and_a_wrong_sidecar_switches_off_cyriuss_own_consumer_check"
description = "`dist/<lib>.deps` says it lists 'stdlib leaves this fold requires in scope', but until v6.5.10 it was built **ONLY by scanning bundled module sources for literal `include \"lib/X.cyr\"` lines** — so it captured what a module INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the manifest declaration. setu 0.8.2 emitted **8** leaves against a declared **12**; the four missing (`result`, `net`, `chrono`, `args`) are all referenced at top level in `src/client.cyr`. **THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT A MISLEADING FILE — IT IS A DISABLED CHECK.** `cyrius deps` validates a consumer's `[deps] stdlib` against the dep's sidecar, correctly and loudly. The filer measured both directions: with a corrected 12-leaf sidecar an under-declaring consumer got a hard error naming `chrono` and `args`; with the raw 8-leaf one **the same consumer built `OK`, silently**, on agnos. A wrong sidecar switches OFF the machine check that exists to catch exactly that mistake. Fixed by making the sidecar the declared `[deps] stdlib` **UNIONED** with the include-scan, not either alone — the scan can legitimately catch a leaf the author forgot to declare, so the union can never under-report relative to either source. **THE CONSTRAINT THAT KEEPS THE FIX FROM BEING WRONG IN THE OTHER DIRECTION: base bundle only.** A `[lib.X]` PROFILE is a narrow subset of modules, so its true dep set is a SUBSET of the declaration; unioning the whole declaration into e.g. a `sha` profile would OVER-report and could fail a legitimately-narrow consumer against the very check this re-enables. Profiles keep the pruned inference, and `tests/distlib_deps_sidecar.sh` asserts that asymmetry explicitly. **THREE HYPOTHESES WERE MEASURED AND FALSIFIED BY THE FILER BEFORE FILING** — it is not the declaration, not an inline-comment parse bug, not a direct-symbol scan — and they are recorded in the gate so none of them gets re-investigated. See CHANGELOG [6.5.10]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["distlib", "deps-sidecar", "cyrius-deps-validation", "under-report", "union-declaration-with-scan", "base-bundle-only", "profiles-keep-pruned-inference", "over-report-hazard", "setu", "falsified-hypotheses-in-the-gate", "v6.5.10"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_tail_call_guard_was_syntactic_so_a_pointer_laundered_through_a_local_freed_the_frame_under_the_callee"
description = "`return f(args)` takes cycc's tail-call path: the epilogue tears the frame DOWN and JMPs, so the callee executes on released stack. v5.8.16 §8 already knew this and guarded it — **by scanning the call's argument list for a literal `&` token**. That scan is SYNTACTIC, so it only ever saw the direct spelling. `return f(&buf)` was guarded from v5.8.16; `var p = &buf; ... return f(p)` walked straight past it and read dead stack. **Same source semantics, opposite results, no diagnostic — a silent wrong answer, not a crash.** Fixed at v6.5.14 by replacing the syntactic test with a semantic one: `_fn_local_addr` is set at the SINGLE `ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` site in `parse_expr.cyr` wherever a fn materialises the address of one of its own locals, reset per fn in `PARSE_FN_DEF`, and read by the guard in `parse_fn.cyr`. One site covers every laundering path — through variables, arithmetic, structs, globals — because they all have to go through `&local` first. A fn that never takes a local's address keeps the flag at 0, so its codegen is byte-identical (cycc itself grew **16 bytes**). **THE ARITY IS WHY IT HID FOR YEARS AND IS THE PART WORTH CARRYING:** cycc already declines TCO above **6 arguments** (stack args are clobbered by the epilogue), so the defect is reachable ONLY at ≤6 args — the identical helper called with 7 args was fine. Downstream that does not look like a compiler bug, it looks like a cursed *buffer*. sigil hit exactly this: its 10-arg RSA verify wrappers localised cleanly at 3.12.3 while their 6-arg SIGN twins 'broke the KATs', so **three consecutive sigil releases (3.12.3, .4, .5) each shipped a source comment saying localisation regressed tests for reasons not understood** — and the shared `cbank()` lanes that comment justified were an **authentication bypass** (1 of 800 forged PSS signatures accepted under lane aliasing). **THE DIAGNOSTIC LESSON IS SHARPER THAN THE FIX.** 3.12.3 recorded that 'callee-clobbers-caller was ruled out'; that came from a probe window over the buffer's first 8 bytes, and the corruption was at **offset 223**. A probe that cannot see the defect is not evidence of absence — it froze a security fix for three releases. The measurement that finally settled it was two addresses printed side by side: a callee's local sat ABOVE every one of the caller's locals, which is only possible if the caller's frame is already gone. **This is the FIFTH thing found missing from this tail path** (v6.3.36 plain-struct params, v6.4.53 value-form SIMD params, v6.5.1 overload dispatch, v6.5.2 the literal-to-cstring error, now this) — when you touch `PARSE_FNCALL`, ask what the tail path does NOT do. Gate: `tests/tcyr/crossos/tailcall_frame_escape.tcyr`, in `crossos/` because the epilogue exists on every backend and the frame it frees is ABI-shaped; acceptance is **aliased ≡ direct**, not aliased ≡ a literal. See CHANGELOG [6.5.14], sigil [3.12.6]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["tail-call", "TCO", "frame-escape", "_fn_local_addr", "v5.8.16-section-8", "syntactic-vs-semantic-guard", "ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR", "6-arg-threshold", "silent-wrong-answer", "sigil-3.12.6", "rsa-pss", "authentication-bypass", "probe-window-too-narrow", "offset-223", "fifth-miss-on-the-tail-path", "crossos-gate", "v6.5.14"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_line_the_roadmap_called_the_whole_cross_bb_defect_was_a_deliberate_correctness_guard_and_deleting_it_fails_69_of_282"
description = "v6.5.35, band F. The roadmap had said for months that the cross-BB regalloc defect was **live and localised to ONE line**: `parse_fn.cyr` force-sets every live interval's end to the function end, so the linear-scan picker's expire step never fires and assignment is greedy-forever. That description is accurate and **acting on it ships a miscompile**. The line is a DELIBERATE v5.6.22 guard — naive time-sharing is unsound across a backward edge, because when the picker reuses a register for a later interval a `JMP_BACK` into a position inside an EARLIER interval re-reads that register expecting the earlier value and finds the later one. **Mutation-measured at v6.5.35: restoring naive expire fails 69 of the 282 corpus tests**, across 13 buckets (crypto 13, formats 11, text 10, stdlib 8, derive 8, frontend 5 …), with WRONG ANSWERS rather than crashes. ⭐ **AND THE PIN NAMED ONLY HALF THE DEFECT.** A second, unmentioned blocker made the first one moot: `picked` counted assignments over the whole FUNCTION's lifetime and was capped at `_cur_fn_regalloc` (5), so once five locals had ever received a register every later interval was blocked no matter how many registers expire had just freed. **The tell was a null result** — fixing the famous one line changed NOT ONE BYTE of any consumer program's output (`json_engine`, `math_pack_integration`, `sakshi_full` all byte-identical), which is what sent the investigation looking for a second cause. A codegen fix that produces byte-identical output is EVIDENCE, not reassurance. The real fix is loop-aware liveness: `RA_SCAN_LOOPS` (`backend/x86/decode.cyr`) walks the emitted range with `DECODE_LEN` + `CLASSIFY_CF`/`CF_TARGET` and records backward branches; `_ra_loop_extend` extends an interval overlapping a loop body to that loop's branch, iterating to a fixpoint for nesting. ⚠ **The scan returns -1 for `no information`, NOT `no loops`** — undecodable range or edge-cap overflow — and the caller must then fall back to the whole-function extent, because a PARTIAL edge list is worse than none: the picker would time-share across exactly the loop the scan failed to see. ⚠ **Only three of the seven forks include `decode.cyr`** (main, win, x86_macho); the three aarch64 forks and cx take a `return -1` stub in their own `emit.cyr`, the same pattern the `E*_PE` reroutes use. Gate: `tests/gates/ir-opt/regalloc_cross_bb.sh`. See CHANGELOG [6.5.35]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["regalloc", "linear-scan", "poletto-sarkar", "cross-bb", "live-intervals", "expire", "v5.6.22-guard", "backward-edge", "loop-aware-liveness", "RA_SCAN_LOOPS", "_ra_loop_extend", "lifetime-cap-vs-simultaneous", "byte-identical-is-evidence", "fail-safe-minus-one", "decode-cyr-fork-split", "69-of-282", "v6.5.35"]

[[entries]]
name = "removing_a_cap_unmasks_whatever_the_cap_was_hiding_and_here_it_was_an_O_n_squared_safety_scan"
description = "v6.5.35. Lifting the register allocator's lifetime cap (see the band F note) was correct and immediately cost **+8.1 % compile time** — self_compile 712 ms -> 770 ms on a quiet box, reproducible across three interleaved rounds. Nothing new was added to the hot path; the cap had been HIDING an existing cost. `Is this local safe to promote?` was answered by walking the whole function's bytes ONCE PER CANDIDATE, which is O(candidates x function bytes) — affordable only while the cap stopped the loop after five picks. **The general shape: when you remove a bound, look for what the bound was making affordable, not only for what it was preventing.** The question turned out to be per-LOCAL and independent of which candidate asks, so it now fills a per-local flag in ONE pass and every lookup is O(1) — +58 ms back to +0-4 ms, flat within noise. Behaviour-preservation was proven the cheap way: compile 60 corpus files with the pre- and post-hoist compilers and require byte-identical output (60/60). ⚠ **The perf claims in this release were nearly all wrong on first measurement, in both directions.** A best-of-7 A/B said the generated code got 11-12 % faster; best-of-25 collapsed it to ±2 % — noise on 1.3 ms programs. And `bench-history.csv` records a -6.6 % self_compile improvement that is not real either: it compares against v6.5.34's 767 ms, which v6.5.34's own CHANGELOG documents as a LOADED-box reading. **On a busy box, A/B the two binaries interleaved rather than comparing to a stored number — and pick a repetition count that makes the difference survive.** See CHANGELOG [6.5.35], [6.5.34]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["performance", "regalloc", "safety-scan", "hoisting", "O-n-squared", "cap-was-hiding-cost", "byte-identical-verification", "benchmark-noise", "best-of-25", "interleaved-ab", "loaded-box", "bench-history-baseline", "v6.5.35"]


[[entries]]
name = "visibility_enforced_at_reference_not_insertion"
description = "A feature enforced only where a name is USED is not a boundary — private fns collided for five minors (v6.5.0-.37)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: `private` was implemented as a CHECK AT
THE REFERENCE SITE layered on top of an UNCHANGED global symbol table. That
makes the feature look complete — the error message fires, the gate is green,
`nm` output shrinks — while the thing it claims to guarantee is absent.

What actually happened (v6.5.0 through v6.5.37): the fn table held exactly one
entry per NAME. A second definition reused that entry and overwrote its body,
so a private helper was silently replaced by any same-named fn in another file,
INCLUDING for its own file's internal calls. Declaring `private` on BOTH sides
changed nothing, because neither declaration touched INSERTION.

    a.cyr:  private fn _helper(s) { return 1; }
            public  fn a_entry(s) { return _helper(s); }
    b.cyr:  private fn _helper(c) { return 0; }
            public  fn b_entry(c) { return _helper(c); }

    a_entry(0) * 10 + b_entry(0)  ->  exit 0.   Correct is 10.

⭐ THE TELL WAS IN THE DOCS THE WHOLE TIME. The guide promised "private fns are
omitted from the exported symbol table". That is a statement about the SYMBOL
TABLE, and nothing in the implementation touched the symbol table. When a
feature's documented guarantee names a data structure the code never modifies,
the feature is a check, not a boundary.

⚠ AND THE WARNING ACTIVELY MISLED. The collision surfaced as
`duplicate fn ... last definition wins`, which reads as benign shadowing and
usually is. It is not benign when the two definitions disagree about arity,
struct layout, or RETURN POLARITY — and nothing in the warning says which case
you are in. The filed instance (vyakarana + sankoch, both defining a private
`_stream_grow`) disagreed about all three; a wrong binding there reports every
successful buffer grow as a FAILURE, inside a tokenizer, on file content.

⚠ ZERO ADOPTION HID IT. 0 of 292 first-party files across seven repos declared
`private`, so nothing in the ecosystem exercised the boundary for five minors.
A feature with no users generates no bug reports — that is not evidence it
works. The first consumer to actually try it filed this within days.

⚠ THE GATE COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT, for a mechanical reason worth copying:
the v6.5.37 gate builds every probe by CONCATENATING sources into one file. The
defect keys on ORIGIN FILE, so every concatenated probe gets a single file id
and exercises nothing. A multi-file defect needs a multi-file gate, on disk,
with real `include`s.

FIX: split same-named definitions into separate symbols at insertion when a
private one is involved and the origin files differ; resolve preferring the
caller's own private definition, then the first non-private one, then fall
through so the precise privacy error still fires rather than "undefined
function". See tests/gates/frontend/private_scoped_at_insertion.sh (v6.5.38).

[[entries]]
name = "one_ifdef_two_backends_different_pointer_contracts"
description = "One #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS covers two Mach-O backends whose syscall reroutes disagree about whether an argument is dereferenced (v6.5.43)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: a single `#ifdef` names an OPERATING
SYSTEM, but the reroutes underneath it are per-BACKEND and can differ in
CONTRACT, not just in number. Code written and tested against one of them
compiles unchanged for the other and is silently wrong there.

The instance: `syscall(228)` (clock) is rerouted on three targets and they
disagree about argument 3.
  arm64-macOS   EMACHO_CLOCK_ARM  -> __got _clock_gettime_nsec_np(id)
                                     ns in the return register; arg 3 IGNORED
  x86_64-macOS  EMACHO_CLOCK_X86  -> gettimeofday composition
                                     `pop rdi` / `mov rax,[rdi]` / `mov ecx,[rdi+8]`
                                     arg 3 DEREFERENCED
  Windows PE    EGETTICKS_PE      -> GetTickCount64(); arg 3 IGNORED

`_prof_clock_ns()` wrote `return syscall(228, 4, 0);` inside
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS`. Correct on ecb. A NULL dereference on ach.
Measured on real hardware: `syscall(228, 4, 0)` exits 139, `syscall(228, 4, &ts)`
returns a live nanosecond count.

The two backends diverged for a real reason, not carelessness: Darwin has NO
clock_gettime syscall at all, arm64 solves it by binding libSystem through
__got, and the x86 Mach-O writer emits a static LC_UNIXTHREAD binary with no
dylib and no __got to bind through. So the composition was the only option on
that fork — and composing from gettimeofday REQUIRES a buffer the __got route
does not.

⚠ WHY IT SURVIVED: it was LATENT. CYRIUS_PROF is wired in src/main.cyr alone,
so the macOS forks never call the fn, and every lib/ caller already passed a
real buffer. A latent defect generates no failure to investigate. It would have
become a SIGSEGV the moment profiling was wired into a macOS fork.

FIX: pass a real buffer unconditionally — every route accepts one, only some
require it. Pinned by tests/gates/platform/macho_clock_buffer_contract.sh, which
tracks #ifdef nesting (the literal-0 form stays legal under CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN)
and PREMISE-CHECKS that EMACHO_CLOCK_X86 still dereferences arg 3 before
enforcing anything.

[[entries]]
name = "a_diagnostic_that_exists_on_one_backend_only"
description = "The x86 Mach-O backend had no unrouted-syscall warning for eleven minors while arm64 did — an asymmetry no route-parity gate can see (v6.5.43)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE: route tables get compared across backends; DIAGNOSTICS do not. A
warning that exists on one backend and not the other is invisible to a gate
that compares routes, and it silently changes what each target's failures look
like — one warns at compile time, the other crashes at runtime.

Since v5.5.15 the arm64 Mach-O backend warned on any syscall ESYSXLAT/__got did
not handle. `_TARGET_MACHO == 1` was never checked, so the x86 Mach-O backend
warned on NOTHING. On Intel-Mac a missing route was silent at compile time and
a SIGSYS at runtime. `ioctl` sat unrouted there and was found at v6.5.36 only by
reading the table; v6.5.16 lists five ach corpus failures with the same cause.

⭐ TWO LESSONS WORTH COPYING.

1. ANSWER FROM THE EMITTING ROWS, NOT A SECOND LIST. `_macho_x86_routes` replays
   EMACHO_SYSXLAT itself in a query mode (a flag makes each row compare instead
   of emit) so the answer cannot drift from the behaviour. v6.0.65 had already
   had to DELETE a parallel hardcoded list on the ARM side after it drifted to 9
   entries against ~40 real routes. The same list, re-typed on the other
   backend, would have been the same bug.
   ⚠ The first version omitted the numbers `parse_expr` reroutes BEFORE they
   reach the chain (228 clock, 35 nanosleep), so the new warning fired on the
   two syscalls x86-macOS handles BEST. Reroutes are routes.

2. NAME THE THING. The ARM warning fired 470 times during cycc's own build
   without once saying WHICH syscall. That is why it read as noise and got
   scrolled past, and why five unrouted Darwin concurrency syscalls sat
   unnoticed. Both diagnostics now print the number (PRNUM). Same lesson the
   v6.4.77 reserved-keyword diagnostic taught: a diagnostic that does not
   identify its subject is barely a diagnostic.

⚠ NOT EVERY HIT IS A BUG — check, do not assume. Turning it on named two
numbers in cycc's own macOS build. 228 was a false positive (see lesson 1).
12 (`brk`) is real but benign: it sits in EMITELF_OBJ, which has no callers
anywhere in the tree. The warning stays because it is truthful.

Pinned by tests/gates/platform/macho_diagnostic_parity.sh (mutation-proven five
ways, including a straight revert to the pre-6.5.43 state).

[[entries]]
name = "one_shot_process_registration_closes_the_raw_thread_path"
description = "bsdthread_register is one-shot per process and libSystem always takes it first, so arm64-macOS cannot install its own thread trampoline (v6.5.44)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: a kernel facility can be PRESENT, correctly
ROUTED, and still UNUSABLE, because something else in the process already consumed
a one-shot resource. Routing proves reachability, not availability.

v6.5.43 routed all five Darwin concurrency syscalls on both Mach-O backends and
proved them reaching the kernel on real ecb and ach. Every one of those routes is
correct. And `bsdthread_create` is STILL unusable on arm64-macOS, because:

  * `bsdthread_register` installs the process-wide thread-start trampoline and is
    ONE-SHOT. A second call returns EINVAL.
  * cyrius's arm64 Mach-O writer emits a dyld/LC_MAIN PIE that links
    /usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib, so libpthread's initializer registers first.
  * Measured from a REAL cyrius-compiled binary on ecb: exit 22 (EINVAL).
  * A libSystem-free process is not an escape either — on macOS 26 a static
    LC_UNIXTHREAD arm64 binary is SIGKILLed by AppleSystemPolicy.

⭐ THE ASYMMETRY IS THE INTERESTING PART, and it is a property of the two WRITERS,
not of the OS. The x86 Mach-O writer emits a static MH_NOUNDEFS/LC_UNIXTHREAD binary
with no dyld and no libSystem — so on Intel-Mac registration DOES work and the raw
bsdthread path is the right one, while on Apple Silicon it is closed. One "#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS" spans two targets whose thread primitives cannot be the same.

WHAT WORKS INSTEAD, and it needed no new machinery at all: __got[5] had been bound
to `_pthread_create` since v5.6.6 and never used. No GOT growth, no new symbol, no
bind-opcode arithmetic. And no trampoline: a Darwin start routine is
`void *(*)(void *)`, which is exactly the shape of a cyrius `fn body(arg): i64` —
arg 1 in x0, result in x0, stock AAPCS64 prologue.

⚠ THE TEMPTING WRONG TURN was to hand-build a pthread_t and use libpthread's
already-registered trampoline. It WORKS — it was prototyped end to end — but it
depends on sig@0x00 (validated against a per-process ptr_munge cookie), fun@0x90,
arg@0x98, tsd@0xE0 and a PAC modifier, none of which is stable ABI. Working is not
the bar; surviving an OS update is.

[[entries]]
name = "a_corpus_that_cannot_tell_real_threads_from_inline_calls"
description = "Six concurrency tests passed on macOS for four minors while thread bodies ran serially — every assertion was honestly satisfiable by an inline call (v6.5.44)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE: a test suite measures the OBSERVABLE contract, and for concurrency the
obvious observables are all satisfied by a serial implementation. "The worker ran."
"join returned 0." "The final count is exactly 8000." "The mutex excluded." A
backend that runs the body INLINE at thread_create satisfies every one of them,
honestly, with no second thread anywhere.

macOS ran thread bodies inline from v6.5.11 to v6.5.43. The crossos corpus was
green the whole time — 57 assertions across six files on real hardware.

⛔ WORSE, THE ACCEPTANCE CRITERION WAS SATISFIABLE WITHOUT WRITING THE FEATURE. The
filed issue asked to "un-guard four crossos tests". v6.5.41 did exactly that: it
removed stale macOS guards — one of which was the TAUTOLOGY
`assert_eq(_cm_counter, _cm_counter, ...)`, a value compared with itself — and every
test went green having changed ZERO lines of backend behaviour. The roadmap had to
carry an explicit warning that its own criterion no longer discriminated.

THE DISCRIMINATOR, once you look for one, is trivially simple: make the worker WAIT
for something the parent can only do AFTER thread_create returns. An inline backend
runs the body to completion inside thread_create, so the flag is still unset and the
rendezvous cannot complete. Bound the wait so a serial target FAILS rather than
HANGS — a hang in the cross-OS leg takes down the release gate instead of reddening
one test, which is a strictly worse signal.

⭐ GUARD IT ON A PUBLISHED CAPABILITY, NOT A TARGET NAME. `THREADS_CONCURRENT` is
declared by each backend (1 = real, 0 = bodies run inline). A capability guard starts
asserting automatically the day another peer becomes real; a target-name guard waits
for a human to remember. The same file then has a gate axis checking the capability
is HONEST, because a serial peer declaring 1 would redden the cross-OS leg.

⚠ MUTATION-PROVE THE TEST AGAINST THE OLD BACKEND. Running the new discriminator on
real ecb with the pre-v6.5.44 serial thread_macos.cyr and THREADS_CONCURRENT forced
to 1 makes it fail both concurrency assertions. Without that step "it passes" means
nothing — it passed before too.

[[entries]]
name = "a_declared_region_size_becomes_the_bound_someone_guards_at"
description = "Three capture loops guarded at 4095 because the heap map said [4096]; two live things sat inside that span, and a path LENGTH silently changed which #ifdef branch compiled (CVE-39, v6.5.45)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: a bounds check written from a DECLARED size is only as
correct as the declaration. When the map overstates a region, every guard derived from it
inherits the overstatement — and the guard LOOKS careful, which is why nobody re-derives it.

The instance. `src/main.cyr` declared `0x190400 include_fname [4096]`, and all three
include/`#ref` filename capture loops in lex_pp.cyr guarded at 4095. Two live things sit
inside that declared span:

    S+0x190700   768 B in   PP_EXPAND's output-write-cursor return slot
    S+0x190800  1024 B in   the #ifdef FEATURE-FLAG TABLE (name hashes, 16 slots)

So a path of >=768 chars corrupts the preprocessor's own output cursor, and >=1024 overwrites
the hashes of CYRIUS_ARCH_X86 / CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS — after which `#ifdef` picks the WRONG
BRANCHES. MEASURED against a compiler built with the pre-fix guards: a 1210-character include
path makes the probe return 7 where 42 is correct, compile rc=0, no diagnostic. A path LENGTH
changed the generated code.

⭐ THE NUMBER WAS 768 AND THE FIRST FIX GOT IT WRONG. This landed at 1023 first, reasoned from
the flag table alone — the cursor slot 256 bytes lower is what makes the real bound 768. Two
independent audit agents disagreed (one said 768, one said 1024) and only grepping the LIVE
writes settled it. If you are fixing a bounds bug, enumerate every live address in the span;
do not stop at the first neighbour you find.

⚠ THE GATE'S OWN FIRST RUN FAILED FOR THE SAME FAMILY OF REASON. Extracting "the next live
address above 0x190400" without stripping comments matched a v6.4.81 NOTE in that very file —
which explains that the map once documented a phantom scratch at 0x190500, "an address NO code
has ever written" — computed a 256-byte span from that sentence, and rejected a correct bound.
Reading a number out of prose is the same defect the note is about. Strip comments first.

FIX: bound at 767; declare all three regions in the map; and make the gate DERIVE the bound
from live writes rather than trust the map, because the map is what was wrong.
See tests/gates/frontend/preprocessor_scratch_bounds.sh and
docs/audit/2026-09-03-security-audit.md.

[[entries]]
name = "an_empty_test_environment_makes_environment_code_unreachable"
description = "The suite ran every test with envp={NULL} for reproducibility, so no test could exercise getenv at all — and getenv was broken on macOS for as long as nobody built a macOS consumer (v6.5.45)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE: a test harness choice made for REPRODUCIBILITY can remove a whole capability from
reach. `EMPTY_ENVP` handed every `.tcyr` a genuinely empty environment — deterministic, and a
good property — but it also meant no test in the suite could read an environment variable,
because there was nothing to read. The code path had zero in-suite reachability.

What that hid: `getenv` returned 0 for EVERY name on macOS. `_env_load` opened
/proc/self/environ; Darwin has no /proc, so `_env_len` stayed 0 and every consumer behaved as
if the variable were unset. A silent wrong answer, not a crash. It was found by thoth 0.44.3 —
its first macOS build since 0.6.4 — i.e. it survived exactly as long as nobody built a macOS
consumer that read the environment.

⭐ THE UNDERLYING DEFECT IS A CLASS ERROR, worth recognising on sight: the AGNOS branch in
`getenv` exists precisely because THAT target has no /proc, and Windows is served by the PE
reroute to GetEnvironmentVariableA. macOS is the same class of target and had no branch. The
general case had been handled once per target instead of once for the class — so the next
target added inherits the bug by default.

FIX (both halves, and the second one matters more):
 1. Read the init-stack envp through the args peer's existing anchor (arm64 x28 / x86-macOS
    r15) — the same derivation cycc's own `_read_env` has used since v6.0.33.
 2. Give the runner ONE FIXED entry (CYRIUS_TEST_ENV=1) instead of an empty array. Determinism
    is preserved exactly — results still do not depend on the invoking shell — and the path
    becomes testable. A fixed entry is not the same as inheriting the caller's environment.

⚠ AND THE TEST MUST ACCEPT EITHER SOURCE, because there are two runners with different
contracts: `cyrius test` and the remote cross-host runner exec through a shell (PATH is set),
while the check.sh suite runner execs with an explicit envp. The first version of the test
asserted PATH, passed by hand, and failed in the suite for a purely environmental reason —
which is the trap its own header warned about.

[[entries]]
name = "a_guard_that_looks_like_a_limit_is_half_a_two_pass_algorithm"
description = "Closures dropped every parameter past the 6th because the in-loop guard was correct but the deferred second pass it implies was never written (v6.5.46)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: `if (i < N) { do_the_thing(i); }` inside a parse loop
can be one of two very different things — a LIMIT (values past N are unsupported) or the
first half of a TWO-PASS ALGORITHM (values past N are handled later, once something is
known). They look identical. If the second pass is missing, the guard silently becomes a
limit that nobody documented and no diagnostic reports.

The instance. PARSE_CLOSURE homed parameters with
`if (clpc < 6) { ESTOREPARM(S, clpc, cpli, 0); }`. That guard is CORRECT: SysV's stack slot
for parameter N is `16 + (pc - 6 - 1 - (N - 6)) * 8`, which counts DOWN from the TOTAL, so
the overflow genuinely cannot be homed until the whole list has been parsed. PARSE_FN runs
exactly that deferred pass — "Emit stack param stores (7+) now that we know total pc". The
closure path never had one, so parameter 7+ was never stored and the body read an
uninitialised frame slot.

⛔ THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG AND COMPILES CLEANLY. Deleting the guard so ESTOREPARM always
runs calls it with pc = 0, so `nstack = pc - 6` is -6 and the displacement is garbage. The
count has to be known first — which is the whole reason the pass is deferred. Recognising
that the guard is half an algorithm, not a limit, is the actual insight.

⚠ IT DID NOT FAIL RELIABLY, which is why it survived: a 7-parameter closure returned the
RIGHT answer (the argument register still happened to hold the value) while an 8-parameter
one returned garbage. Test SEVERAL arities past the boundary — one is not enough, and the
one you pick may be the one that accidentally works.

⚠ AND TEST ORDER, NOT JUST PRESENCE. A deferred pass that homes the stack parameters in the
wrong DIRECTION still sums correctly, because addition is commutative. Use a subtraction, or
distinct powers of two in positions you can distinguish.

[[entries]]
name = "a_cap_raise_must_sweep_every_consumer_of_the_cap"
description = "v6.5.40 raised the fn ceiling and widened live[], but neither loop using it followed — making CYRIUS_DCE=1 unsound above 32768 fns, for the second time (v6.5.46)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE: raising a capacity constant is never a one-line change. Every DERIVED quantity —
array sizes, clear loops, index bounds, warning denominators — is a separate consumer, and
the ones that are loop bounds rather than declarations do not fail loudly when missed.

v6.5.40 raised the fn-table ceiling 32768 -> 131072. It widened the DECLARATION
`var live[4096]` to `var live[16384]`. It did not update:

  * the clear loop, still `while (ci < 4096)`, so three quarters of the reachability
    bitmap started as whatever the stack frame held;
  * the address-taken root-seed bound, still `if (t3idx < 32768)`, so a function reachable
    ONLY through `&fn` was never seeded as a DCE root.

The second one is UNSOUND, not conservative: nothing can follow a raw pointer back, so DCE
concluded the function was dead and NOP-filled it. MEASURED with a 33,001-function program
whose target is referenced only by its address — `CYRIUS_DCE=1` exits 139 (SIGSEGV), and the
same program with DCE off is correct. A defect that only appears under a flag is one nobody
runs into until they turn the flag on.

⭐ SECOND OCCURRENCE. v6.4.75 fixed this exact clear loop for the 4096 ceiling. The v6.5.40
raise reintroduced it. That is the argument for the fix being a GATE that DERIVES the bounds
from the ceiling the compiler actually enforces (`_capacity_warnings`), rather than a
corrected constant — a one-off correction of a self-drifting value is not a fix, and this
cycle has now proved that three times (heap-map sizes, capacity denominators, DCE bounds).

⚠ TWO EXTRACTION TRAPS WHEN WRITING THAT GATE, both hit on its first run and both the same
family: matching the whole clear statement and taking every number yields "16384 8 0" — the
8 from `store8`, the 0 from `, 0` — and the identifier `t3idx` contains a 3. Anchor on the
comparison (`ci < N`, `< N`), never on the statement.

[[entries]]
name = "apply_a_consolidation_to_both_symmetric_places_or_neither"
description = "v6.5.43 built the query-mode route table and applied it to one of two symmetric backends; the other kept 279 hand-written hex words and a 95-entry hand mirror for five releases (v6.5.48)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE: when a codebase has two symmetric implementations and you fix a structural problem in
one, the fix is only half done. The unfixed side keeps the defect AND now diverges from a proven
pattern sitting a file away — so the next reader has two conflicting precedents.

v6.5.43 gave the x86 Mach-O backend a computed emitter (`_msx`) with a QUERY MODE, so
`_macho_x86_routes` answers "is N routed?" by replaying its own emitting rows. The arm64 side —
same purpose, same file structure — kept:

  * 93 syscall rows spelled as three literal `EW()` words each (279 hand-written hex words), and
  * `_macho_arm_routes`, a 95-entry hand mirror of exactly those source numbers.

⭐ THE MIRROR HAD ALREADY DRIFTED, TWICE, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A missing entry made a WORKING call
warn "syscall not routed" — seven socket numbers did that from v6.2.24 to v6.5.16, and a warning
that cries wolf is how a real one gets ignored. A stale entry suppressed the warning for a route
that no longer existed. v6.0.65 had ALREADY deleted one such parallel list on this same side.

⚠ AND THE HAND-WRITTEN ENCODINGS WERE NOT THEORETICAL EITHER: v6.5.43 shipped a `cmp` in this
branch computed by hand as 0xF115511F when the assembler says 0xF115411F.

HOW TO DO IT SAFELY — the part worth copying:
 1. PROVE THE FORMULA AGAINST THE EXISTING ROWS BEFORE REPLACING ANY. All 90 plain rows had to
    reproduce byte-exactly first. (A first scan sliced too wide, caught the aarch64-LINUX arm
    where `movz` targets x8 rather than Darwin's x16, and reported 35 "exceptions" — a slicing
    error, not a formula error. Check which arm you are reading.)
 2. BUILD A REFERENCE COMPILER FIRST, then push the same sources through both and diff the
    OUTPUT. The compiler BINARY legitimately differs — you changed its source — so comparing
    binaries proves nothing. 61/61 crossos tests plus the compiler's own 1.2 MB arm64-macho
    source came out byte-identical.
 3. EXPECT A MINORITY SHAPE. Three rows renumber AND drop an argument (openat 56->5, mkdirat
    34->136, fchmodat 53->15), and their branch displacement depends on the shift count — which
    is exactly why they were the three left in raw hex when the other 90 were consolidated.

⚠ THE GATES READING THOSE ROWS ALL NEEDED UPDATING, and that is a feature: `macho_route_parity`
carried its own aarch64 instruction decoder to read the raw words, and its header records the
trap that decoder set. It now reads the emitter calls as source, and its axis changed from
COMPARING TWO LISTS to ASSERTING THE DERIVATION — the drift it existed to catch is impossible by
construction, so checking for it would be vacuous.

[[entries]]
name = "scratch_sized_for_an_old_cap_fails_SILENTLY_long_before_it_crashes"
description = "v6.5.50: EMITELF_OBJ (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr, the `object;` emitter) carved four sub-regions — strtab / fn_strtab_off / symtab / rela — at HARDCODED offsets inside a FIXED 1 MB brk block, sized by its own comment 'for the v4.7.1 4096 fn cap', with no bounds check on any of them. The loud failure is at 31,398 fns: symtab runs off the end of the block, SIGSEGV, zero bytes out, EMPTY STDERR. THE DANGEROUS HALF IS THE SILENT BAND BELOW IT, where each region walks into its NEIGHBOUR and a corrupt `.o` ships with rc=0. ⛔ THE FILED THRESHOLDS WERE BOTH WRONG AND RE-MEASURING THEM WAS THE SUBSTANCE OF THE FIX: the issue recorded the silent band as '~12,000 fns with LONG names' and asserted '6,000 fns is clean'; bisected against a pre-fix compiler, ORDINARY SHORT NAMES corrupt from 5,528 fns (deterministic 3/3) and 6,000 gives 116 corrupt symbols — about twice as reachable as filed and needing no unusual naming at all. The fix derives every offset from the unit (walk the actual names to size strtab) and CHECKS the brk extension. LESSON 1: a region sized for a cap that has since moved 32x is not 'probably fine' — bisect it, and bisect with a VERIFIED-BAD endpoint (a first attempt converged on 4,200 because `hi` was never probed inside the loop, and 4,200 was clean). LESSON 2: the corruption MAGNITUDE is not stable — the overrun reads residual brk content, so one input gave 927 then 1,106 corrupt symbols on repeat runs — so a gate must assert `corrupt = 0` and NEVER an expected count, or the gate is itself flaky. LESSON 3: the issue also estimated '~90-line refactor'; it was four local assignments plus a derived-size prologue. Read the code before believing a filing's cost estimate."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "check_which_manifest_key_CONSUMERS_write_not_the_one_your_repo_writes"
description = "v6.5.51: v6.5.49 taught `cyrius build` to fall back to `[build] src` / `output` in ./cyrius.cyml when the positional arguments are omitted. Measured across ~/Repos at v6.5.51: **120 of 125 cyrius.cyml files declare `entry`, and only 5 declare `src`** — one of the five being the cyrius repo itself, which is where the implementer read the key from. So the convenience shipped INERT for 96 % of the ecosystem: a pre-fix CLI handed an `entry=` manifest prints the usage text and exits 1, which reads as 'this feature does not exist'. ⚠ AND ITS GATE PASSED, because the gate's fixture manifest was written with the same key the implementation read. That is the transferable failure: a gate authored alongside an implementation tends to encode the implementation's assumptions, so it confirms the code does what the code does rather than what users need. LESSON: when a feature reads a config/manifest key, count the key across real consumer repos BEFORE implementing (`awk '/^\[build\]/{f=1;next}/^\[/{f=0}f&&/^(src|entry)/' ~/Repos/*/cyrius.cyml`), and make the gate's fixture use the key CONSUMERS write. Fixed by accepting both with `entry` first, plus a gate axis for each and one for the both-keys precedence."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "every_unaligned_byte_scanner_over_a_fn_range_must_know_about_inline_switch_tables"
description = "v6.5.53, found by adversarial review of a new peephole and it turned out to indict the OLD one too: since v6.5.20 a cyrius function's emitted byte range can contain inline switch JUMP-TABLE DATA as well as instructions (the table is `(range+1)` 4-byte displacements sitting in the code stream). `_ra_frame_trim`'s NOP recorder already guards against this with `_sw_tbl_hits`, and its comment says exactly why — it is 'an UNALIGNED byte-pattern walk of the whole fn range', so a window landing on table bytes must not be treated as instructions. THE LASE PEEPHOLE IS THE SAME SHAPE AND WAS NOT GUARDED. It scans byte-by-byte for a store/load pair and NOP-FILLS the load in place; a 4-byte table displacement is arbitrary bytes, so it can spell the pattern by coincidence, and NOP-filling there corrupts the table — a silent wrong answer, not a crash. The review was aimed at a NEW 128-bit SIMD arm and noticed it was the only unaligned scanner in the file without the check; checking the twin showed the pre-existing integer arm had the same gap. Both are guarded now (hoist the clamped `L64(S + 0x1AC800)` count once, test `_sw_tbl_hits` alongside `IS_JUMP_TARGET`). LESSON: when a codebase adds DATA to a stream that was previously all instructions, the invariant breaks for every pass that walks that stream unaligned — not just the pass that prompted the fix. Grep for the other walkers the same day. COROLLARY on gates: the review also showed one of the new gate's axes was VACUOUS — it claimed to defend a ModRM 'pin' that no emitted code discriminates on, so the pin's removal was undetectable by any constructible fixture. The honest fix was to stop claiming it and say what the axis actually guards; a gate that overstates what it proves is worse than one that admits its limit, because the next implementer trusts it."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "copy_semantics_beats_lifetime_analysis_for_unboxing_a_sum_type"
description = "v6.5.55: the way to stop boxing a payload enum was NOT to find a safe place to put the box — it was to stop having one. Two relocations were disproven; the register pair was never in that set."
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.15 tried to stop cyrius boxing `Ok(v)`/`Err(e)` by RELOCATING the 16-byte box to a
per-call-site anonymous global. It passed every gate and shipped a compiler that "reported a
failed file open as SUCCESS in a retaining loop", and was reverted. The project's standing note
then read: caller-frame is too short-lived (`?` in an unannotated helper returns the box out of
that helper's frame), a global is too shared, therefore "no relocation-based design can work;
the survivors are escape analysis or a scope-tied arena with reclaim."

⭐ THAT ENUMERATION SILENTLY DROPPED THE OPTION THAT WORKS, and the dropped option needed no
new machinery: return `(tag, payload)` in the multi-return register pair the language already
had. **Copy semantics kills both failure modes at once** — each binding is its own copy of two
registers, so two values built at one call site cannot alias, and there is no storage for
anything to dangle into. Verified before designing anything: a probe of
`fn mk2(x): (i64, i64)` called at ONE call site in a loop returned distinct values with
`alloc_used()` delta of exactly 0.

The general trap: **when every candidate is a variant of "where do we put the object", the
question to ask is whether there needs to be an object.** Lifetime analysis is the expensive
answer to a question that value semantics deletes.

⚠ WHAT IT COST TO NOT SEE THIS: the standing note was framed as a closed set of survivors, so
each revisit re-derived escape analysis and re-concluded it was too big. A closed-set claim in
a design doc deserves the same premise-check as a perf number — ask what it EXCLUDED and why.

⚠ SECOND LESSON, ON SCOPE. The default was NOT flipped, and that was the right call rather than
timidity: the boxed layout is the documented API. The guide spells `Ok(42)` as "16-byte heap
alloc — tag at +0, payload at +8", `?` desugars to `load64(rax + 8)`, and ~300 ecosystem sites
read those offsets by hand. A register pair read as a pointer is a plausible-looking address,
so a default flip breaks them SILENTLY. Opt-in via a keyword that was ALREADY RESERVED (`stack`,
token 111, from `stack var buf[N]`) costs no lexer change and cannot collide with an
identifier — adding a `value` keyword would have broken any program using `value` as a name.

[[entries]]
name = "a_property_held_by_an_unrelated_invariant_breaks_silently_when_that_invariant_moves"
description = "v6.5.56 P0: cyrius's identifier dedup was a PREFIX compare that was exact only because bucketing put one length per chain. Changing the bucketing removed the invariant and nobody added the check it had been standing in for."
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius's `LEXID` deduplicates identifiers through a hash chain. The compare walked `klen` bytes of
a stored pool entry and never checked that the stored entry ENDED there — a PREFIX compare. It was
nonetheless correct for years, because `bucket = klen` put exactly one length on each chain, so
two entries in a chain could not differ in length. **The exactness was a side effect of the
bucketing, not a property of the compare.**

v6.5.50 replaced `bucket = klen` with a content hash — a real fix for an O(N²) chain walk — and
thereby removed the invariant, without adding the terminator check the invariant had been standing
in for. Result, live for six releases: a shorter identifier that is a prefix of a longer one in the
same bucket took the longer one's pool offset and **the two names became one symbol**.
`var ah = 7; var ahxaa = 99;` then read `ah` as **99**. Exit 0, no diagnostic. 127 repos under
`~/Repos` carried at least one colliding pair.

⭐ THE COMMENT ASSERTED THE PROPERTY AND THAT IS PART OF THE FAILURE. Three lines above the compare
sat "dedup remains an exact byte-compare … verified: self-host fixpoint + differential corpus". It
was false. A reader checking whether the hash change was safe found a sentence saying it was.

⛔ THE SELF-HOST FIXPOINT IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THIS ENTIRE CLASS, and this was PROVEN, not
argued: a compiler with the fix deliberately removed **still reproduces itself byte-identically**
while miscompiling consumer code. The reason is that the defect is a function of the INPUT's
identifier set — cycc's own source has 0 colliding prefix pairs out of 54,089 — and the aliasing is
deterministic, so it reproduces identically across generations. **Byte-identity after a hashing or
interning change proves nothing about consumer output.** Any change that alters which inputs collide
needs a gate on a known-colliding input, not a fixpoint.

⚠ THE RECOGNITION RULE, worth more than the fix: when you remove or replace an invariant, ask what
was RELYING on it — not just what it was for. `bucket = klen` was introduced for lookup speed; it
was silently also providing compare exactness. Grep for code that reads the same state and check
each one against the NEW invariant. A helper whose correctness argument mentions a property you are
about to change is the code to re-derive first.

[[entries]]
name = "regalloc_param_exclusion_is_by_count_not_slot"
description = "The picker excluded params by param COUNT while its frame-slot map is by SLOT — a scalar after a by-value vector param got promoted to a register and read as 0"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.62. The register-allocator picker (an inline block inside `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL`,
`src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr`) skipped parameters when choosing promotion candidates with:

    var iv_li = pc;        # pc = PARAMETER COUNT

but the frame-slot indices it walks are SLOTS. A by-value vector parameter occupies
`nslots` slots — `nslots-1` anonymous fillers (name sentinel -1) and then its NAMED slot,
which carries the SLTYPE. So for `fn f(v: f64v4, s)`:

    slot 0,1,2 = fillers for v      slot 3 = v (named)      slot 4 = s
    pc = 2  ->  the loop started at slot 2  ->  s was a "local"

`s` became a promotion candidate and was held in a callee-saved register, while the
prologue's homing store still wrote the incoming argument to its frame slot. The callee
read the register. `s` came back as 0 — silently, exit 0, from ordinary user code.

THE FIX is to capture the real boundary where the parameter list closes, because GFLC
there counts exactly the param slots:

    var ra_pslots = GFLC(S);     # right after the `)` of the param list
    ...
    var iv_li = ra_pslots;       # instead of `pc`

For a function with no wide params this equals `pc`, so nothing moves: 406 corpus inputs
byte-identical, 0 codegen diffs.

HOW TO RECOGNISE IT. Four things must coincide, and dropping any one makes it vanish —
which is why it survived so long and why a narrow probe can "disprove" it:
  1. a by-value VECTOR param (both widths reach it; f64v4 reliably, f64v2 whenever the
     picker actually spends a register on the slot),
  2. another param AFTER it (scalar-first is fine),
  3. a body the inline-replay path REFUSES, so a real call happens. A FLAT body inlines
     and dodges it entirely,
  4. the picker enabled.

⭐ THE ONE-COMMAND OWNERSHIP TEST. `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0` recompiles with the
picker disabled. If the wrong answer becomes right, the picker owns the bug; if it stays
wrong, the picker is exonerated and you are looking in the wrong subsystem. Run this
BEFORE reading any allocator code. In the same session it exonerated the picker for a
different SIMD defect (v6.5.61's value-form binding bug, which was in `PARSE_VAR`) and
then convicted it for this one — two bugs with overlapping symptoms in the same wrappers.

⚠ AND THE TRAP THAT ALMOST BURIED IT: the same release flattened `lib/simd.cyr`'s
wrappers for performance, which makes `f64v4_scale` INLINE — and an inlined callee never
takes the broken path. The perf change would have made the symptom disappear while the
compiler stayed broken for every consumer writing their own non-flat wrapper. When a
library change makes a wrong answer go away, find out whether it fixed the bug or only
stopped reaching it: rebuild the PRE-change library against the FIXED compiler and check
it passes on its own.

[[entries]]
name = "simd_value_form_cost_is_store_to_load_forwards"
description = "The cost of a chained SIMD value-form expression is 16-byte store-to-load forwards, NOT instruction count — removing 22 of 28 instructions per link measured 1.00x"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.64. A value-form vector chain like

    var t1: f32v4 = f32v4_add(a, b);
    var t2: f32v4 = f32v4_add(t1, b);

expands, per link, to 28 real instructions and ~15 memory ops for exactly ONE `addps`. The
obvious conclusion — "delete the scaffolding" — is WRONG, and it was measured wrong before
the code was written.

A hand-written asm replica that removes exactly that scaffolding (3 lea + 3 pointer spills +
3 reloads + a bounded loop that runs one iteration) and changes nothing else runs **33.99 ms
against the current 33.96 ms**. Twenty-two instructions per link removed, zero time saved.

WHY. The critical path is made of 16-byte STORE-TO-LOAD FORWARDS, and the pointer machinery
is not on it. A calibrated cost model over five variants predicts within 3.6%:

    time(2M iters) = 4.111ms x (critical-path store->load forwards) + 1.370ms x (packed ops)

Per link there are two forwards: the inline replay's copy of a param into the callee slot
(reloaded by the kernel), and the kernel's result store (reloaded by `return r`). What
actually bought 1.48x was making the store and its reload ADJACENT so the v6.5.53 SLASE
peephole could collapse them — the peephole matches at exactly +8, and before this change the
pair sat ~0x31 bytes apart, which is why SLASE's own comment recorded it deleting nothing.

MEASURE THE REGIME, NOT JUST THE SHAPE. Two of my own baselines were mis-framed the same way:
a benchmark chain that is DEAD at the end of each iteration lets consecutive iterations overlap
in the out-of-order window, so the dependency stall reads as ~20%. Carry the chain across
iterations (feed the accumulator back) and the same code shows ~2.3x. If you are measuring
dependency latency, the value must be live into the next iteration; otherwise you are measuring
throughput and will under-read the prize by a factor of two.

Staged prize for a vector register class, all measured:
  direct form alone .................. 1.00x
  + elided return reload ............. 1.59x   (v6.5.64)
  + elide replay param copy .......... ~2.07x latency / 5.20x throughput
  + full register residency .......... 8.26x latency / 9.48x throughput
And spilling the result once per iteration is FREE (4.114 vs 4.111 ms) — a register class only
has to kill the per-link RELOAD, not all stores.


[[entries]]
name = "wrong_instruction_length_is_worse_than_unknown_one"
description = "A length decoder that returns 0 fails safe; one that returns a length ONE BYTE SHORT desynchronises the walk and manufactures phantom control flow — and the mutant is the same SIZE as the correct compiler"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.68. `DECODE_LEN` (src/backend/x86/decode.cyr) is not a diagnostic tool. It feeds
`RA_SCAN_LOOPS`, which finds the backward edges that drive the register allocator's loop-aware
live-interval extension — on the DEFAULT path, for every compile. Nothing had ever verified it
against the bytes cyrius actually emits, and two gaps had accumulated with OPPOSITE severities.

THE SAFE GAP: `0x99` (CQO) had no case at all, so the function returned 0. Every caller treats 0
as "stop": `RA_SCAN_LOOPS` returns -1, which its own call site documents as "no information, NOT
no loops", and the picker falls back to whole-function intervals. `ECQO` emits `48 99` before
EVERY integer division, so v6.5.35's register time-sharing had been silently OFF in every
function containing `/` or `%` since it shipped. Expensive, never wrong.

THE UNSAFE GAP, and the one worth remembering: the two-byte opcodes carrying an imm8 AFTER the
ModR/M byte — `0F BA` (BT/BTS/BTR/BTC), `0F 70`-`0F 73` (the PSRL/PSRA/PSLL groups), `0F C2`,
`0F C4`, `0F C5`, `0F C6` — took the generic `0F`+ModR/M path and returned a length ONE BYTE
SHORT. 559 sites across a 39-binary corpus. `48 0F BA F0 3F` (`btr rax, 63`, the f64 tag strip)
is five bytes and the decoder said four, so the walk resumes ON THE IMMEDIATE and decodes it as
an opcode. A desynced walk can manufacture a phantom Jcc, or step over a real backward edge —
which is exactly the v5.6.22 miscompile the loop extension exists to prevent.

⛔ NO SIZE-BASED GATE CAN SEE THE SECOND CLASS. The compiler built with the imm8 fix reverted is
byte-for-byte the SAME SIZE as the correct one (1,213,864 B both) and differs only in content.
The only assertion that catches it is walking real emitted code and requiring the walk to land
EXACTLY on each function's end — which is what `CYRIUS_DECODE_AUDIT=1` and
`tests/gates/codegen/decode_len_coverage.sh` now do (1,281 fns, 1,281 clean).

HOW TO RECOGNISE IT AGAIN: any hand-written length decoder acquires this shape as the emitter
set grows, because adding an opcode to the emitter and adding it to the decoder are edits in
different files with no compile-time link. The tell is a *fail-safe* story ("unknown returns 0,
callers fall back") being used to justify not testing coverage — it only covers the gaps that
return 0, and says nothing about the ones that return a plausible wrong number. Audit by
walking, and separate "undecodable" from "desynced" in the report: they need different fixes.

[[entries]]
name = "raw_emitted_disp32_is_in_no_registry"
description = "The entry trampoline's E9 is hand-emitted rather than through EJMP0, so no compaction registry knows about it — it never mattered until a pass moved bytes program-wide"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.68. Whole-program NOP compaction has to repair every stored code position, and the
enumeration people reach for is "the tables the per-function compactor repairs". That list is
incomplete in a way that is invisible until the scope widens.

The per-fn pass only ever deleted bytes INSIDE one function, so it needed exactly the positions
that could move relative to each other within a function. Three things fall outside that and are
therefore absent from every existing table:

  * fn START offsets (`_fnt_offsets`) — a function's own start is below all of its own runs, so
    the per-fn pass never had to shift it.
  * fn END offsets (`_fnt_codeend`) — `SFNE` is called AFTER the per-fn compaction, so it is
    recorded already-correct.
  * THE ENTRY TRAMPOLINE. `main.cyr` and `main_win.cyr` hand-emit `EB(S, 0xE9)` plus four raw
    placeholder bytes and patch them later with `EPATCH`, bypassing `EJMP0` — so the site is in
    NO registry, not the per-fn `jump_src_tbl` and not the fixup table. Its source and target
    both sit outside every function body, which is exactly why it never mattered before.
    Unregistered under a program-wide pass, deleting one 5-byte run below the target makes cycc
    SIGSEGV at its own pre-compaction entry address. `main_x86_macho.cyr` uses `EJMP0` for the
    same jump — a fork divergence, so ELF and PE are affected and Mach-O is not.

THE GENERAL SHAPE: when a pass widens from per-unit to whole-program, the existing repair list
is not a starting point, it is a subset defined by the OLD scope. Re-derive it from the code —
grep for every writer of a code position — rather than inheriting it from the narrower pass or
from an issue file. Here the issue file named four tables; seven needed repair.

[[entries]]
name = "aggregate_copy_by_type_not_by_occupancy_clobbers_neighbours"
description = "v6.5.57 P0, live SEVENTEEN releases: `X = Y;` between two struct-POINTER locals copied STRUCTSZ/8 slots and wrote over neighbouring locals. The discriminator is the FILLER SENTINEL (name -1), not the type — and the fixpoint was structurally blind because cycc's own source never uses the shape"
category = "cyrius"
---
`X = Y;` where both are locals of a struct type copied **the number of slots the
TYPE implies** rather than the number the VARIABLES occupy. For two inline
aggregates that is right. For two **pointers** to a struct it is catastrophic: the
locals are one slot each, so the copy walked `STRUCTSZ/8` slots off the end and
overwrote whatever sat beneath them — in yukti, a loop-bound PARAMETER, which then
walked its buffer off into the process stack.

**The discriminator is not the type.** An inline aggregate OWNS the slots beneath
its named one, and the frame marks them with a **filler sentinel** — name `-1`. A
pointer-to-struct local does not. So the guard is: before copying `nslots`, walk
both source and destination and refuse unless every slot beneath the named one is
a filler:

    while (ac_k < nslots) {
        if (L64(names + (dst_li - ac_k) * 8) != 0 - 1) { return 0; }
        if (L64(names + (src_li - ac_k) * 8) != 0 - 1) { return 0; }
        ac_k = ac_k + 1;
    }

⛔ **WHY IT SURVIVED SEVENTEEN RELEASES, and this is the part to carry.** The
byte-identical self-host fixpoint could not see it: **cycc's own source never uses
the shape.** Guarded and unguarded compilers emit `src/main.cyr` byte-identically
(1,247,608 B) — so the fixpoint agreeing proves the compiler does not *exercise*
the path, never that the path is correct. Treat "the fixpoint is green" as coverage
ONLY for constructs cycc itself contains.

**How it was actually found**, after instrumentation kept making it vanish: bisect
the TRACKED compiler binaries. Every tag carries `build/cycc`, so

    git show 6.5.56:build/cycc > /tmp/cc_56    # PASS
    git show 6.5.57:build/cycc > /tmp/cc_57    # FAIL

is an instant, download-free bisect over the real artifact. One fix then cleared
failures in TWO downstream repos that had been written off as pre-existing.


[[entries]]
name = "value_form_return_payload_alone_reads_as_success"
description = "v6.6.0 migration fail-open: `return res;` on an Err path returns the PAYLOAD by itself, so Err(77) reads downstream as tag=77 / is_err=0 — an error that reads as SUCCESS. 19 real sites in yukti alone"
category = "cyrius"
---
When `Result`/`Option`/`Either` became the value form (v6.6.0), a payload variant
returns `(tag, payload)` in a register pair. The natural-looking propagation

    var t, v = inner();
    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return v; }     # ⛔ WRONG

returns the **payload alone**. The caller then binds `t` from rax — which now holds
the payload — so `Err(77)` reads as **tag=77, is_err=0**: the error is silently
reclassified as a success carrying 77. Correct:

    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return (t, v); }

**This is the migration rule's own fail-open**, and it is worse than the bug the
flip fixed: an allocation leak is a resource problem, an error that reads as success
is a correctness one. Measured: **19** real sites in yukti (→2.3.10) and 3 in vani.

A mixed-return function — some paths returning a pair, some a single value — now
raises a compiler **warning**. It is deliberately not fatal (the dual-mode shape is
legitimate and tested both ways in vani), so the warning must actually be read.
⚠ Do NOT "fix" a warning by wrapping the single-value path in a constructor without
checking the tests: that was tried on vani and broke 3 of them, because those
returns were raw-by-design.


[[entries]]
name = "compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not"
description = "v6.6.1: CYRIUS_DCE=1 shrinks the code buffer, but PE geometry is computed at the TOP of FIXUP from the pre-elimination length — so the import payload was written at the new cursor while the section header named the old offset. The loader mapped the IAT from zero padding and the first call *IAT(%rip) faulted 0xC0000005 before main"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.72 made `CYRIUS_DCE=1` physically REMOVE dead bodies (`wp_compact`) and shrink
`GCP(S)`. But `_pe_layout(S)` runs at the **top** of FIXUP off the **pre-elimination**
length, so every PE geometry field — section sizes, RVAs, `PointerToRawData`, and the
IAT RVA the ftype=4 fixups patch against — described a layout the emitted code no
longer had. `EMITPE_EXEC` then writes `.idata` at the **post-compaction cursor**
(`o = o + cp`) while the header still names the pre-compaction offset.

Measured on the filed repro: the import payload moved from file offset `0x220F6` to
`0x2AF6` — **128,512 B earlier**, against the 128,555 B DCE reported — so the loader
mapped the Import Address Table from what had become zero padding, every import
resolved to 0, and the first `call *IAT(%rip)` faulted `0xC0000005` **before main**.

⚠ **Two independent breakages, and fixing one is not enough.** The displacement was
ALSO wrong on its own terms: patched against the old geometry, then the instruction
moved, so it resolved to RVA `0x39DD` for an IAT the header puts at `0x23000`. A
compaction pass that repairs geometry but not rip-relative displacements — or the
reverse — produces a binary that looks fine and faults later.

**Two tells worth recognising.** (1) The flag reported "N bytes eliminated" while the
FILE SIZE did not change — output written earlier inside a fixed-size buffer looks
identical from the outside. (2) The crash read a function pointer whose bytes were
ASCII (`0x7465470000000057` = `W`…`Get`): an IAT slot holding hint/name-table content
means the loader never resolved it.

**Current state**: PE and x86 Mach-O DECLINE compaction, exactly as `_pie_mode`
already did and for the same stated reason — a rip-relative reference shape the pass
does not repair. They keep NOP-fill (correct, size unchanged). ELF still eliminates
for real (123,048 → 16,552 B, −86.5%).

⛔ **The filing said "PE / `--win` target only" and that was wrong.**
`main_x86_macho.cyr` includes the same `x86/fixup.cyr`, so Intel-Mac Mach-O took the
identical path and SIGSEGV'd on real hardware — absent from the report only because
the reporter does not build that platform. **A filing's target list is a report about
what the reporter builds, not about the bug.** Reproduce on every host that shares
the code path before scoping the fix.


[[entries]]
name = "guard_must_precede_a_range_reduction_not_sit_inside_it"
description = "v6.6.1: f64_exp/f64_exp2 returned NaN for ±inf in BOTH implementations. The reduction computes `inf - inf`, AND the exponent it assembles comes from f64_to(inf) saturating to i64::MIN — two ways to produce garbage, both upstream of the actual exponential"
category = "cyrius"
---
`f64_exp(+inf)` and `f64_exp(-inf)` returned **NaN**. The range reduction subtracts a
multiple of the argument *from the argument*, so for an infinite input it computes
`inf - inf` = NaN. A guard placed *inside* the reduction cannot fix this, because
there is a **second** failure on the same path: the exponent is assembled from
`f64_to(inf)`, which **saturates to `i64::MIN`**, so `(n + 1023) << 52` is not an
exponent either. The check has to come **first**, before any arithmetic touches the
argument.

    if ((x & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) == 0x7FF0000000000000) {
        if (((x >> 63) & 1) == 1) { return 0; }    # exp(-inf) = +0
        return 0x7FF0000000000000;                  # exp(+inf) = +inf
    }

⚠ **There are TWO implementations and a consumer hits whichever its target selects**:
the `_f64_exp_polyfill` / `_f64_exp2_polyfill` pair in `lib/math.cyr`, and the emitted
x87 `f2xm1`/`fscale`/`frndint` sequence in `src/backend/x86/emit.cyr`. Fixing only the
polyfill leaves every x86 build wrong. The backend guard is emitted ahead of the x87
sequence and its two exits are patched after the x87 pop.

**NaN deliberately gets NO guard.** It already propagates correctly through both
paths, and adding one would only create a branch that has to stay correct forever.
Not every special value needs handling — check which ones actually break.

[[entries]]
name = "the_confidently_named_cause_was_wrong_four_times_measure_the_thing_itself"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from four v6.5.50-.54 entries. cycc's compile-time superlinearity was blamed on _fnt_grow, on an amortized-linear rehash, and on a DCE walk that never ran — while the real causes were a hash keyed on LENGTH ALONE and an open-addressed table grown only when FULL. Separately a planning number was carried across releases and was off by 20x. The habit that catches all of them: instrument or count the thing, never design around a stated mechanism"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces four** (`a_hash_keyed_on_LENGTH_ALONE…`,
`an_open_addressed_table_grown_only_when_FULL…`,
`a_cost_jump_at_a_doubling_boundary…`, `a_perf_number_carried_forward…`). They were
four faces of one lesson and cost four lookups to learn once.

**The two real causes, both trivial once measured:**

1. **A hash keyed on LENGTH ALONE is not a hash.** LEXID (`src/frontend/lex.cyr`) had
   used `bucket = klen` saturated at 255 since v5.10.40, so EVERY identifier of the
   same length shared ONE chain and the per-lookup walk WAS the O(n²) scan the index
   existed to remove. Real code is full of same-length names. 60,000 fns named
   `f0..f59999`: **20,168 ms → 1,005 ms** once the key includes content, output
   byte-identical (dedup is still an exact byte-compare, only chain ORDER moves).
2. **An open-addressed table grown only when FULL is quadratic by construction.**
   The fn-name hash sized itself `mask = nc - 1` — exactly as many slots as entries —
   and REGFN grew only at 100% load. Two innocuous lines, different files.

**The misattributions, which are the durable part:**

- A **cost jump at a doubling boundary is not evidence the doubling is the cost.**
  60,000 fns 737 ms → 70,000 fns 2,078 ms was read as "one `_fnt_grow` costs
  ~1,145 ms", which is absurd on its face: the grow costs 4–9 ms across an entire
  120,000-fn compile. The boundary was where LOAD FACTOR crossed, not where the
  copy happened.
- A **rehash that doubles is amortized-linear by construction** — it cannot be the
  superlinear term, and saying so needs no measurement at all.
- A **DCE walk that is OFF by default never ran in any measurement** that blamed it.
- A **planning number carried across releases was off by 20×**: `CYRIUS_IR=3` was
  planned around "+3.6 % compile time (670 → 694 ms)". Measured live: **13,967 ms**
  vs 672. The size half of the same note was accurate, which is exactly why the time
  half survived — a half-right number reads as a checked one.

⭐ **The habit:** instrument or COUNT the thing itself before designing around a
stated mechanism, and re-take any number you are about to plan against. Three of
these survived multiple releases and two prior investigations.


[[entries]]
name = "inline_replay_is_a_prerequisite_for_simd_residency_and_it_binds_params_by_slot"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from four v6.5.52-.53 entries. A SIMD value chain cannot keep a vector in a register across a call because SysV has NO callee-saved XMM — so inlining is a PREREQUISITE, not an optional optimisation. Admitting SIMD params to the inline-replay path then exposed four defects at once, rooted in the replay binding params to ADJACENT 8-BYTE slots that a 16-byte value overruns"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces four** (`no_callee_saved_XMM_on_SysV…`,
`inlining_is_a_PREREQUISITE…`, `inline_replay_binds_params_to_8_BYTE_slots…`,
`an_inline_replay_that_binds_params_BY_NAME…`). One arc, one lookup.

**The ABI fact that sets the order.** SysV has **no callee-saved XMM registers**.
So a vector CANNOT survive a call, and a value-form SIMD chain that emits a `call`
per link must spill and reload around every one of them. The roadmap and state.md
both pinned the blocker as "the allocator's picker only recognises REX.W mov to/from
`[rbp+disp32]`, so no xmm/ymm local is a candidate" — a TRUE statement about the
code and the WRONG lever. Removing the calls (inlining) has to come first;
teaching the picker about xmm is downstream of it. **Inlining is a prerequisite,
not a nicety.**

**What admitting SIMD params to inline-replay then exposed — four defects, and the
instructive part is that each one ALONE still looked nearly working**, so every
single fix "almost" succeeded:

- The replay binds a callee's params to **temporary local slots** —
  `var p0_slot = saved_flc; var p1_slot = saved_flc + 1;` — i.e. ADJACENT **8-byte**
  slots. A 16-byte SIMD param written into one **overruns into the next**, so two
  vector params collide.
- `SFINL` packed the two param NAME offsets from local slots 0 and 1, which is only
  correct when the callee's first two locals ARE its params.
- The replay's param COPY was read back two instructions later — a 16-byte
  store-to-load FORWARD that the hardware cannot elide; the emitter must read the
  CALLER's local instead (v6.5.65, 1.27×).
- Dead inline-param copies left an `xmm0` clobber that stopped SLASE collapsing the
  inter-link pair; harvesting them is what finally gave register residency across
  chain links (v6.5.66, 2.50–2.60× cumulative).

⚠ **Measured, not assumed**: deleting the value-form scaffolding alone — 28
instructions for ONE `addps` — moved 33.99 → 33.96 ms, i.e. **ZERO**. The cost was
never the instruction count; it was the store-to-load forwards and the calls.


[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_only_proves_something_if_you_prove_the_mutation_reached_it"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from three v6.5.50-.54 entries. A mutation applied with a sed pattern that never matched left the tree UNCHANGED and the gate printed PASS; some gates need a source revert rather than a compiler swap; and a comfortable hypothesis about a flaky gate was wrong. Mutation-prove the gate AND check the mutation was actually applied"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces three** (`a_mutation_that_silently_fails_to_apply…`,
`some_gates_need_TWO_different_mutations…`, `test_the_comfortable_hypothesis…`).

- ⛔ **A mutation that does not apply reports PASS and proves nothing.** A gate was
  mutation-proven with `sed -i 's/                        if (IR_ENABLED(S) != 0) {/…/'`
  — **twenty-four** spaces of indentation against the file's **twenty**. The pattern
  never matched, the tree was never modified, the gate ran against the ORIGINAL code
  and printed PASS. Always assert the file CHANGED (`cmp`, or a grep count that moves)
  before believing a mutation result.
- **Some gates need a source revert, not a compiler swap.** Swapping `build/cycc` for
  a pre-fix compiler is not always the mutation: a gate axis that BUILDS from the
  working-tree source (`cycc < src/main_aarch64.cyr`) and runs the result is testing
  the SOURCE, so only a source revert perturbs it. Pick the mutation that reaches the
  axis you are proving.
- **Test the comfortable hypothesis before fixing it.** A gate went red once under
  load and never reproduced; the filing's leading explanation (a sampler counting a
  SIGKILLed-but-unreaped process) was checked and REFUTED. Fixing the comfortable
  story would have left the real cause live and closed the investigation.

⭐ Companion traps already known and still true: a gate fixture whose decoy sits
AFTER the real key is never reached (the scan stops at the first match), and a gate
whose fixture shares the implementation's assumption agrees with it forever — the
v6.5.49 manifest-key case, where the feature shipped inert to 96 % of consumers and
its gate stayed green.


[[entries]]
name = "install_sh_copies_are_where_the_toolchain_breaks_use_temp_plus_rename"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. install.sh has broken the toolchain twice by HOW it copies: `cp -L` over a symlink pair aborts with 'same file' under set -e before the symlink-update block (v5.10.37), and `cp` writes THROUGH the directory entry so installing over the running binary is ETXTBSY (v6.6.1). Both leave a half-written tree; rename() replaces the entry instead"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces two** (`install_sh_cp_L_same_file_silent_fail`,
`installing_over_a_running_binary_needs_rename_not_cp`). Same file, same class:
**how install.sh copies is the thing that breaks the toolchain.**

**Failure 1 — `cp -L` on a symlink pair (v5.10.37).** The `--refresh-only` lib-copy
loop uses `cp -L lib/*.cyr`. When `lib/<dep>.cyr` is a `cyrius deps`-resolved symlink
AND the snapshot dir holds a parallel symlink to the SAME real file, `cp -L` exits
with "same file". `set -e` makes that fatal, so install.sh dies BEFORE the
symlink-update block — `~/.cyrius/bin` and `~/.cyrius/current` stay pointing at the
PREVIOUS version. The user-visible symptom is a stale `cycc --version` in the shell
prompt, which reads as prompt/config corruption rather than a failed install.
⚠ Still live: `grep -c 'cp -L' scripts/install.sh` → 2.

**Failure 2 — `cp` over a running binary (v6.6.1).** `cp` writes THROUGH the existing
directory entry, so overwriting a currently-executing binary is `ETXTBSY`. `cyriusly`
re-execs the pinned toolchain, so it is **always running during its own install** —
structural, not a race. The install then aborted part-way, which is why deleting
`cyriusly` and re-running the installer did not recover it: the tree was already
half-written.

**The fix for both shapes**: write a temp file beside the target and `rename` it over.
`rename` replaces the DIRECTORY ENTRY; a running process keeps its open inode and
finishes, and the next exec picks up the new file. A per-file failure warns and
continues rather than aborting mid-tree (`_install_file`, `scripts/install.sh:90`).

⚠ **v6.5.3 had already fixed the ETXTBSY shape in ONE of THREE copy paths.** The
tarball path — the one `cyriusly install` actually uses — survived and was reported
from a clean machine three minors later. When a fix is "make this write atomic",
sweep every writer of that kind in the same pass.
⚠ The installer is frozen into each release's immutable tag, so a broken one cannot
be hot-fixed for an already-published version.

